{"basis" 1688 "status" "STATUS database=\"math\" lineage=01a12a15-3e0a-75a1-9dac-f8b8c5d5c3f1 basis_t=1688 generation=0\n" "questions" [[{:db/id 17592186054987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "058a0bec-443e-5691-ba0f-473fbc4a32ef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186253710} {:answer/id #uuid "136d7516-047a-5f19-b65a-d650137e6cd7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186253838} {:answer/id #uuid "3212f762-64a0-547c-90de-3d647c6ae143" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186254102} {:answer/id #uuid "54d746d5-3697-5980-8711-62bace1a807a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186254410} {:answer/id #uuid "666f6e15-03ca-5add-9346-421f3637c229" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186254593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3212f762-64a0-547c-90de-3d647c6ae143" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186254102} :answer-field/id #uuid "1553d09e-038b-5d1a-b170-1ab51570b9d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186255957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0906e8f1-346b-507f-9289-d9fb6006311a" :question/math-academy-id "q-1210" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in Z:-2\\le x<6}$ and $B={x\\in N:x+1<6}?$\n\n- $∅⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $A⊈B$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all integer numbers greater than or equal to $-2$ but less than $6.$ So,\n\n$$\nA={-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $x+1<6.$ So,\n\n$$\nB={1,2,3,4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $∅⊆B$ is true. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $A⊈B$ is true. For example, $-1\\in A$ but $-1\\notin B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186054993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6128eca-7f76-5386-9d67-d81db2943eeb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Cartesian Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "196e093f-03be-5f08-b076-d621b0e1f3f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186257000} {:answer/id #uuid "53f30868-1437-5931-801c-542ae9db9646" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186257501} {:answer/id #uuid "c68f82ae-5663-5f1b-b44f-c2c9f755251a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186258518} {:answer/id #uuid "f147f689-f111-564c-8b19-a90facfa2838" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186258881} {:answer/id #uuid "f6913a70-bd5e-5927-a8ff-79c39dc44ae8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186258940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f147f689-f111-564c-8b19-a90facfa2838" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186258881} :answer-field/id #uuid "51f7066f-d0c9-5b83-af5b-6f18771d4180" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186259177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c012134a-99e0-5f06-b534-c2d2ae072768" :question/math-academy-id "q-1257" :question/problem "For the sets $A={2,4,6},$ $B={x,y},$ and $C=A\\times B,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(2,4)\\in C$\n- $(2,x)\\in C$\n- $(3,y)\\in C$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $A\\times B$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/df/df4277feac4c4e838538f941aa0a07d087beb9005d8f7a8347dadc1da7fe975e.png)\n\nHence, the set $C=A\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nC={(2,x),(2,y),(4,x),(4,y),(6,x),(6,y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186054995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6128eca-7f76-5386-9d67-d81db2943eeb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Cartesian Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae566481-20f8-5e52-99d8-23a6c82691b6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186051501} {:answer/id #uuid "cdadc5ef-7415-5dcf-b7cf-505d2e49c925" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186051502} {:answer/id #uuid "d138c6e0-6807-55b7-ab39-2b7416f93002" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186051503} {:answer/id #uuid "4129157c-b81d-59dd-8ec5-f67444db60ed" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186051504} {:answer/id #uuid "574a525d-c730-52ff-a8ed-06f70442fbc9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186051505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdadc5ef-7415-5dcf-b7cf-505d2e49c925" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186051502} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dd58087-ae96-5ce0-a2c8-bc32e79b782d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186051500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1ff208f5-5023-5db1-8017-ca1f7ce1c080" :question/math-academy-id "q-1262" :question/problem "For the sets $Z=\\{-1,0,1\\}$ and $Y=Z\\times Z,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\nI. $(-1,0,1)\\in Y$\n\nII. $(-1,1)\\in Y$\n\nIII. $(0,0)\\in Y$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $Z\\times Z$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $Z$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $Z.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for ${Z}^{2}=Z\\times Z$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/6c/6cb962e74701db361807c237a521f13a1c753e4acc6d68a1cfb1694bdfbd45bc.png)\n\nHence, the set $Y=Z\\times Z$ is given by\n\n$$\nY={(-1,-1),(-1,0),(-1,1),(0,-1),(0,0),(0,1),(1,-1),(1,0),(1,1)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186064586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186064166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59cfc260-e8fe-57d4-ad54-4b868b37ddc0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186288660} {:answer/id #uuid "66d6f3bf-0042-5273-b186-aed0e736c5e6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186288753} {:answer/id #uuid "e9b7b58a-5f27-5686-b835-eeb3986ac127" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186289584} {:answer/id #uuid "eab07fee-4975-5076-9f67-782c1e81e08a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186289586} {:answer/id #uuid "eca5348d-66fa-5683-af8f-c0e85df5fe4f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186289596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59cfc260-e8fe-57d4-ad54-4b868b37ddc0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186288660} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce046a0d-27c7-5c04-a831-89f387c8a392" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186290180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "13f8cc2a-4183-5051-a10e-eea4f7de407e" :question/math-academy-id "q-21528" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{5}+5{x}^{3}-90x+108.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(-\\sqrt{3})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-\\sqrt{3})<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-\\sqrt{3}$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-\\sqrt{3}$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=-\\sqrt{3}$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={x}^{5}+5{x}^{3}-90x+108,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=5{x}^{4}+15{x}^{2}-90.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=20{x}^{3}+30x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\sqrt{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-\\sqrt{3}) & =20{(-\\sqrt{3})}^{3}+30(-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =20\\cdot 3(-\\sqrt{3})+30(-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =-90\\sqrt{3}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186092714 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092329 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33a26184-5273-5d7b-963d-1c837e1fbe20" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186327382} {:answer/id #uuid "40b46a99-f713-5214-8460-e570126e3cca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186327505} {:answer/id #uuid "6048dbbb-5886-53ab-8840-13042ec39014" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186327780} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d1727c-d4ec-523d-8bb1-ca4b05fb4320" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186328830} {:answer/id #uuid "ff69e44d-4be5-5d52-8c96-e3bdea5ba3dc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186329177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8d1727c-d4ec-523d-8bb1-ca4b05fb4320" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186328830} :answer-field/id #uuid "94d36922-3a2e-5cd7-95fc-1f2d41a57d8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186329434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5f528577-792d-5dde-937b-39993322cd56" :question/math-academy-id "q-46457" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $6,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P+Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $4$\n- $P-Q$ is always a polynomial\n- $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is always a polynomial" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}$ and ${x}^{6},$ respectively. In $P+Q,$ the leading term still contains an ${x}^{6}.$ There is no ${x}^{6}$ term in $P$ that could possibly cancel the ${x}^{6}$ term in $Q.$ Therefore, the degree of $P+Q$ must always be $6.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{2}$ and $Q={x}^{6}.$ Then $\\frac{P}{Q}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{6}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$ which is not a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186101319 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186100869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b494140f-206a-5317-8079-ff719a253140" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Matrices Are Conformable for Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "941d2244-9073-56f4-8d2f-0c0fe22b6556" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186289024} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8bc73c-b723-51a9-a49d-5f0f5a80d6e6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186289161} {:answer/id #uuid "d8312bbe-f9b8-5c4e-bdbc-82f20ccd1ad0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186289472} {:answer/id #uuid "f6336316-a793-57a9-b829-0bc9e811e3df" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186289657} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d07690-46a8-5a59-8bfe-ff65f8eb6982" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186289669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8312bbe-f9b8-5c4e-bdbc-82f20ccd1ad0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186289472} :answer-field/id #uuid "47ff94a5-e509-5d97-98db-be49bb28132a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186289889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9258b6d8-3a2c-54d4-bc2f-8b9ea4ccd825" :question/math-academy-id "q-22063" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 7 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $A,B,$ and $C?$\n\n- The product $AB$ is well defined\n- The product $BA$ is well defined\n- The product $AC$ is well defined" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, $B$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ column vector\n\nThere are $3$ columns in $A$ and $3$ rows in $B.$ Since the number of columns in $A$ equals the number of rows in $B,$ the matrices $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $2$ columns in $B$ and $3$ rows in $A.$ Since the number of columns in $B$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $A,$ the matrices $B$ and $A$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{B}_{3\\times 2}{\\times }_{\\ne }{A}_{3\\times 3}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $3$ columns in $A$ and $3$ rows in $C.$ Since the number of columns in $A$ equals the number of rows in $C,$ the matrices $A$ and $C$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{✓}{C}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 1}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statements I and III are true, while statement II is false."}] [{:db/id 17592186106189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105710 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Are Neither Local Minima nor Local Maxima"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30b21e2d-88bd-50b0-8af4-21b8c318675b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186288353} {:answer/id #uuid "74c4b405-c22a-50be-b92f-5ada6996d136" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186288843} {:answer/id #uuid "91aa838e-118c-544e-b374-67115a305596" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186289011} {:answer/id #uuid "b701ca02-f2fb-5eb3-95cd-14b7232500c5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186289243} {:answer/id #uuid "f30f98cb-5e51-51f4-a6db-83e1d85f8fa5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186289637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30b21e2d-88bd-50b0-8af4-21b8c318675b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186288353} :answer-field/id #uuid "43697c19-3c87-573a-9be2-3e86767cae6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186289880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "53c65332-08ea-53f5-952a-39779d99b5b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-21465" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)=-{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+6,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=0$ is a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum\n- $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a local maximum" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}=-4{x}^{2}(x-\\frac{3}{2}).\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-4{x}^{2}(x-\\frac{3}{2})=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-4(-1)^{2}(-1-\\frac{3}{2})=10>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-4(1)^{2}(1-\\frac{3}{2})=2>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-4(2)^{2}(2-\\frac{3}{2})=-8<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186106208 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105711 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Other Types of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48001923-8999-5eb6-b560-8aeda2b2397e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186288530} {:answer/id #uuid "53a57412-d5de-5af2-99ec-7256da7c30b9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186288609} {:answer/id #uuid "6826fa0c-0224-5ec5-bc91-0492b4d6601a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186288762} {:answer/id #uuid "760dc0db-9987-5d0e-af02-b41854ca8c5d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186288849} {:answer/id #uuid "f423f240-3ba1-5f95-9947-977f60261629" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186289643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "760dc0db-9987-5d0e-af02-b41854ca8c5d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186288849} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a08ae3d-c09f-5a7a-8386-c387325c21f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186289747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37b4a96f-ccf7-5874-8bcd-7955ee9c3b7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-22810" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ defined for $x\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local minimum\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum\n- $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$   on $(0,2\\pi )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points  $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\pi ,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1-\\sqrt{3}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=1-\\sqrt{3}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$ | $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ | $(\\frac{5\\pi }{3},2\\pi )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |  | $\\pi $ |  | $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186106377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e93fd7f-070f-5359-aabb-cba5cc275015" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent or Divergent Integrals With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04669e9c-9d0f-58e8-8793-3657d9ee41f0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186329996} {:answer/id #uuid "1bd4d6b6-8ac8-54bc-9249-04799b6eaf49" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186330194} {:answer/id #uuid "43a379dd-8a5a-50c9-8642-f63a231a3698" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186330557} {:answer/id #uuid "64586c0d-af1a-5647-926a-a4b555ad5d01" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186330844} {:answer/id #uuid "67546541-7bbf-543c-b06f-2d38778501d8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186330878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bd4d6b6-8ac8-54bc-9249-04799b6eaf49" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186330194} :answer-field/id #uuid "11564464-e4af-50c7-963e-46a79dfa1f3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186332222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d136f69-0617-5d12-ad3b-b952611b8b09" :question/math-academy-id "q-51075" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n\nII. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nLet's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({x}^{3}{|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({a}^{3}-{0}^{3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{a}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, we have that ${a}^{3}\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({x}^{3}{|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({0}^{3}-{a}^{3}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{a}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, we have that ${a}^{3}\\to -\\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements II and III are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186111141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186110667 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7ef1dd-58cc-57f5-9ff9-fc997f4280ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14d716c0-c74a-57f6-833b-a5816bf99cd8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186295386} {:answer/id #uuid "67139bf8-96d1-5cee-b658-3ffcabbeb7f8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186295848} {:answer/id #uuid "68c9ab76-11b5-597a-b277-a022ff7d7ce0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186295856} {:answer/id #uuid "8406301a-c3a9-5c90-b099-4f4b2c78ba3c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186295973} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a38ae3-a755-5134-b132-4a013f680737" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186296308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14d716c0-c74a-57f6-833b-a5816bf99cd8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186295386} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cc8c0d6-c061-513b-98bf-4315a1ac025c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186296752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4102b2c-f2ad-56ac-9dd6-7dd59719b972" :question/math-academy-id "q-28973" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}?$\n\nI. Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\nII. Its range is $y\\in (0,\\infty )$\n\nIII. It is a decreasing function" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of ${(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/c1/c1bbffed32708d30355ed3592deae845d1a84cd1f1772ef1d0fae7ac39518295.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f(x)={(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}$ is defined for all input values $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The set of possible outputs of $f(x)={(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}$ consists of all the positive numbers.\n\n- Statement III is true. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\nIn conclusion, statements I, II, and III are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186115743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "344c7786-e693-5c20-b2fc-98f4340ff1c4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186297763} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb51106-6181-5ad7-b12d-14aad5844a9d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186297951} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ae0aff-18d3-5011-9760-882bbabdbfb5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186298290} {:answer/id #uuid "af5db198-8013-5610-bac5-a6d829c42d3f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186298316} {:answer/id #uuid "bd7f5d92-7a5f-5380-a6d8-e602001b039f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186298381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5eb51106-6181-5ad7-b12d-14aad5844a9d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186297951} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdba6124-93e6-5f09-9472-eda3b6210b63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186299492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44e70f28-a4b9-56ce-989d-752d9ef0f612" :question/math-academy-id "q-32088" :question/problem "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$\n- ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the first part of the invertible matrix theorem, the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ must exist.\n\nFinally, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186115744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115234 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11c21c6d-1733-55e6-ae62-ff9455761bba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186297633} {:answer/id #uuid "39ec9bc9-bb09-5cab-b8c8-16ed6e2d2ba1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186297779} {:answer/id #uuid "9dffde52-21e5-5601-b349-6c8d7ff77873" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186298244} {:answer/id #uuid "cc4e1a09-9e67-57f1-8045-c8a0276fa706" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186298439} {:answer/id #uuid "dd29ba58-48e3-5699-abf9-17ba99e9924b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186298500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39ec9bc9-bb09-5cab-b8c8-16ed6e2d2ba1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186297779} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf059d7e-0a61-50de-b3d7-bd32d0f212a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186299334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "947396a7-1dd0-5962-800b-c42f03f930a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-32091" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+\\sqrt{7}y=0.3 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{0.1}x+3y=\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The lines $3x+\\sqrt{7}y=0.3$ and $-2\\sqrt{0.1}x+3y=\\sqrt{7}$ intersect exactly at one point\n\nII. The determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $0$\n\nIII. The coefficient matrix is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186115837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115228 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a870c4a-daa5-5df2-8968-d8bf9ac0bd75" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Matrix Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eb9b888-7ec0-58f2-bc6c-71fe4d4de7ab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186327061} {:answer/id #uuid "6dd0f962-092d-5749-9eb1-8f5f1a894196" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186327885} {:answer/id #uuid "9eaae3d8-b2ab-56d8-85b6-40164a609eab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186328302} {:answer/id #uuid "a5e554d7-aa81-5df9-bc7f-0a9dbdbc26f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186328368} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ecf0c5-6d1c-53c1-b826-41e70bdfa445" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186328525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6dd0f962-092d-5749-9eb1-8f5f1a894196" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186327885} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8d19542-3c5a-5e47-8c97-509d0485a04e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186329586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e648555-87e2-52bf-ac70-24ae7f7d32c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-42265" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $4\\times 4$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\n- $AB=BA$\n- $(AB)C=A(BC)$\n- $AC+BC=(A+B)C$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+B)C=AC+BC,$ and $A(B+C)=AB+AC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186115838 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115228 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a870c4a-daa5-5df2-8968-d8bf9ac0bd75" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Matrix Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08e832d7-0674-5b61-8679-411cb174a34f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186327005} {:answer/id #uuid "104273e8-5101-54e5-8f7f-47b5f59e7175" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186327078} {:answer/id #uuid "13762ae9-c9d6-51b4-9e32-456c01a1f13d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186327100} {:answer/id #uuid "1a901eda-005b-5518-b595-966405039263" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186327164} {:answer/id #uuid "30ede4a3-85f4-578a-ab58-1e3c8b42e655" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186327367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08e832d7-0674-5b61-8679-411cb174a34f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186327005} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a8a5647-cfa1-58cd-80bb-d8ea288f2a29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186329258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "174631dd-f119-50e8-bf7b-ac669b330d64" :question/math-academy-id "q-42266" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $6\\times 6$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\n- $(A+B)C=AC+BC$\n- $AB=BA$\n- $A(BC)=(BA)C$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+B)C=AC+BC,$ and $A(B+C)=AB+AC$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general. However, since matrix multiplication is not commutative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(BC)=(AB)C\\ne (BA)C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186115882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115381 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Functions are Differentiable at a Given Point From the Function Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60191a14-5ad2-55ab-8217-e91fe73325eb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186327778} {:answer/id #uuid "7d5449f6-af3e-5d64-928d-75f1cc3ae7bc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186328014} {:answer/id #uuid "a4614944-9ea5-5d63-9245-8473caa7f386" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186328356} {:answer/id #uuid "d16e7253-9c7c-5f19-8b69-51e227595eb9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186328775} {:answer/id #uuid "edfe6469-630a-5f72-bd88-748a646f1d7b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186329016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4614944-9ea5-5d63-9245-8473caa7f386" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186328356} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab6e4de6-e749-5260-9e23-6206beb6fb02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186329482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "995f3b18-ad68-56fb-a94d-9e7f0a50f6c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-47367" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ 2, & x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nI. It is continuous and differentiable at $x=1$\n\nII. It is **not** continuous at $x=1$ but it is differentiable at $x=1$\n\nIII. It is **not** differentiable on the interval $x\\in (-2,2)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement individually.\n\n- Statement I is false. First, we investigate the continuity of $f(x)$ . It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, $x=1.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}{x}^{2}={1}^{2}=1 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}{x}^{2}={1}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(1)=2,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$ (it is a removable discontinuity).\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1,$ it is also not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval that includes this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable in $x\\in (-2,2).$\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186115884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77f536b1-d8f0-561b-9297-aa5081bf1309" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186327959} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf19129-29cf-5c05-a879-322b79a84d0b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186328412} {:answer/id #uuid "ab4df1a0-a24e-5f0b-8c8f-56f6058ad129" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186328418} {:answer/id #uuid "bed094d9-e410-5e9c-90ff-058d5f92f3ef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186328602} {:answer/id #uuid "cc07753b-7289-5038-90ee-cb0a4dba45b3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186328723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bed094d9-e410-5e9c-90ff-058d5f92f3ef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186328602} :answer-field/id #uuid "92775213-86bd-5bd6-b7a5-ff4eaaf06c02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186329429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07b54935-f6c1-5cc5-a220-23bab58f39a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-47653" :question/problem "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne -1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $det(A)\\ne 0$\n\nII. $Ax=0$ has no solutions\n\nIII. The lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2$ intersect at infinitely many points" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\cdot k-2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6k+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $6k+6\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -1.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be a unique zero solution to $Ax=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186120147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf6dca7d-0ce4-5e8e-b9b2-3303fd96d94e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186117089} {:answer/id #uuid "d51a6c38-bbb9-53c8-9e8d-d08531e3e2fa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186117090} {:answer/id #uuid "e90efca0-0287-5643-adc8-6e36ca3d9e73" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186117091} {:answer/id #uuid "3f5fc17f-c8a6-5808-8a11-b0ae8b5d686d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186117092} {:answer/id #uuid "669cbbfb-8d77-5f7b-a387-18e5ffbd5163" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186117093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e90efca0-0287-5643-adc8-6e36ca3d9e73" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186117091} :answer-field/id #uuid "01b88459-f835-5654-8214-92ece439c385" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186117088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9ec28c6e-377f-591a-9037-3511702a70c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-155965" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+{a}_{3} & =2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-3+1 \\\\ -2-0+2 \\\\ 4-0+0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186120148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5806b72c-a0de-522d-9575-297c97f66e09" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186273142} {:answer/id #uuid "82608251-0a92-5dd0-8eb5-d194076eb4e1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186273505} {:answer/id #uuid "afbc140c-4487-5281-9e71-ad12441a9572" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186273906} {:answer/id #uuid "bd7c417e-8874-5c6d-81ad-7db977b43fd2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186274028} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f9a778-7655-51b8-a6f6-a2b9c41e7f8a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186274419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afbc140c-4487-5281-9e71-ad12441a9572" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186273906} :answer-field/id #uuid "3579701a-9198-5e56-82f7-d569880f78f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186274728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62e09db0-cf5e-5972-a5d2-6baa771c45b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-155967" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{1}-2{a}_{2}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}-2{a}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186120149 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a7c401-1681-598e-9abf-d2f9ca414b87" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186272562} {:answer/id #uuid "60abeb2d-e0d1-5f50-a1c6-7afcd9b42ec4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186273222} {:answer/id #uuid "ca803a2f-4ca0-521f-a518-87801863c83a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186274146} {:answer/id #uuid "db512396-c1dd-5e30-849b-7311e2259284" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186274307} {:answer/id #uuid "efa4b95b-4038-5603-bfc9-36f6de08c56a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186274507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db512396-c1dd-5e30-849b-7311e2259284" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186274307} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2c035c7-57af-563e-acbe-9ccd93c9ed27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186274919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d522f4eb-6fdb-5796-8d35-98401ee7b35b" :question/math-academy-id "q-156591" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-2{a}_{3}=0.$\n- ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-2{a}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+1-2 \\\\ 2+0-2 \\\\ 0+2-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=1$ and ${x}_{3}=-2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186120150 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19c33c51-bf11-5a81-a0fd-8381d8e0b9d7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a455298a-2dea-548c-af42-b4eb6fbcd065" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186117107} {:answer/id #uuid "a6859d0e-7040-5364-8637-4d6093fae5ea" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186117108} {:answer/id #uuid "d17960bd-e382-5e5d-a7c3-f912ca58bf1c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186117109} {:answer/id #uuid "5454773d-2167-5300-a067-9d49b7868481" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186117110} {:answer/id #uuid "6accb1a9-ced4-5c3e-a301-638457731ee9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186117111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6859d0e-7040-5364-8637-4d6093fae5ea" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186117108} :answer-field/id #uuid "be49487d-1d4f-535e-afdc-3fb6eb834fb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186117106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d2b6331-122d-5166-a305-241f7f5b32d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-156607" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $y=1-2x$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 1-2x\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $y=1-2x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1-2x \\\\ (0) & =1-2(0) \\\\ 0 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 1-1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1-2x \\\\ (0) & =1-2(1) \\\\ 0 & =-1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186120151 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83796fe8-f045-5dbc-94d1-6d6e031f3ed8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "735b9f06-16c3-525e-8e71-064577376d3a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186376042} {:answer/id #uuid "89d90843-0e5b-5282-91a5-459c23d358b6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id 17592186376043} {:answer/id #uuid "1c7681ca-cf09-59b0-9214-97acea3380ad" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186376044} {:answer/id #uuid "8c102d18-68ae-569a-88c9-f7a90ad5be2d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186376045} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb7d32d-49b3-5fe0-94cf-c277de74c950" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186376046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c102d18-68ae-569a-88c9-f7a90ad5be2d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186376045} :answer-field/id #uuid "734e052a-7544-5481-b6fa-03e39bd162c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186376041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe544997-b5f0-5f74-9a6d-c604c1790f93" :question/math-academy-id "q-156679" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,-1,1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\nx=-y=z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ -b \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ -b \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ -a+(-b) \\\\ a+b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ -(a+b) \\\\ a+b\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-y=z.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ k(-a) \\\\ ka\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ -(ka) \\\\ ka\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-y=z.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186120152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f3018fd-9d16-578f-b050-966890844c9b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Plane Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56367304-94a3-532f-932a-a6a009eb1f9a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186273119} {:answer/id #uuid "8598a628-17cc-5fd1-9b55-e3ad503f0213" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186273532} {:answer/id #uuid "8c543d58-af14-5083-bca6-698e1e93ba2a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186273597} {:answer/id #uuid "92fb9d1c-aa8d-5c76-b479-e9d488ea1f6c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186273665} {:answer/id #uuid "c6de225f-fcf6-5420-b1a7-34d4eefa9309" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186274115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92fb9d1c-aa8d-5c76-b479-e9d488ea1f6c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186273665} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b3ab815-cda1-53c4-82ce-396893f98644" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186274762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f59c175-a7f1-58ec-b16f-fd24b9c9dd63" :question/math-academy-id "q-156685" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $x-y-z=1$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ x-y-1\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x-y-1 \\\\ (0) & =(0)-(0)-(1) \\\\ 0 & =-1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 0+0 \\\\ -1+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x-y-1 \\\\ (-1) & =(1)-(0)-1 \\\\ -1 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186120153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f3018fd-9d16-578f-b050-966890844c9b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Plane Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03f7c43b-2707-5aeb-907d-bde2a57533c3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, and IV only" :db/id 17592186272420} {:answer/id #uuid "215c81fa-0b9c-536f-92eb-d88943787f05" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186272684} {:answer/id #uuid "2fa6385e-4a1f-512f-aae9-719f94d9da4f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186272809} {:answer/id #uuid "77b046c2-362d-59fc-9c54-750b9da5d978" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186273407} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f2eb7a-3575-5a19-af87-f081d41c4176" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186273972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6f2eb7a-3575-5a19-af87-f081d41c4176" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186273972} :answer-field/id #uuid "145758a4-08b7-5389-b849-536c7ecafb4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186274670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ceae9baf-b635-5e21-b116-a9e3e8cfea9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-156719" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=x-y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ x-y\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=x-y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x-y \\\\ (0) & =(0)-(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}-{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}-{a}_{2})+({b}_{1}-{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})-({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x-y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k({a}_{1}-{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k{a}_{1}-k{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x-y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186124767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bd5bbe3-da17-5476-84db-f70827deccc2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186241283} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb163a6-5ef1-5d54-b94c-23b0b50d862c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186242166} {:answer/id #uuid "b16b4442-ffc4-5d04-a805-24e8546e2030" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186242725} {:answer/id #uuid "c36f8aea-29cb-5b79-a559-1287d80828c0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186242896} {:answer/id #uuid "fb0726ff-286a-5ad2-a5e5-8b4b1997a03c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186243392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c36f8aea-29cb-5b79-a559-1287d80828c0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186242896} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d03d1e2-dfbd-5d9a-b466-6eebce1e667d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a98e5d99-323b-544b-bce7-36fa3d3ba9e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-106806" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices with determinant $1.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. For any $A\\in S,$ we have $1\\cdot A=A.$\n\nII. For any $A\\in S$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.$\n\nIII. There is a matrix $A\\in S$ such that $\\frac{1}{2}(2A)\\ne A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in S,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n1\\cdot {a}_{ij}={a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=A.\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in S,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta ){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\beta {a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For a matrix in $A\\in S,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(2A)=(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2)A=1\\cdot A=A\n$$\n\nsince the scalar multiplication of matrices is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186124821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a4ceb8c-14f0-56c6-8c95-f0ee15b4651f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186121524} {:answer/id #uuid "eac39722-5b4b-5e3f-abe3-0ec8369122a4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186121525} {:answer/id #uuid "f4428592-ed70-51da-961c-e1f39f88ff4e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186121526} {:answer/id #uuid "0f8e477a-ee9a-5e8a-a0d8-7609b5654512" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186121527} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb59655-80cf-5782-9754-fdb5fa7a7b78" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186121528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eac39722-5b4b-5e3f-abe3-0ec8369122a4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186121525} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d8caccd-460a-5673-8d6f-d66dff746801" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186121523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5496aa6-8f04-55cb-807e-38886a8c0e30" :question/math-academy-id "q-163722" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-12+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-2$ and $det(A)=-24$\n\nII. $A$ is positive-definite\n\nIII. $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-2$ and\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}|=-24.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-2<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}|=-24<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186124822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aa2aeb3-b01d-5155-aeac-f21b63e93dd1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186281787} {:answer/id #uuid "29600d0b-9152-5088-bc79-75183b812cbb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186282048} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce748ed-eb8d-513b-9cf0-734951ab3d25" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186282479} {:answer/id #uuid "66543e7d-0cd8-5cac-8dc8-6bbf3d29dc33" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186282572} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d26d7b-d55b-5486-a774-74cc00513f66" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186283090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ce748ed-eb8d-513b-9cf0-734951ab3d25" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186282479} :answer-field/id #uuid "65c2bad6-e6f3-56f1-91fc-d5fba36e11b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186284102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f4f668d6-a868-59b1-9e4d-3e52d3a52a67" :question/math-academy-id "q-163723" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-9+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=6$ and $det(A)>0$\n- $A$ is positive-definite\n- $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=6$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =40>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=6>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}|=20>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=40>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is positive-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186124899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124485 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b699f55-4019-5dc2-b439-26b3200bb0d0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186321607} {:answer/id #uuid "4019cb4a-9b42-5959-b339-693ecc932658" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186321652} {:answer/id #uuid "5a7b8e69-1402-5fb1-a1db-03531dd368db" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186321861} {:answer/id #uuid "ada862dc-29af-554a-bc4b-f52a239113b2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186322508} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbeebae-9d4e-5b4e-a520-1a98e999ddcf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186323134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ada862dc-29af-554a-bc4b-f52a239113b2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186322508} :answer-field/id #uuid "172368fc-3d61-5e22-9cc1-92d9c21cd25e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186323211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1d95ba8a-32c3-52ab-a4a0-101091efe61a" :question/math-academy-id "q-35706" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent and ${v}_{4}=3{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}+5{v}_{3},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{3},$ and ${v}_{4}$\n- ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ has only zero solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0$\n- ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly independent set" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, solving equation ${v}_{4}=3{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}+5{v}_{3}$ for ${v}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}{v}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{v}_{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}_{4}.\n$$\n\nThis means that ${v}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{3},$ and ${v}_{4}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${v}_{4}=3{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}+5{v}_{3},$ we have that\n\n$$\n3\\cdot {v}_{1}-2\\cdot {v}_{2}+5\\cdot {v}_{3}+(-1)\\cdot {v}_{4}=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ has a non-zero solution.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ has a non-zero solution, the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly dependent set by definition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186124910 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124485 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "476afb4f-5866-5b5d-851d-fc43cf1faf22" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186321707} {:answer/id #uuid "68a8b573-cb9f-5306-b0ca-cbd79f46231b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186321964} {:answer/id #uuid "9350ee0e-5849-573e-96df-eb4fc7d244ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186322313} {:answer/id #uuid "94cdf8ce-c777-5098-a437-a578ada9a9cc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186322332} {:answer/id #uuid "eb545235-20f4-5106-b81f-fb888f30d5ad" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186322989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "476afb4f-5866-5b5d-851d-fc43cf1faf22" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186321707} :answer-field/id #uuid "556ebefe-4115-5472-aa5b-f94fb78b23b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186323332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8bba7c53-725c-5edb-a90b-13e8c293c9e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-35758" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{v}_{1}+{v}_{2},{v}_{2}+{v}_{3},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${0,{v}_{1},2{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})+{x}_{2}({v}_{2}+{v}_{3})+{x}_{3}{v}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}){v}_{2}+({x}_{2}+{x}_{3}){v}_{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ which means that \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1}+{v}_{2},{v}_{2}+{v}_{3},{v}_{3}}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot {v}_{1}+0\\cdot (2{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}0+{x}_{2}{v}_{1}+{x}_{3}(2{v}_{2})=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that ${0,{v}_{1},2{v}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0$ must have only the zero solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$  If this equation has a non-zero solution, $a\\cdot {v}_{1}+b\\cdot {v}_{2}=0,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\na\\cdot {v}_{1}+b\\cdot {v}_{2}+0\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186124938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0306cd6f-13b1-5819-9d88-589ec82a7567" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186321175} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac8ed75-50aa-5ca3-bafd-98e8c74b71c0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186321734} {:answer/id #uuid "4b9e085e-efb1-5d87-8499-f4904b6d43fb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186321742} {:answer/id #uuid "76705b99-83bc-5403-bcfe-a66751883b54" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186322070} {:answer/id #uuid "baa62fd1-3528-56bb-aa7a-f445a9213a3e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186322595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "baa62fd1-3528-56bb-aa7a-f445a9213a3e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186322595} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca5a9fd7-e92d-59d5-a7b2-336e791742fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186323565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a81953f-1927-53ea-b174-321fab218b53" :question/math-academy-id "q-35953" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y=x$\n- The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y=-x$\n- The limit exists and is equal to $1$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}=\\frac{0\\cdot 0-0\\cdot {0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+2\\cdot 0\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(x)-x(x)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2x(x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-x}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-0}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(-x)-x(-x)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2x(-x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(1+x) \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186124983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4771d2c5-e76c-5c6d-b03d-8fc0e2f1b02c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186417795} {:answer/id #uuid "8c25eeeb-3961-56fd-8538-a5080285faaa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186417798} {:answer/id #uuid "b2486cd6-bba5-5ab1-b4ec-64ec7a261726" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186417802} {:answer/id #uuid "c8abcd16-e7db-5b58-b6bc-d3000736599d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186417805} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d9d96b-0d1b-5959-be29-c5d2995ca8f4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186417806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2486cd6-bba5-5ab1-b4ec-64ec7a261726" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186417802} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e293cef-2f84-5bc4-9212-2735610d6da3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186417811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b10043b-af03-5e4b-8ef7-b7ee2892ed6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36409" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(-1,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or inside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/bb/bbc3e1bfb40c12cfdc655b4207c5f34ea0a01ce254b3f04b96b05ffbbad3f5af.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ and $Q(-1,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/10/100a2b2c1aa58cdfa59980cae4253e1de8b4b2c984c0793c66f7e7cc6edb5211.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $Q$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is fully contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'"}] [{:db/id 17592186124995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bd9afc8-512b-56c2-afc6-b3347a088b73" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186321244} {:answer/id #uuid "3252858d-3260-5656-8f96-1eaaabf8ca26" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186321529} {:answer/id #uuid "582022fe-2db1-5582-ae97-406054e80b9f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186321838} {:answer/id #uuid "ecce7324-98e3-5316-83f2-15e820f4b790" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186323017} {:answer/id #uuid "f4bf132c-9f60-54a4-be17-6f37b30c73b6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186323063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bd9afc8-512b-56c2-afc6-b3347a088b73" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186321244} :answer-field/id #uuid "08a1e133-840b-570d-8100-ef89f0eed624" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186323183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cee8f940-b17d-518d-bf59-666639ac6929" :question/math-academy-id "q-36504" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{x+y})?$\n\n- Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,1)$\n- ${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $f$ is defined only when\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{x+y}>0.\n$$\n\nAt points in the domain of $f,$ we can compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1\\cdot (x+y)-x\\cdot 1}{(x+y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{y}{(x+y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x(x+y)} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{(x+y)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is defined at $(1,1),$ since\n\n$$\nf(1,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{1+1})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}),\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are defined there:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =\\frac{1}{1(1+1)}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,1) & =-\\frac{1}{1+1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. At $(0,1),$\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{x+y}=\\frac{0}{0+1}=0,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\nf(0,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined. Therefore, ${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is not defined.\n\n- Statement III is false for the same reason. Since $f$ is not defined at $(0,1),$ the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is not defined. The simplified expression $-\\frac{1}{x+y}$ cannot be used to define the partial derivative at a point outside the domain of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186125000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03b11e04-051b-51b5-8a91-13c6b71df68b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186321183} {:answer/id #uuid "217870f0-67b2-5594-9fd0-31238b20b606" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186321399} {:answer/id #uuid "a2bbb516-80a2-5407-9fb3-c81f9ad7db48" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186322432} {:answer/id #uuid "e3db67de-5384-5980-8136-1339023a68e2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186322932} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4beedf-b190-50ca-b9f4-b9efac457e7e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186323128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2bbb516-80a2-5407-9fb3-c81f9ad7db48" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186322432} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaeeded2-37a6-548d-9e4e-185ab818016b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186323632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff5209de-df39-5705-8c96-c53acb60628b" :question/math-academy-id "q-36522" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}?$\n\n- The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(1,-1,0)$\n- ${f}_{x}(0,1,1)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{z}(1,1,0)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})^{2}}\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x{z}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})^{2}}\\cdot (-2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y{z}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(1,-1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,-1,0)=\\frac{(0)^{2}}{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,1,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,1,1)=\\frac{(1)^{2}}{(0)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=-1,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(0,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1,1) & =-\\frac{2(0)(1)}{(0)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(1,1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,1,0)=\\frac{(0)^{2}}{(1)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'."}] [{:db/id 17592186125062 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124571 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef311af0-0e95-5574-a151-2971ad66bd08" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Eigenvalues of a 4x4 Matrix Containing a Block of Zeros"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08b84ff5-4873-5847-ad69-723f5136d9aa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186321215} {:answer/id #uuid "3ddd2395-53d8-54c0-bd86-c597005e66e2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186321632} {:answer/id #uuid "99197694-172f-58f3-819b-f3608767e4ed" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186322363} {:answer/id #uuid "d34c4ee0-a109-57af-bb18-6dbdec953f13" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186322798} {:answer/id #uuid "dca8a51f-11ad-5fb9-a502-863404f1d921" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186322872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dca8a51f-11ad-5fb9-a502-863404f1d921" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186322872} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1deec07-22ce-59a9-8917-9f15fef7cfa4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186323582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c699a77c-6393-5572-b010-6743d9f3bb17" :question/math-academy-id "q-37355" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -9 & -18 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -10 & 0 \\\\ -18 & 18 & -36 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\nII. $\\lambda =-10$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\nIII. The eigenvalue $\\lambda =-10$ has algebraic multiplicity $3$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,P-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nP-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -9 & -18 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -10-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -10-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -18 & 18 & -36 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(P-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $2$nd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -9 & -18 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -10-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -10-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -18 & 18 & -36 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-10-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -18 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -10-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -18 & -36 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $2$nd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-10-\\lambda )\\cdot (-10-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -18 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-10-\\lambda )^{2}((-1-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-54) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +10)^{2}({\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda -50) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +10)^{2}(\\lambda +10)(\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +10)^{3}(\\lambda -5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $P$ are $\\lambda =-10$ (with algebraic multiplicity $3$) and $\\lambda =5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186125067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdb2d3f5-d74a-5708-9193-da559a93e3a1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186123003} {:answer/id #uuid "f7242f4d-37c6-5a92-aea4-e1451ea3ff87" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186123004} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd2ad80-e3b2-5853-a503-997a3f10abbb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186123005} {:answer/id #uuid "3c98691f-2445-5880-944f-87cf5f407b96" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186123006} {:answer/id #uuid "5bda5d0c-e9fa-54b7-8032-3675a6282c00" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186123007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bda5d0c-e9fa-54b7-8032-3675a6282c00" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186123007} :answer-field/id #uuid "37fa1294-0d25-5a87-933a-a3795c1356c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186123002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a07adee-cd5f-5906-86f1-fee57d6ba6a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-37378" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{x} & 2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(1,0)>0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,0)>0$\n\nIII. The point $(1,0)$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,0)=det({f}^{″}(1,0))=2\\cdot 2-{0}^{2}=4>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,0)=({f}^{″}(1,0))_{11}=2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(1,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,0)>0,$ the point $(1,0)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186125076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cad3fbf-e03f-5c33-925e-1cd3a32b96bd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186321248} {:answer/id #uuid "4847d516-2434-5054-9209-a04048122935" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186321718} {:answer/id #uuid "a8426194-64d3-5cd3-9a08-0bf983d51dc4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186322465} {:answer/id #uuid "c8937a32-a7d0-5497-8752-ac5e45c950a1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186322703} {:answer/id #uuid "d9798d29-5e9f-52f8-98c5-4be1c0475165" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186322851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8937a32-a7d0-5497-8752-ac5e45c950a1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186322703} :answer-field/id #uuid "a69a25ed-0b0b-5f96-937f-7a3ab44d1467" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186323489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3be4d323-0e25-52c5-8eb8-5afe165a1c98" :question/math-academy-id "q-37523" :question/problem "A matrix $A$ has the characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{3}-{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda =0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lambda =3$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- $A$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =3$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{3}-{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (3)^{3}-(3)^{2}-6(3) & =0 \\\\ 27-9-18 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the characteristic equation. As a result, $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ according to the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186125153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124591 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b1bb974-826a-590f-8d32-cdc868ca827c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Polynomial Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2288da33-1c07-56f9-9d72-2c82796a5cb1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186327232} {:answer/id #uuid "642c6774-8862-5855-b26a-45048c2297c0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id 17592186327806} {:answer/id #uuid "99d3c4b8-a8d2-5ac9-85c4-6825e9556442" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id 17592186328264} {:answer/id #uuid "9e98f605-524d-5bc8-884f-cd7b91636a4d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186328300} {:answer/id #uuid "a9825f1b-1a80-5269-98a1-925bf03c1905" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186328403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2288da33-1c07-56f9-9d72-2c82796a5cb1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186327232} :answer-field/id #uuid "210ebe58-c5f0-58cc-82b3-1752b4e7838c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186329241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4e607a0-6afc-5179-8279-85636fe2408c" :question/math-academy-id "q-43769" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2,$ and its subset $B={{t}^{2}+at+b\\,|\\,a,b\\in R}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $B$ is closed under polynomial addition\n- $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $B$ is a subspace of ${R}_{2}[t]$\n- $B$ is a **not** a subspace of ${R}_{2}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(t)=g(t)={t}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & ={t}^{2}+{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(t)={t}^{2}$ and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =2{t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186125156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124488 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ea53dfa-f30a-58c3-a250-b57d66b11c6e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186327059} {:answer/id #uuid "18a3d24c-f123-5b0c-bc22-bbd190196e3f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186327148} {:answer/id #uuid "e7673b1a-cab2-527e-bd26-28796994e001" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186328957} {:answer/id #uuid "f45e7e0b-7434-58e5-8677-5856e2dcc54e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id 17592186329071} {:answer/id #uuid "f57f74c0-1f66-5a66-870b-6159162249c2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id 17592186329082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ea53dfa-f30a-58c3-a250-b57d66b11c6e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186327059} :answer-field/id #uuid "063dd20d-e886-581c-87ea-4937531b8db5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186329194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d2def00-4689-55b3-90a3-9823d892535b" :question/math-academy-id "q-43773" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $K$ containing all constant functions.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $K$ is closed under function addition\n- $K$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $K$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $K$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n\n*Hint: $f$ is a constant function if $f(x)=\\text{const}$ for all $x\\in [0,1].$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The sum of two constant functions is a constant function. So, $K$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. The product of a constant function by a scalar is again a constant function. So, $K$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $K$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186125165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09a1934b-5fbc-5908-a4bd-2f28e5f70ab8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186327015} {:answer/id #uuid "505d769d-bed6-572c-9f89-2cc476994466" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186327632} {:answer/id #uuid "e6aace94-71f5-5393-a1d9-8c1c1cdaa21d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186328952} {:answer/id #uuid "eb56dd8b-12c4-5d39-94e1-fe6f8788ade6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186328992} {:answer/id #uuid "fae2209d-a843-547e-b022-9f6f86e6a840" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186329133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fae2209d-a843-547e-b022-9f6f86e6a840" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186329133} :answer-field/id #uuid "12431824-94ed-5f8d-acbd-01425b4b5531" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186329217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0e0c0ef-5198-55d8-9aeb-10b338580777" :question/math-academy-id "q-47568" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(A-3I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- $\\text{Col}(A-3I)={R}^{2}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A-3I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A-3I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A-3I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $M=A-3I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A-3I)\\ne {R}^{2},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-3I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186125219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e69ab93-723d-5565-8b12-c9c9e92f4afe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186342360} {:answer/id #uuid "1766df50-f341-5eb6-89ac-0097e98a3fc8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186342444} {:answer/id #uuid "6815ad43-217d-52e9-a8da-0aa78e231e03" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186343124} {:answer/id #uuid "ca897927-fa4a-50ee-97a5-f96cb800a091" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186344018} {:answer/id #uuid "f42d0555-d3d6-510b-a3a2-94555e1c8df5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186344388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca897927-fa4a-50ee-97a5-f96cb800a091" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186344018} :answer-field/id #uuid "840022d0-492a-56fe-bf5a-c6203cea1ff0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186344725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73f62269-52b9-5341-87d2-165ee03b0821" :question/math-academy-id "q-74700" :question/problem "Let $U$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{3},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The zero-polynomial belongs to $U.$\n\nII. For any $p(x),q(x)\\in U,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x).$\n\nIII. For any $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in U,$ we have $[p(x)-q(x)]-r(x)=p(x)-[q(x)-r(x)].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the zero-polynomial can be represented as \n\n$$\n0=0\\cdot {x}^{3}.\n$$\n\n \nHence, $0\\in U.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in U,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\np(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x)\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, consider polynomials $p(x)={x}^{3},$ $q(x)=2{x}^{3},$ and $r(x)=3{x}^{3}$ from $U.$ Then: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[p(x)-q(x)]-r(x) & =[{x}^{3}-2{x}^{3}]-3{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-4{x}^{3} \\\\ p(x)-[q(x)-r(x)] & ={x}^{3}-[2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{3}] \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-[-{x}^{3}] \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $[p(x)-q(x)]-r(x)\\ne p(x)-[q(x)-r(x)].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186125241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124628 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f808ccf-b004-57a2-9163-b560aed3c40d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186358027} {:answer/id #uuid "28828aab-3810-5045-8068-cfca57fe82f4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186358117} {:answer/id #uuid "85921138-8064-5110-95f1-f36162bd60f4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186358925} {:answer/id #uuid "9c071abb-f059-5d2b-a1ab-912e941be17b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186359117} {:answer/id #uuid "ac164f9f-ddd3-54da-91ee-6573f958d979" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186359233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f808ccf-b004-57a2-9163-b560aed3c40d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186358027} :answer-field/id #uuid "c88eff33-055e-5cf0-9bab-aa2a49a23d0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186360340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d4d76d9-0c38-5edc-a8b9-c85d1bae32b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94694" :question/problem "Let $U$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{3},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $h(x)\\in U,$ where $h(x)=3x.$\n\nII. For any $p(x)\\in U$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot p(x)\\in U.$\n\nIII. The set is closed under scalar multiplication." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $U$ contains only polynomials of the form $a{x}^{3},$ where $a$ is a real coefficient. However, $h(x)=3x$ is *not* a polynomial of this form. So, $h(x)\\notin U.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $p(x)=a{x}^{3}\\in U,$ where $a$ is a real coefficient, and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\nk\\cdot p(x)=k(a{x}^{3})=(ka){x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $(ka)$ is a real coefficient, we conclude that $(ka){x}^{3}\\in U,$ i.e., $k\\cdot p(x)\\in U.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set is closed scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186125242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11895d5e-ec15-52d5-b1da-2ea8cfaa9f24" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186357901} {:answer/id #uuid "870e6c94-c16c-5951-84fe-ce7fd0ada5a7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186358935} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef7838b-433a-57b1-900f-2b0a4cd6178a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186358996} {:answer/id #uuid "a4947eec-4e77-5e84-956c-4edb215df1e8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186359181} {:answer/id #uuid "fce426a1-d549-58e3-baed-3495ef34d2fb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186359982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11895d5e-ec15-52d5-b1da-2ea8cfaa9f24" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186357901} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec006c64-72e5-50ae-ace2-52f11b2619ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186360420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd415fe2-3ead-5cbb-88ba-59d9d1273317" :question/math-academy-id "q-94740" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the set of all functions from $(0,\\infty )$ to $R$ of the form $f(x)=a{log}_{2}⁡(x),$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The scalar multiplication of functions in $L$ is associative.\n- For any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$ and $f(x)\\in L,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).$\n- For any $\\alpha \\in R$ and any $f(x),g(x)\\in L,$ we have $\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta f(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186125245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124489 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dc69c43-a9f7-5f43-890e-96fe06c46748" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186406857} {:answer/id #uuid "5129302f-a0d3-5c48-9cd2-710367d9ef9b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186406858} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2afd25-1b47-58bd-a63a-fb16ca010eec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186406866} {:answer/id #uuid "e47c35f7-6343-5a83-8c29-d0f93c6a25ce" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186406871} {:answer/id #uuid "fecfaf5f-f070-5827-a2e0-854b742a089f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186406874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba2afd25-1b47-58bd-a63a-fb16ca010eec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186406866} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a0b26bf-4452-55f8-96b7-34adf0df266d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186406877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cceedad6-c377-5d6a-a68c-beab3237c2c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-94866" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-matrix is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]=0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186125252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124529 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "517aa7ec-57ac-5705-8d2a-53d9f27e78be" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186358453} {:answer/id #uuid "8843e1ee-5a5e-5631-ae97-1ea5ab30c3ff" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186358942} {:answer/id #uuid "8d26c39b-f1fb-5173-8a71-2f0d4b4f9eda" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186358980} {:answer/id #uuid "cce0f752-83dd-5ad5-9178-0edc023149f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186359551} {:answer/id #uuid "f0be6f1d-d291-596f-b529-f9bb5684d0f0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186359895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cce0f752-83dd-5ad5-9178-0edc023149f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186359551} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dac5d5c-9735-5efd-841b-5656693eb71c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186360174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bef513f1-2d4e-54ff-9389-039c04a6d703" :question/math-academy-id "q-97051" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A+2I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{rank}(A+2I)=2$\n- $A+2I$ is singular\n- $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A+2I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(A+2I) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A+2I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A+2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=A+2I$ is singular, then $(A+2I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186125258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2193fe44-50a9-5f96-85a0-16da6f977f03" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186360980} {:answer/id #uuid "9c8627d1-932b-51e7-b170-cbf98c2024b8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186361534} {:answer/id #uuid "f1b97dd5-24b4-5ddd-b41d-c00ce9b6a2d4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186361913} {:answer/id #uuid "f27deee0-c031-5629-b7b8-c1cb6fee2879" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186361918} {:answer/id #uuid "f7bfd063-ea69-532f-926a-ca755a6b8b9d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186361945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c8627d1-932b-51e7-b170-cbf98c2024b8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186361534} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b3d4101-d9c5-5d4f-9654-044f6ba76ff2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b68a0678-9348-57e6-b4b3-d9e83bab09ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-97961" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A+I$ is a non-singular matrix\n- $\\text{Col}(A+I)={R}^{2}$\n- $\\text{Null}(A+I)\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A+I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A+I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A+I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II is false. Since $M=A+I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A+I)\\ne {R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statements III is true. Since $M=A+I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A+I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186129640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129213 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "434a82c2-7964-525a-85b9-eb17fa7b5b26" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Connections Between Matrices of a Linear Transformation Relative to Different Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f542f252-cabb-5ae6-be52-71ede74205d2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186127322} {:answer/id #uuid "f86e2a77-e373-53ff-91da-4afcea8bff3e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186127323} {:answer/id #uuid "01af984e-b98d-5360-becd-55598849a21e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186127324} {:answer/id #uuid "2360d955-fc90-55ae-8391-a0ca33c5c286" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186127325} {:answer/id #uuid "68dce08e-1ffa-5ce3-a38e-daf6da264321" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186127326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2360d955-fc90-55ae-8391-a0ca33c5c286" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186127325} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ab4619b-148b-5109-bb9a-98dd96196200" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186127321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a7452af-fe2e-5944-8a03-a396c12645a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-38360" :question/problem "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $B$ to basis $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$\n- $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$\n- $[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186134123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dea8612-414c-5394-869d-1e404b048156" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type III Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b60b0dc-df4e-5f81-bad5-1b7efb46b547" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186275995} {:answer/id #uuid "560e6196-dde7-531a-babe-e498edd4a6f0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186276241} {:answer/id #uuid "b56b5aa0-00ae-5c5c-a95c-2f81e03b489e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186277094} {:answer/id #uuid "c579a7d8-0a4b-57f1-811d-bf22cdb4cc69" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186277232} {:answer/id #uuid "f1b8890f-4cb0-571c-aad8-8b663380a1a9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186277599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c579a7d8-0a4b-57f1-811d-bf22cdb4cc69" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186277232} :answer-field/id #uuid "2338cdb8-2d73-5b36-8707-4432010db395" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99a0a793-b31c-5c54-888b-faa620b4ddae" :question/math-academy-id "q-160961" :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2x.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2x$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. First, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2x$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $y.$ Substituting this expression for $y$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={x}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $xz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have that $y$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$, and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,z)=2x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186134125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcc1bf5-5eea-5615-8b16-b8ced5de7984" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type II Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "263d3b33-2118-54fb-9157-e428ab2af71f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186275824} {:answer/id #uuid "719962e7-b1f1-507a-88a6-180cd32e0681" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186276500} {:answer/id #uuid "a374049e-6c16-5670-af31-6c40379ae628" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186276949} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a1960a-e082-5a26-b417-d0336dcda57c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186277209} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ceed4b-c5c8-570c-8560-a252e54c563e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186277505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7ceed4b-c5c8-570c-8560-a252e54c563e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186277505} :answer-field/id #uuid "16745e05-00c8-5e67-af3b-d78211d836b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186277764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "34378ce6-ab69-512f-b1fe-41e79c6115fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-160965" :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $x={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $x=2y.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,{g}_{1}(y)\\le z\\le {g}_{2}(y),\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=2y$\n- ${g}_{1}(y)=0$ and ${g}_{2}(y)=1-{y}^{2}$\n- $a=-1$ and $b=1$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type II representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z).$ Since all $x$ in the region are bounded *below* by $x={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and *above* by $x=2y,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(y,z)}\\le x\\le {{2y}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $x={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $x=2y$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y & ={y}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ ({y}^{2}-2y+1)+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. \n\n![](images/01/01cb7f25e98c8607258f673a5c5be6a1e55869b379c4484c05a04cebbe5dc5f6.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le z\\le {{\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=0$ and bounded *above* by $y=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{0}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le y\\le {{2}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186134227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b03ea89-5152-5ff4-813a-ca985d60ed5b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "252b8c31-0d1d-5fec-97bf-d410a5a76d2d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186324276} {:answer/id #uuid "91bdd21c-3884-5f46-b4ab-8dea96f936cf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186325206} {:answer/id #uuid "a42cbaf4-426e-5d63-9b83-f7c29afd84aa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186325366} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2b6f86-096b-59b1-b532-df21b5b82535" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186326070} {:answer/id #uuid "fa4a15bf-2013-5ecb-bb8b-43496b9988b3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186326174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "252b8c31-0d1d-5fec-97bf-d410a5a76d2d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186324276} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dc3fce0-7172-5e80-9dbb-cd4c06aeae43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186326412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c796dfc-19c9-5431-bc95-d9fa1ccc2efa" :question/math-academy-id "q-39623" :question/problem "For the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nII. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $V=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $H,V$ and $W$ as $\\{{h}_{1},{h}_{2}\\},\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ and $\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2}\\}$, respectively. \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-1)=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H.$\n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-3)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =1\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot (-1)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{3} & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot (-1)=-2\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is *not* orthogonal to the set $V.$\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 4+1\\cdot (-3)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =1\\cdot (2)+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1=2\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is *not* orthogonal to the set $W.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186134303 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e81030a-0151-5dea-b9bc-74aae2499482" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Orthogonal Projection Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14e03093-45e3-5a29-8e56-5c627469229d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186324158} {:answer/id #uuid "96f5d810-b983-5dd0-9374-08ba9c895889" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186325257} {:answer/id #uuid "c22e9da9-c19b-5939-ae6a-6ea72bf20ac6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186325638} {:answer/id #uuid "e35a4f72-3e21-5735-891d-271c0479b06c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186325968} {:answer/id #uuid "f4622c11-3b33-54f5-ad6f-ba2120f1d298" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186326123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e35a4f72-3e21-5735-891d-271c0479b06c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186325968} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9007cd6-14d2-52e3-b682-7062e520d6ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186326642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04ff9ec0-bc04-5bc6-a60f-5b7459d9c0ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-40662" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${A}^{T}=A$\n- ${A}^{2}=A$\n- $A$ is an orthogonal projection matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $Q$ is an orthogonal projection matrix if and only if ${Q}^{2}=Q$ and ${Q}^{T}=Q.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${A}^{T}=A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}20 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true since both statements I and II are true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186134414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abaeea03-6a2a-5d37-a2af-a9ea109175ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reasons Why a Determinant Equals Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d8e0b89-42bf-519d-9d97-5be58994dc91" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186327337} {:answer/id #uuid "43e90ae2-41ae-5a8b-bd0d-a84be897dc3e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186327526} {:answer/id #uuid "cd965883-d1dd-572c-b5e0-9d1d81b810b7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186328742} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d7cf84-3b4e-527a-beaf-e866d3ca0aa6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186328938} {:answer/id #uuid "f499f0e7-80e6-54c9-b9ec-b1cb786a0558" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186329076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d8e0b89-42bf-519d-9d97-5be58994dc91" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186327337} :answer-field/id #uuid "73a52cef-1ba2-5963-a084-9155ceff6344" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186329376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ef67ff18-5c70-5c00-bdf0-27b79dc90b72" :question/math-academy-id "q-43306" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $|A|=0.$ Which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. $A$ has two identical rows\n\nII. One of the rows/columns of $A$ is proportional to another row/column of $A$\n\nIII. $A$ has two identical columns" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I and III are false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe have that \n\n$$\n|A|=1\\cdot 8-4\\cdot 2=0\n$$\n\nbut $A$ has neither two identical rows nor two identical columns.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nIn the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, this means that one of the rows/columns must be proportional to another row/column.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186134434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10778a8b-7dba-5224-9e37-47a0aadec5f4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186327082} {:answer/id #uuid "a4311801-5138-5fda-a1d0-047b32a3b223" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186328355} {:answer/id #uuid "d59c2d87-1ef9-57e1-b2cb-7cb68d03fce7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186328802} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c08adc-5f73-5580-84a6-853eac64f9db" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186328836} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ae25fd-16ab-5fef-bc5f-e15edc6b79c7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186329086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5ae25fd-16ab-5fef-bc5f-e15edc6b79c7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186329086} :answer-field/id #uuid "be8fef60-6273-5a72-b265-d3cad6fa4786" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186329519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "65ce77e2-71c5-5054-a9e0-2b1a859bf906" :question/math-academy-id "q-43981" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2\\pi $\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }+{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi ))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\pi +\\pi )+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186134475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da20ffe7-173b-5fc0-8629-3b9b70222860" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4767179b-e760-5407-b987-281c5a994c5c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186327557} {:answer/id #uuid "6b926fe7-010d-5765-9b05-57838d45ac48" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186327860} {:answer/id #uuid "86a36528-0f78-50c5-9a03-d78ef2331796" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186328094} {:answer/id #uuid "bd47911c-9b82-5690-9142-16046e5e2086" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186328584} {:answer/id #uuid "ebbbd8bb-9a46-54da-a559-203f0e5113e9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186328997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86a36528-0f78-50c5-9a03-d78ef2331796" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186328094} :answer-field/id #uuid "25309370-e18c-5f8d-b9d3-a52855a34253" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186329252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14d8b1c2-9248-513d-b061-309a07f2a6a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-47458" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the orthogonal matrix $Q?$\n\n- ${Q}^{T}$ is orthogonal\n- The identity matrix $I$ is orthogonal\n- $2Q$ is orthogonal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the product ${Q}^{T}\\cdot ({Q}^{T})^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot ({Q}^{T})^{T} & ={Q}^{T}Q \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The columns of the identity matrix are orthonormal. Therefore, $I$ is an orthogonal matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. Computing the product $2Q\\cdot (2Q)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2Q\\cdot (2Q)^{T} & =2Q\\cdot 2{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =4Q{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =4I \\\\  & \\ne I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186134551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41142936-cfbe-5358-9b91-3e2d43f3c58b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186358304} {:answer/id #uuid "70a72cff-725b-53d4-b2cf-8f7f18a9bb31" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186358735} {:answer/id #uuid "bc46cd74-5213-519d-8f9e-edaff37ef6a6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186359388} {:answer/id #uuid "db810e3f-d7a4-5a4f-ab4b-03f9496d6199" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186359675} {:answer/id #uuid "f7701a76-35a6-5241-b465-7ea12079f8e3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186359942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db810e3f-d7a4-5a4f-ab4b-03f9496d6199" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186359675} :answer-field/id #uuid "65b84e28-d1a0-561a-90f6-59485ff13324" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186360157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45f8ab8c-12d7-5df5-b151-ee8250c362bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-96833" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n- $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement II is false. To factor out a constant multiple from the second argument, we must change it to its complex conjugate.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number in general.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186134552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c6180fa-2359-5a99-b2b5-34128319c8d8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186358412} {:answer/id #uuid "7092b4b7-325b-5d7b-b9c6-c4bd41b138b9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186358733} {:answer/id #uuid "83d09de0-992e-5b1e-a4ea-45adb87c5408" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186358911} {:answer/id #uuid "bc73eac1-a2fb-5cac-8557-c079683d8c0f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186359390} {:answer/id #uuid "fe8e6bf0-b059-5de6-9ae3-ca00875b1dfa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186359991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7092b4b7-325b-5d7b-b9c6-c4bd41b138b9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186358733} :answer-field/id #uuid "42d61567-5eab-57c4-ad4f-14de1b67d5e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186360095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f527ee5b-22e1-5696-9bd4-0e444190bb44" :question/math-academy-id "q-97305" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,0⟩=0$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $2⟨a,b⟩=⟨a,-2b⟩$ for any  $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. By the antilinearity in the second argument we have that  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,0⟩ & =⟨a,0\\cdot 0⟩=\\overset{―}{0}⟨a,0⟩=0⟨a,0⟩=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. By the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,-2b⟩=\\overset{―}{(-2)}⟨a,b⟩=-2⟨a,b⟩\\ne 2⟨a,b⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number, in general.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186134557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12f9020e-83ec-5df7-80bf-cc00b4552045" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186357913} {:answer/id #uuid "3a3a03df-0544-5fe2-8d9e-660b17164ad7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186358262} {:answer/id #uuid "4760fcbe-f25b-58f0-b9b7-40841152fb7c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186358366} {:answer/id #uuid "6f977601-6585-5dd5-9942-3f690514f370" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186358722} {:answer/id #uuid "7082d1bc-5f39-5a4b-8cb1-aab831d836b3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186358732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4760fcbe-f25b-58f0-b9b7-40841152fb7c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186358366} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f2385db-7d71-5467-9779-556ead63ba51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186360066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3c169d7e-31f7-5721-948b-24c63fd291d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-97451" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2\\pi $\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )).$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the given identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-3t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)}{4}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\pi )}{4}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186148175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60fa0f9c-bae5-51cb-8799-a9c8917d8f0d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186333870} {:answer/id #uuid "9abe6754-b348-56ad-9faa-a28fca8c1fd9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186334359} {:answer/id #uuid "ad826703-d06c-542f-b160-56dcd681b5a7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186334520} {:answer/id #uuid "ae32c2a5-78fd-5cd9-a0b3-e8646064834c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id 17592186334524} {:answer/id #uuid "cea03520-191b-5259-a7a2-7ad74d03b7d6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186334792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae32c2a5-78fd-5cd9-a0b3-e8646064834c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id 17592186334524} :answer-field/id #uuid "76582c08-f455-5c5c-a722-a27fb8767982" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186335466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55cfa82a-9072-54fc-bfe1-353f6a508e53" :question/math-academy-id "q-64576" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+10{y}^{′}+25y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+3y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping" :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={2}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =-4<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement I is correct.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+10{y}^{′}+25y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +25=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={10}^{2}-4(1)(25) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement II is correct.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+3y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +3=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={5}^{2}-4(1)(3) & =13>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement III is correct.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186148179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "398637b9-9de8-5603-8107-020a5725e120" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186333526} {:answer/id #uuid "5d6f2b94-98ce-59eb-9977-cd986817c2ff" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186333844} {:answer/id #uuid "75af940b-97d4-5a46-8776-33f173701d48" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186334052} {:answer/id #uuid "d124aebd-c863-5507-aa4c-186618654ad0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186334815} {:answer/id #uuid "f57a5947-95ad-5807-ba8c-d3f854bb8495" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186335142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75af940b-97d4-5a46-8776-33f173701d48" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186334052} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d4eec8b-2a2a-5ef1-a4bf-644458b1a312" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186335532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5a95c4f-6f0b-5fe1-b32c-ae9f75fde633" :question/math-academy-id "q-64617" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n\nII. ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+8y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping.\n\nIII. ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping." :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +7=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={3}^{2}-4(1)(7) & =-19<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement I is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+8y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(8) & =4>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement II is correct.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={8}^{2}-4(1)(16) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186148197 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "277ea4c2-b095-5302-8554-096a68fb2d62" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186336400} {:answer/id #uuid "71dc7733-2769-570f-8ea5-f271a16e4888" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186337051} {:answer/id #uuid "8c31f83e-254b-5fa6-acd0-6920afa48391" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186337302} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d7e380-ed7f-5aa8-bddb-a9dbeeaf4491" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186337516} {:answer/id #uuid "ef6d5a8c-a78a-5649-b2ca-f8c09e74fcaa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186338129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c31f83e-254b-5fa6-acd0-6920afa48391" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186337302} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bace304-8adf-5d3c-bdab-afe547432273" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ddee53f3-5b0d-5dc0-9b9e-825db292f3e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-71110" :question/problem "Given the two planes $3x-y+z=0$ and $x-z+4=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes are parallel.\n- The planes intersect on a line." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x-y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,-1,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-z+4=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,-1,1⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+4j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,4,1⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186148198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4842e95d-7478-5e61-8e97-0569f4aba80e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186336700} {:answer/id #uuid "5e795725-b836-5864-ab56-5d08aa98f334" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186336890} {:answer/id #uuid "674e190c-095c-5a32-a660-8e0e15b8da20" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186336955} {:answer/id #uuid "8f9ba1ff-d028-59eb-91d3-ed4a2a264754" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186337329} {:answer/id #uuid "bd5d65cf-8f26-5d2d-b7f3-57567712e43e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186337716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4842e95d-7478-5e61-8e97-0569f4aba80e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186336700} :answer-field/id #uuid "19b8455e-1c0e-5ab5-a911-7a1e4db64e32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c8314953-42cd-5ac5-902d-92a3c570bac9" :question/math-academy-id "q-71112" :question/problem "Given the two planes $-x-2y+z=0$ and $2x+4y-2z+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n- The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n- The planes are coincident." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $-x-2y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-1,-2,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y-2z+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,4,-2⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${n}_{2}=-2{n}_{1}.$ So, the normal vectors are parallel.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. \n\nFor the two planes to coincide, the equation of one plane should be a scalar multiple of the other. However, if we try to manipulate the equation of the second plane to resemble the equation of the first plane, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4y-2z+1=0 \\\\ x+2y-z+\\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ -x-2y+z-\\frac{1}{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nClearly, this equation is not the same as the equation of the first plane. So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186152382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23cd2439-d216-5cbf-bf00-7ad9407dacb7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186241491} {:answer/id #uuid "2b44f6e5-88b2-590f-96b6-ef4b64192cd5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186241562} {:answer/id #uuid "811d0610-0dd3-509c-a04f-c67e87559e8f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186242311} {:answer/id #uuid "b49460a7-1387-524f-bfec-7e510bcd5344" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186242758} {:answer/id #uuid "bd073272-d100-5bc2-8647-20da76fb7a00" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186242841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23cd2439-d216-5cbf-bf00-7ad9407dacb7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186241491} :answer-field/id #uuid "3750e017-5e8e-5458-a060-9ecd41a3fa30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4b429ab-49a9-5137-bfd6-13315591e96f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109243" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A-5I$ and $A$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is a generalized  eigenvector of rank $2$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ of $A$\n\nII. $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of $A$\n\nIII. $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nv\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-5I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is an generalized eigenvector of rank $2$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A=A-0I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since\n\n$$\nu\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-5I$ maps $u$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3,$ not $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186152502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152067 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "968df6ec-e87e-5770-9c11-e7c8d8a27e6c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Relations on Finite Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d66d2717-83d7-51b0-a227-fa1fc6809409" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186149569} {:answer/id #uuid "30eef23f-1a55-5ac4-8a57-9c63c6f949e7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186149570} {:answer/id #uuid "5b128d60-e594-5aa5-bff5-a1e2fa17357d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186149571} {:answer/id #uuid "66c8be3b-ec27-54c3-a77d-bf9d20dd0b93" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id 17592186149572} {:answer/id #uuid "7410e289-bd9b-5799-8292-33605aba5faf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186149573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d66d2717-83d7-51b0-a227-fa1fc6809409" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186149569} :answer-field/id #uuid "692a1b0c-9534-5975-bd7a-3a92a99093ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186149568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14a51040-e179-5b66-9e0b-fca0e478f23c" :question/math-academy-id "q-214171" :question/problem "Consider the relation $R$ on the set $A={0,1,2}$ defined as \n\n$$\nR={(1,1),(0,2),(2,0)}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $R$ is reflexive\n- $R$ is symmetric\n- $R$ is transitive\n- $R$ is an equivalence relation" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- The relation $R$ is *not* reflexive because  $(0,0)\\notin R.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is symmetric because $(y,x)\\in R$ whenever $(x,y)\\in R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The relation $R$ is *not* transitive because $(0,2)\\in R$ and $(2,0)\\in R,$ but $(0,0)\\notin R$\n\n- The relation is *not* an equivalence relation since it's neither reflexive nor transitive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186152747 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152074 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eebe007-8a95-578d-b553-e37a7bd8a327" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Negative Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e6491d9-1d90-5386-995a-284889bdd49e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186409351} {:answer/id #uuid "2b860813-d4b1-5fef-b506-6ffcc8c555af" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186409357} {:answer/id #uuid "2e4173a3-d315-5fb7-9f00-3bd771b4750f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186409359} {:answer/id #uuid "956c7dd0-6d81-5565-ae83-7839bf085246" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186409387} {:answer/id #uuid "b68067c2-b8d7-5f62-b933-7366f89a68bb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186409400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "956c7dd0-6d81-5565-ae83-7839bf085246" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186409387} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b7ce273-1469-5ebe-853b-6ac76cc6e24b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186409421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc725849-ec72-5565-a2a3-c18e7bade536" :question/math-academy-id "q-76784" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $11≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$\n- $27≡15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$\n- $30≡-18\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true because $5∣(11-1).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(11-1)÷5=10÷5=2\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false because $7∤(27-15).$ Notice that $27-15=12,$ which is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- Statement III is true because $4\\,|\\,(30-(-18)).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(30-(-18))÷4=48÷4=12\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186152758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152073 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "398716c2-d0e6-5106-a702-2f71d957726f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02e87fd7-263e-5c85-ae11-ab373b3633a4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186409342} {:answer/id #uuid "05752e2f-49d8-5029-8640-38d4fb2c4302" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186409344} {:answer/id #uuid "79a9d6c6-5a61-5865-93e5-90b3c4aec498" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186409379} {:answer/id #uuid "d45d7b30-c602-5bbb-b327-69ce48a4fb37" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186409408} {:answer/id #uuid "e8143651-dbed-5f90-b38d-d9e1c91175c1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186409414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02e87fd7-263e-5c85-ae11-ab373b3633a4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186409342} :answer-field/id #uuid "d27defb4-bb86-5b12-8d1d-2f258f68bd34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186409431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "665aa776-ec33-596f-ae58-1e8a076473e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-76952" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $19≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n- $22≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $20≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $4∤(19-5).$ Notice that $19-5=14,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Statement II is true because $3∣(22-7).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(22-7)÷3=15÷3=5\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false because $8∤(20-9).$ Notice that $20-5=15,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186152878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152126 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7063140-eebe-598c-b530-3c13509d359b" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Numbers as Rational"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d28a50a6-7308-5e7d-9506-1e4175511186" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186151776} {:answer/id #uuid "ed8b1e51-4f39-5465-8987-f4a307226cf5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186151777} {:answer/id #uuid "171962fe-cdfc-56f5-897b-aa03946cf77e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186151778} {:answer/id #uuid "61310617-8554-57e3-9ce9-7002b33a7a58" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186151779} {:answer/id #uuid "643cedf7-507f-5e63-91cf-0aa5f8a535ee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186151780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "643cedf7-507f-5e63-91cf-0aa5f8a535ee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186151780} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd4be8de-607c-5578-91b6-081cf9216fab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186151775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0d528fe-7741-52a6-a947-e2ff7f9a758d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98382" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $-2$ is a natural number\n\nII. $-2$ is an integer\n\nIII. $-2$ is a rational number" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall our definitions.\n\n- The natural numbers consist of all positive whole numbers: $1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,5,…$\n\n- The integers consist of all of the positive and negative whole numbers, and zero: $…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$\n\n- The rationals consist of any number that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $-2$ is not a natural number.\n\n- $-2$ is an integer.\n\n- $-2$ is a rational number because it can be written as $\\frac{(-2)}{1}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186157753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186157453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bf2e96a-8b3a-5175-a84c-79bb426aa3af" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186305253} {:answer/id #uuid "1e830247-ccc1-59cf-8ab1-52e97736b3f9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186305440} {:answer/id #uuid "5d2b201d-68ed-5c22-a614-3f40b0102596" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186306048} {:answer/id #uuid "5f4d336c-e643-5ced-921e-470ef6e9ebbd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186306068} {:answer/id #uuid "a41a73eb-f8d7-52b1-8075-5729f765c02a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186306697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f4d336c-e643-5ced-921e-470ef6e9ebbd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186306068} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8a891d9-a977-5225-95b8-2aaad9af9dd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186308311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0a1373b-0f2f-546e-8300-5cb2dc8f926e" :question/math-academy-id "q-334693" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=x+2{e}^{2x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({e}^{2x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({e}^{3x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty .$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=x+2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x+2{e}^{2x})^{′}}{(x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1+4{e}^{2x}}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1+4{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)={e}^{2x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{2x}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}+2 \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x)^{′}}{({e}^{2x})^{′}}+2 \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2x}}+2 \\\\  & =0+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({e}^{2x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x)={e}^{3x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{3x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{x}{{e}^{3x}}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{3x}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{e}^{3x}}+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }2{e}^{-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x)^{′}}{({e}^{3x})^{′}}+0 \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{3{e}^{3x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({e}^{3x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186157980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186157368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f66a476-e65f-57ab-8b49-0ce935eee143" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186354928} {:answer/id #uuid "2562860b-9b12-5dc3-940b-b70009c64f28" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186354976} {:answer/id #uuid "33dddaf2-ccc4-5a95-a6a4-01a05a938888" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186355122} {:answer/id #uuid "73340b9a-a4b7-52a6-beef-165ab017eb23" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186355679} {:answer/id #uuid "d9b24e70-cfc1-54ae-aee2-0a7f0ebc97cf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186356574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33dddaf2-ccc4-5a95-a6a4-01a05a938888" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186355122} :answer-field/id #uuid "c53bf1fa-e6e2-5962-8491-0b6cab04e1b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186357274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "71ecf34a-b807-5183-a2fd-953c773f847a" :question/math-academy-id "q-89428" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2{x}^{3}-y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(1,2)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(1,2)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(1,2)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n- $(1,2)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(1,2),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)} & ={(2{x}^{3}-y)|}_{(1,2)} \\\\  & =2(1)^{3}-(2) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-(2{x}^{3}-y) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}+y \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,2)} & ={(6{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}+y)|}_{(1,2)} \\\\  & =6(1)^{2}-2(1)^{3}+(2) \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)}=0,\n$$\n\n we obtain that $(1,2)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is a critical point at $(1,2).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,2)}>0,\n$$\n\n we have a local minimum (not a local maximum) at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186162343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bddcdc3-0117-5789-a3ad-ba6aafc6b104" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186383377} {:answer/id #uuid "a1249cf7-75f0-5961-81c3-1869c734e389" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186383378} {:answer/id #uuid "efd31bc8-a127-522a-8023-054d96b8314f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186383379} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f97e0c-20e3-563b-8b65-319653d528c7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186383380} {:answer/id #uuid "439ce31e-29f2-52ad-8058-a3a0cdd5a72f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186383381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bddcdc3-0117-5789-a3ad-ba6aafc6b104" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186383377} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aa5d990-d2c9-53e1-986d-feac4f9aa275" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186383376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "25122d3d-9cb1-5f5e-b327-d0566bb4de5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-136853" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.6$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(X=1,Y=1)=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n\nII. $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.6$ | $0.75$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=1)=f(1,1)=0.6.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.75\\cdot 0.8=0.6.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =0.05=0.25\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,0) & =0.15=0.75\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(0,1) & =0.20=0.25\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186162464 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186161919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "559e653a-2ab9-54a8-84e3-02bf106a8f47" :knowledge-point/title "Inferring Divisibility in Algebraic Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "077fe147-bb8c-5245-adec-2f208cac3d00" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186407342} {:answer/id #uuid "4e16abfb-19a2-5993-ba56-d6ea9e861af4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186407371} {:answer/id #uuid "8d91791d-f4c6-51bc-b004-2d1b8f7e6d98" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186407404} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ada6aa-c40c-51ac-b29e-9b542bd9b596" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186407415} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d961f4-d0e1-5413-9854-bf9e8c1f9ccb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186407436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d91791d-f4c6-51bc-b004-2d1b8f7e6d98" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186407404} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce80592a-d65e-5a24-ac6e-46f8654903b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186407485}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f097a3f6-b1f4-531f-833b-43edfbbc8726" :question/math-academy-id "q-221882" :question/problem "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\n- $6∣(42x+24y)$\n- $7∣(42x+24y)$\n- $2∣(42x+24y)$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n42x+24y=6(7x+4y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $7x+4y$ must be an integer, too. So, $6∣(42x+24y).$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $x=0$ and $y=1,$ we have $42(0)+24(1)=24,$ which is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n42x+24y=2(21x+12y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $21x+12y$ must be an integer, too. So, $2∣(42x+24y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186162568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186161921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ad9700-5aaa-5b00-8d2f-acce6ed59c9a" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fed5044-9dc6-5bdb-b912-3e1c49523233" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186409939} {:answer/id #uuid "233d2adf-428c-5279-b1bb-5fdd3b009f28" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186409942} {:answer/id #uuid "803100ed-12d4-50e9-86ef-adf6635a27b2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186409961} {:answer/id #uuid "8839c5c1-33ac-599b-9b3b-008f227eed3f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186409965} {:answer/id #uuid "bed36c99-5a14-5541-a946-9449a10dcdbd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186409985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "233d2adf-428c-5279-b1bb-5fdd3b009f28" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186409942} :answer-field/id #uuid "1528434c-93fa-59dc-b65b-46299ca0a431" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186409993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "615b6ed0-1120-5f2d-a6c0-2c676050f1ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-90846" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)={3}^{x}?$\n\nI. The range of $f$ is $(0,\\infty )$\n\nII. $∀\\,y\\in R,$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n\nIII. $f$ maps $R$ **into** $R$" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/a2/a2a6352a60b420f8721d6c7e4553d3157cfb045a39b6a5119c11c5c8189023e2.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the function takes all $y$-values greater than $y=0$, excluding $y=0$ itself. So the range of the function is indeed $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false. Take, for example, $y=-1.$ Since the range of the function is $(0,\\infty ),$ there can' t be an $x\\in R$ whose image is $-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the function is not a surjection.  So, $f$ maps $R$ *into* $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186162637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162009 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4105f1cb-eb48-5946-92f7-cd5e67689912" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type by Calculating the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0083218c-aba9-5d4d-b323-2dc13bcc37a8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186354661} {:answer/id #uuid "bf668c7d-67c5-589e-9066-9cfa85ecdf23" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186356338} {:answer/id #uuid "c02ef90a-4b7d-5c3d-86af-85e9c052f575" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186356346} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e52708-e9c7-51c4-b416-ec80ac478adb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186356413} {:answer/id #uuid "f932dedf-4976-5e69-84af-ddcad303b44f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186356840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0083218c-aba9-5d4d-b323-2dc13bcc37a8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186354661} :answer-field/id #uuid "07e85be0-6fb7-542e-a964-f2590b051fa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186356931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb99fd8a-40c2-5ae9-b122-b1e03bf9e31a" :question/math-academy-id "q-94465" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is indefinite" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(-6-\\lambda )-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -70 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-7,\\,10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186162638 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162010 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbebdd1b-0716-55cb-8df6-12a35b517ab5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6315d0e1-42d4-5c4e-aba1-c811b0d9e0f0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186355547} {:answer/id #uuid "7e9dc734-033b-5536-aeb2-bc5d613df824" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186355780} {:answer/id #uuid "d8498f0f-24f3-531e-bc2d-67ae855a8974" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186356560} {:answer/id #uuid "eacea395-10f3-5b0a-97f9-9e63077830b8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186356714} {:answer/id #uuid "f437379a-f3cf-5c0a-8f35-0368b6280e04" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186356796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f437379a-f3cf-5c0a-8f35-0368b6280e04" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186356796} :answer-field/id #uuid "f04840df-52d1-5d7c-a82e-494bc20ca2e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186357335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a27d2cf-0561-5715-9600-290d8804741e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94466" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=9$ and $det(A)=18$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=9$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}|=18.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=9>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}|=18>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186162676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16b1023c-e16f-5a0d-9093-f4068f33116d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186357949} {:answer/id #uuid "19c030d7-744f-525b-9821-64d02a9665ce" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186357978} {:answer/id #uuid "23e55a89-9b72-58a1-9bb3-18f3c2ba80d7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186358070} {:answer/id #uuid "5548d6d2-8ca2-5f9c-abc7-055657279605" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186358493} {:answer/id #uuid "e4264b76-4d84-5efd-8bdb-1605e0f2e213" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186359776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19c030d7-744f-525b-9821-64d02a9665ce" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186357978} :answer-field/id #uuid "9258361e-f4aa-5fce-b13d-158d1aa2d881" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186360247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8be226ee-498f-567c-82d1-206ed205336b" :question/math-academy-id "q-96046" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- If $A$ is *any* matrix, then $A{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n- The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n- The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A{A}^{T})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{T}{A}^{T} \\\\  & =A{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186167265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43a4ba53-acd7-5305-ab25-bfacca0b23df" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186309260} {:answer/id #uuid "773c9312-be0b-5424-ad66-40ee9439cabc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186309682} {:answer/id #uuid "7ab5dc75-7896-54ab-bb11-8f4d772b75bb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186309706} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e5764f-f500-540c-868d-2767df488781" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186310112} {:answer/id #uuid "ac9c7d96-0db0-52e5-8bef-fabdfc515ffa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186310142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ab5dc75-7896-54ab-bb11-8f4d772b75bb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186309706} :answer-field/id #uuid "82d27a9e-13d4-590a-b9ec-44b8440da9b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186311155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97cef292-5a85-5947-928d-d07517a20540" :question/math-academy-id "q-338489" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi $-periodicity: \n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $\\pi $-periodic, because for any $x\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(x+\\pi ))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $\\pi $-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $f$ is $\\pi $-periodic, shifting by $2\\pi $ does not change the value of $f,$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{2})=f((x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\pi )=f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ does not always equal $f(x).$ For example, take $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=F(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{2})\\ne f(x)$ for $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186172300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72015667-33a9-53e2-a0c4-09d8ce916b54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Are Independent of Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2723dc72-fd63-54a4-bb39-b1f78ca7b53b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186415206} {:answer/id #uuid "5c258c27-caa9-5d08-8078-126afe0392e1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186415217} {:answer/id #uuid "72010d15-094f-5946-8dbc-32f0cde47a90" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186415223} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d0ed6f-03ab-58cb-b103-fad5d1a3eabc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186415245} {:answer/id #uuid "cc091e6f-2ffe-5090-bd21-1c547785f021" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186415247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc091e6f-2ffe-5090-bd21-1c547785f021" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186415247} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd25f073-c3c7-52b2-85d6-ded0f2d4da84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186415268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77c64f72-bc4e-5149-99d1-c9f993fca3fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-125663" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{x+y},\\,{e}^{x+y}⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $F$ is conservative.\n\nII. ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1),$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & =e\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1 \\\\  & =2e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186179993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186179770 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e39ae7fc-ff0a-5b97-8e13-1f15b12a07e5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Invertible Affine Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f2eb645-a4f1-5922-85bd-c5f103f512f8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186257350} {:answer/id #uuid "4d94cb3a-572c-5729-8df1-02ad9167fd86" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186257449} {:answer/id #uuid "70dc234c-6339-5727-b738-797b5cd95b74" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186257774} {:answer/id #uuid "bdfb46fe-1c68-52b5-901b-f6d055f2ed25" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186258439} {:answer/id #uuid "f775cb4b-1fea-56f6-bcfd-066102a8734d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186258952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f2eb645-a4f1-5922-85bd-c5f103f512f8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186257350} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e83dc19-3afb-586c-8d39-b2cf5af58b97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186259249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "267c4ee8-beb7-545b-be5a-57b42fce6778" :question/math-academy-id "q-124111" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=u-v,\\,y=u+2v+1.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=0$\n- The transformation is invertible" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ u+2v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ u+2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\ne [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1=3\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $det(A)\\ne 0,$ the affine transformation is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186180004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186179769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36996ffc-7dbc-51f8-8efe-46ed2c897045" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Affine Transformation is Orientation-Preserving"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c730df9-6548-5576-ba64-558226099add" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186364915} {:answer/id #uuid "b366d454-2c12-5c37-a81b-f48f317a5394" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186364916} {:answer/id #uuid "86c803ea-012c-568d-90f8-bce9b0ec9560" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186364917} {:answer/id #uuid "cb699b26-8700-53e1-89a0-b258c6ab715c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186364918} {:answer/id #uuid "a9a37d2a-7bd5-528f-9255-1cf91b830b77" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186364919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86c803ea-012c-568d-90f8-bce9b0ec9560" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186364917} :answer-field/id #uuid "3654e763-779e-5611-b4b5-b394a98f43a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186364914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "91a59d9f-f2b5-5450-b850-f776f534f3f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-124199" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=3u-v+2,\\,y=v-u.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $det(A)=-2$\n\nIII. The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3u-v+2 \\\\ v-u\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3u-v \\\\ -u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1)=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $det(A)>0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186180027 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186179743 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da220a9-2afc-5b63-a423-a4d2b3de9567" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation in Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22e6bfcd-38c6-52e4-8b81-16a2ee609b0e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186257073} {:answer/id #uuid "6aff1133-9d93-57b2-bdf9-2149c367e3d3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186257725} {:answer/id #uuid "80d6cb62-0324-5c5d-99ae-4c9a206230fa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186257925} {:answer/id #uuid "8248f5bf-f517-5f8c-86de-f89163724871" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186257938} {:answer/id #uuid "c2e0b5ef-abd8-5ce4-84a4-4b4da2a4de2f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186258493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2e0b5ef-abd8-5ce4-84a4-4b4da2a4de2f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186258493} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f3cfd9b-f898-53b9-910d-19385137f770" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186259145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dfac5beb-f3c0-5ece-80d7-1d1e280a780f" :question/math-academy-id "q-125432" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=-\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i-\\frac{2y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is conservative\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{4xy}{(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186184880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186184197 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8063bc9c-a10d-562b-93b1-508b4b26a674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Singular Values of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "058c04c6-70ed-5f40-82ed-2d503073a803" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186416439} {:answer/id #uuid "21ea85fd-6292-5db7-9dc1-8fed164bc983" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186416440} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad14134-11d1-5b66-b73a-b208f1547b8a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186416454} {:answer/id #uuid "dc81e283-4f32-52f2-9dc0-2d02dfad8da9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186416468} {:answer/id #uuid "ef49fb2b-88b6-550b-873b-58a76d4cd449" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186416471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ad14134-11d1-5b66-b73a-b208f1547b8a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186416454} :answer-field/id #uuid "2360b526-d791-5f7a-b1d6-add9140e9172" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186416475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca5e4c86-b607-523c-a857-24250b844373" :question/math-academy-id "q-95299" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Any $6\\times 8$ matrix $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values\n- Any $10\\times 8$ matrix $A$ has at most $10$ distinct singular values.\n- The matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ has two non-negative singular values." :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $6\\times 8$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $8\\times 6$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $8\\times 8$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{8\\times 6}\\times {A}_{6\\times 8}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{8\\times 8}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $A$ is a $10\\times 8$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $8\\times 10$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $8\\times 8$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{8\\times 10}\\times {A}_{10\\times 8}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{8\\times 8}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement III is true.  Indeed, all singular values of any matrix are non-negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186188925 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5540b73e-76fe-5b3b-9e59-bcdd8e64db55" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186406815} {:answer/id #uuid "6da2df3c-89eb-5372-97ad-f660691bfcb8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186406817} {:answer/id #uuid "82cdbbed-d3b0-5cbd-98ec-7fd44334fdc9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186406819} {:answer/id #uuid "8c7f5f0b-b8d0-5234-8de6-49e97dadce22" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186406820} {:answer/id #uuid "d681f156-99f3-5753-a570-2bb3e53d88eb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186406827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82cdbbed-d3b0-5cbd-98ec-7fd44334fdc9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186406819} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a41ad32-7f22-5720-8aeb-d6772bd219f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186406836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b3a8f74-a4d2-5020-9ea6-4077acac1b2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-47524" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $\\text{Im}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,3x+2y-z=0}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$\n- If ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are two different solutions to $Tx=[1,-2,1]^{T}$ then $T$ is invertible\n- If ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}$, then $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Solving $3x+2y-z=0$ for $z,$ we get $z=3x+2y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ 3x+2y\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+y{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are two different solutions to $Tx=[1,-2,1]^{T},$ then \n\n$$\nT{v}_{1}=T{v}_{2}\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}$ is a non-zero vector. This means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Null}(T)\\ne {0}$ and, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $T$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. If the columns of $T$ are such that ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}+2{v}_{2},$ then the set of vectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent and therefore $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Now, according to the matrix invertible theorem, we obtain that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186193133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "441a1de0-dc64-59fe-99a8-0ab502841425" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Whether a Given Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3db6b552-d8cb-5eb8-8ab9-86c9349c6a1e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186272917} {:answer/id #uuid "5e0ab39c-6d94-5ca3-9930-01fc2c85e10c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186273194} {:answer/id #uuid "670ec8c8-79d9-5ba1-a50b-e10f88c92b40" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186273271} {:answer/id #uuid "85e4bdb1-8ef2-579b-a451-b627c7753440" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186273538} {:answer/id #uuid "fca063bf-2f9d-505a-bbdc-b4d448d753cd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186274608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fca063bf-2f9d-505a-bbdc-b4d448d753cd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186274608} :answer-field/id #uuid "94c9e6d9-7389-51c0-ab13-de6c7eadca2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186274891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e8f7202f-949a-5544-b1eb-75cc6a08bdaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-154696" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-x},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 1$, we have $f(x)={e}^{1-x}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{1-x}]}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =0-(-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186193206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "114232b4-2761-5665-9296-c185bd484017" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186275631} {:answer/id #uuid "7880da2d-e957-5ca8-8e13-bcb627935744" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186276569} {:answer/id #uuid "8c2c725f-185f-5f35-871e-79e343f1ed7a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186276749} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c9fbc8-b14c-5cbc-8b85-1785d478d974" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186276972} {:answer/id #uuid "f330e754-04e9-557c-9d3e-6d2ab7556a6d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186277609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f330e754-04e9-557c-9d3e-6d2ab7556a6d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186277609} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8880f9f-c13c-59f9-8dce-1a13792353a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186278077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "68256c62-7faf-5747-9e8c-b2c67a0a95a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-161444" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 4\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n\nII. $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$\n\nIII. $∂A⊂A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/62/62c410c21e79e1bc688c1ff1a3440a207c62bcad42525bdc93fa2770b76d48bd.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/33/33a4738b642443c2535d347aa53ee2b7b80c800142c4844d94578769118ebfbc.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊂A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186199012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b3101aa-ee9e-5940-b170-2f4e78802150" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Divisibility of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aea82579-6011-5d78-815f-f8aabe5c4c68" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186197943} {:answer/id #uuid "bc39ae1c-88fb-59c0-9d44-13ef1d661cec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186197944} {:answer/id #uuid "d784b2c9-3ed2-578f-a801-9d465553f6bc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186197945} {:answer/id #uuid "06483019-fec0-52f6-9a75-e64b84b77d43" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186197946} {:answer/id #uuid "791b39e6-b54c-5276-8c55-f6bb8fdf4bec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186197947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d784b2c9-3ed2-578f-a801-9d465553f6bc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186197945} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f973ddc-35f8-5608-8069-a0955b9994f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186197942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6ff943b-6246-50f5-830d-737c87bafa4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-92248" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $15\\,|\\,32b$ then $15\\,|\\,b$\n\nII. If ${7}^{2}\\,|\\,7b$ then ${7}^{2}\\,|\\,b$\n\nIII. If ${3}^{2}\\,|\\,25b$ then $9\\,|\\,b$" :question/worked-solution "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$c\\,|\\,ab\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $\\,c\\,|\\,b.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(15,32)=1.$ Therefore, we have $15\\,|\\,32b$ and $\\text{gcd}(15,32)=1,$ which implies $15\\,|\\,b.$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, let $b=7.$ Then, ${7}^{2}∣7\\cdot 7$ but ${7}^{2}∤7.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}({3}^{2},25)=1.$ Therefore, we have ${3}^{2}∣25b$ and $\\text{gcd}({3}^{2},25)=1,$ which implies ${3}^{2}\\,|\\,b.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186204623 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186203900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d05194c-249d-54fb-91f1-f614c0f5e584" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordered Pairs Belonging to a Non-Standard Relation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24ec285a-89b6-5e80-85cf-547592c0be93" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186411627} {:answer/id #uuid "29192154-5fca-5c4c-ba05-c1677c12f786" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186411628} {:answer/id #uuid "4a6224af-2752-5783-be20-5ad5862f62d9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186411637} {:answer/id #uuid "516f8b78-7d59-5ec9-ba7c-04b73f06ad86" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186411639} {:answer/id #uuid "85e2f0d3-5246-5ae6-9a99-5800a2b254f6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186411648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24ec285a-89b6-5e80-85cf-547592c0be93" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186411627} :answer-field/id #uuid "856a64a3-53df-597c-9cc5-75ee45f124c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186411674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e6ad6a4-a09f-5fba-92c0-ff271a2f98be" :question/math-academy-id "q-80860" :question/problem "Given the relation $R$ on $Z$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x∣(y-1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $4\\,R\\,13$\n\nII. $3\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,11$\n\nIII. $8\\,R\\,3$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∣(y-1).$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. In this case, we have $4∣(13-1),$ which is true.\n\n- Statement II is true. In this case, we have $3∤(11-1),$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, we have $8∣(3-1),$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186229633 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229158 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bdd2ceb-c14a-5a3d-bf03-692a1475fa41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02c467ea-d283-5e56-8a87-1b18aa02c984" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/id 17592186302472} {:answer/id #uuid "45ccedb0-e55b-599b-8637-db4470585ba1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id 17592186302898} {:answer/id #uuid "7cdec9ab-46e6-5596-8ec2-44c59b6c6b63" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id 17592186303232} {:answer/id #uuid "804dc170-f17c-5982-87c0-36daec01e968" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186303259} {:answer/id #uuid "dc1c1b2b-78f8-5fab-8359-ff09e51e76aa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186303829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "804dc170-f17c-5982-87c0-36daec01e968" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186303259} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c636557-3959-5f68-a368-7e6b6fa87d4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186304240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97216a46-237d-5c6e-afbc-0108a663f4c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-332530" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =5,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\n- The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}=(1-5Δx)^{n}{y}_{n}.$\n- The method is stable for $|1-5Δx|<1.$\n- The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$\n- The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.25.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-5{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-5{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-5Δx){y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-5Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-5Δx)((1-5Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & =(1-5Δx)^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & =(1-5Δx)^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =(1-5Δx)^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-5Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-5Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-5Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-5Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <5Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.25$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186234078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092329 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19a03588-a6f2-5251-be03-4bdaa4eb280e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186327155} {:answer/id #uuid "423f75d6-0783-5897-97b0-99de5db0c8d0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186327515} {:answer/id #uuid "759de309-55be-5420-9616-13433e8c09ee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186327943} {:answer/id #uuid "a248b3a7-3413-5a74-a48b-1e99f9ab94c5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186328339} {:answer/id #uuid "e38e145c-3810-5bad-b273-78afcc7cdd6c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186328928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e38e145c-3810-5bad-b273-78afcc7cdd6c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186328928} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9e8f46b-385c-5762-a3ae-f76405c171c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186329480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c6cc47f7-3bf2-54bf-a0ea-9009aac01bb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-47447" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $7,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P+Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$\n- $P-Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $4$\n- $P\\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, suppose $P(x)={x}^{3}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{7}.$ Then $P+Q={x}^{3}+{x}^{7},$ which is not a polynomial of degree $10.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose $P(x)={x}^{3}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{7}.$ Then $P-Q={x}^{3}-{x}^{7},$ which is not a polynomial of degree $4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}$ and ${x}^{7},$ respectively. Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P\\cdot Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{7}={x}^{3+7}={x}^{10},$ meaning that $P\\cdot Q$ has degree $10.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186237867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a423260-bbbe-5ae5-9f27-db48fce5a7d4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186236382} {:answer/id #uuid "5b27cad1-42d8-5307-8cc2-5cc48adae5ed" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186236384} {:answer/id #uuid "9dce0a55-5641-528c-9f93-21ec3cd96888" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186236804} {:answer/id #uuid "fa19be47-2169-5673-84c3-412cf1b7a226" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186237400} {:answer/id #uuid "feda186f-19bf-5fb8-9907-6309e3735091" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186237439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b27cad1-42d8-5307-8cc2-5cc48adae5ed" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186236384} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8ff4376-b626-5cd5-b078-07242fef4809" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186237738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0efb43bb-72c2-5796-b21b-537b0708d8ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-101995" :question/problem "Given that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$, has a removable discontinuity at $x=1$, and is differentiable at $x=-1$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\nI. It is differentiable at $x=0$\n\nII. It  is **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n\nIII. It is continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false. Continuity does not imply differentiability. For example, the function $y=|x|$ is continuous but it's not differentiable at $x=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true.  A function that is discontinuous at a point can't be differentiable at this point (because differentiability implies continuity).\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, differentiability implies continuity."}] [{:db/id 17592186237868 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91e0fa99-107e-554a-bca7-bad853c6cd4c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186236742} {:answer/id #uuid "9243ba2b-d92c-523d-8153-b97e690974ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186236746} {:answer/id #uuid "c051bc6b-f385-5b06-a71c-62218d812193" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186237041} {:answer/id #uuid "ca847587-1091-5d6b-8cf0-c5d23b115e59" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186237103} {:answer/id #uuid "ecfb2f83-347a-5d8a-8e26-10289635d4e8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186237310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecfb2f83-347a-5d8a-8e26-10289635d4e8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186237310} :answer-field/id #uuid "53dbf63f-8b12-5a21-b442-56f7cd97d4ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186237548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5280401d-3d75-5547-be89-ad4f6f6c9526" :question/math-academy-id "q-101997" :question/problem "Given that a function $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-5$, is differentiable at $x=-2$, and has a  vertical tangent at $x=0$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=-5$\n- It  is continuous at $x=-2$\n- It is differentiable at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "Differentiability implies continuity. Hence,\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-5$, it can't be differentiable there either.\n\n- Statement II is true.   Since $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=-2$ it is also  continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since we have a vertical tangent at $x=0$, the function $f(x)$ is not differentiable at this point."}] [{:db/id 17592186237873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115381 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Functions are Differentiable at a Given Point From the Function Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14bcc2d5-8ff0-5de6-b9b7-8c70c668b43e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186235942} {:answer/id #uuid "1af6eee1-8b75-5cbd-abfe-28d48340133b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186235983} {:answer/id #uuid "5b8299b8-14e4-5661-916f-3e7a91907a97" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186236388} {:answer/id #uuid "758d1cc1-c63d-566d-9f78-5af3c023e6cb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186236554} {:answer/id #uuid "82c9af49-99d8-5695-9534-6ef4ffbf18bc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186236634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1af6eee1-8b75-5cbd-abfe-28d48340133b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186235983} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8a8469b-04e3-5429-9f82-f67f5e5c1400" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186237676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6fe09a9-5d63-559e-a71c-67bb17b36b63" :question/math-academy-id "q-102163" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x-2, & x<0 \\\\ -2, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\n- It is continuous at $x=0$\n- It is differentiable at $x=0$\n- It is differentiable on the interval $(0,2)$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is true  Indeed, let's investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, at  $x=0.$ At this point, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(2x-2)=2(0)-2=-2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}-2=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=f(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$ .\n\n- Statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0$ because it has a corner point there: \n\n$\\,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=2\\,$  if  $\\,x<0$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0\\,$ if $\\,x>0$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $f(x)$ is differentiable everywhere except at $x=0.$   Since $x=0$  doesn't belong to $(0,2)$, the given function is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186238041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186176051 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e2a8cf2-64db-5b2f-b0f7-0c727cf492a9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37bcf381-7f8a-5baa-9290-980479025998" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186236172} {:answer/id #uuid "607dfedf-36ef-54be-84e1-a22d3370bf39" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186236432} {:answer/id #uuid "6b296e58-25bf-5073-a97d-db4dc955015a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186236492} {:answer/id #uuid "7bfca28b-3c9f-56f3-b358-5f4c8bd08815" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186236592} {:answer/id #uuid "ae5c2399-4da8-5d37-a130-7c781b78ea87" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186236926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37bcf381-7f8a-5baa-9290-980479025998" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186236172} :answer-field/id #uuid "19c031a1-a523-51cd-984f-8f8228525268" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186237470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50bb01da-0246-5da5-b527-fc7a59519d36" :question/math-academy-id "q-103685" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=sec⁡x?$\n\n- The range of the function is $y=(-\\infty ,-1]$\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is an output of the function\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is not a zero of the function" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d0/d0f3e760be991c936baf5e9825cd582f086a73a8330032d24fd35fd5594b4864.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).$ Therefore, $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not an output of the function.\n\n- Statement III is true since the function has no zeros."}] [{:db/id 17592186238063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186176055 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a18aadb-3e8a-54d7-a5d7-e78208e77675" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "219b49f6-4024-5608-b300-3db553fd48e3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186236033} {:answer/id #uuid "5373b6dd-8882-5b2f-91b1-c818e364404a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186236346} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b29b66-322b-5608-93ec-631968a3e9c8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186237175} {:answer/id #uuid "d7aeade8-d178-56b1-8a96-ed6d56fccb5f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186237182} {:answer/id #uuid "e7e40cd5-c7e0-586a-ae46-5c9379c1dfb6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186237276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5373b6dd-8882-5b2f-91b1-c818e364404a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186236346} :answer-field/id #uuid "b12e6f2a-4a8a-5fc1-91fb-89ef78acdfcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186237666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e25815e7-4d17-5965-b1f7-fc15c12bd9d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-103836" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x?$ Assume $n$ is an integer.\n\nI. The function has vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pi +\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.$\n\nII. The domain of the function is $[-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nIII. The period of the function is $\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=cot⁡x.$\n\n![](images/77/7785ad73f6e5212772c3053780ffd055f37cfb39927b6ddc3e40cd3a55b8ba4e.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The vertical asymptotes of the function are integer multiples of $\\pi $, i.e., $x=n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is false. The domain of the function is all real numbers except the values where the asymptotes occur, i.e., $x=n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement III is true. The period of $y=cot⁡x$ is $\\pi .$"}] [{:db/id 17592186240863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186184224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25229703-4cfb-5010-ae5c-f6a54503ced3" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity of Three-Piece Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18317632-9a46-5b18-bafd-820209526524" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186238261} {:answer/id #uuid "2b206fef-99a4-534a-b2b0-b6ae959fef99" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186238436} {:answer/id #uuid "6e34f467-9d96-5d96-adbd-58a85537abb3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186239005} {:answer/id #uuid "74c42b8b-18d3-5fba-a1e7-4a175cd7070f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186239062} {:answer/id #uuid "75d8569c-8b78-5fc7-977e-d7799bd0a0bc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186239073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75d8569c-8b78-5fc7-977e-d7799bd0a0bc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186239073} :answer-field/id #uuid "21361fe3-3c71-57a8-ba47-cf7a876c9edd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "23c6c497-7b53-5892-b312-12905adbf824" :question/math-academy-id "q-104384" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3},\\,\\, & x<2 \\\\ 8,\\,\\, & x=2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2},\\, & x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $f(x)$ at $x=2.$\n\nFirst, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=2$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={2}^{3} \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & =f(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=2$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =2(2)^{2} \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & =f(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=2,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186240897 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129086 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9110c50a-62db-5447-b4d0-60819c80eb31" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties Related to Point Discontinuities Given a Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c362354-b121-53da-b1d0-32db9976b524" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186238164} {:answer/id #uuid "8d031b0b-6f60-5c10-a903-198316653435" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186239280} {:answer/id #uuid "a1745f6f-463b-5bbd-8815-814b9176348f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186239460} {:answer/id #uuid "c6da6cc7-debd-54ae-a437-073b08d05ea7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186239806} {:answer/id #uuid "e002f9b5-f859-53ed-bd2b-7e968ac2c54e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186240014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c362354-b121-53da-b1d0-32db9976b524" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186238164} :answer-field/id #uuid "2228e72c-dac2-5e7f-aab5-a7eee153aa98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b8ec7f0-866e-5db4-b2de-0c9d1fe0bd7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-104572" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+11}, & x<5 \\\\ 12, & x=5 \\\\ {e}^{5/x}, & x>5\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements is true?\n\n- $f(5)$ exists\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}f(x)$\n- $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=5$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(5)=12$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true.\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}\\sqrt{x+11}=4, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}{e}^{5/x}=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that statement II is false and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}f(x).\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSince the second condition failed, $x=5$ is not a removable discontinuity. So, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186240969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28bee080-f834-5a65-875e-64ff7347b686" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186238415} {:answer/id #uuid "5996a243-56ab-51f3-ad76-62c2e3bde6cb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186238824} {:answer/id #uuid "7e0ffc73-d2c1-5009-b10d-4151c9d3cd50" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186239152} {:answer/id #uuid "9a7e7d63-45c4-50a5-aaa2-0d3597c02017" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186239400} {:answer/id #uuid "9b6fe7c0-652f-5c4b-aba5-09bac5d9f309" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186239410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b6fe7c0-652f-5c4b-aba5-09bac5d9f309" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186239410} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac3ebdbc-2590-5082-b160-ffed9036c31a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bcc06ba1-1065-5981-a107-14dc46cea80e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104984" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(2{x}^{2}-1) & 2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\ 2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} & {e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(2{y}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(0,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(0,0)>0$\n- $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)>0$\n- The point $(0,0)$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=(-1)^{2}-{0}^{2}=1>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=-1<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(0,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)<0,$ the point $(0,0)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186240970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2384084c-0ad8-5474-a426-2c2d4b61f394" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186238367} {:answer/id #uuid "63e0a616-9b9c-5f18-b88e-79e60e3cf07f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186238930} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd744ae-5143-52bc-be05-7bd8710afef4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186239417} {:answer/id #uuid "c346f462-1b1e-504d-9e90-c52eccd0c1c1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186239777} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e2cde0-867a-5cd6-807e-b33239302266" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186240182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63e0a616-9b9c-5f18-b88e-79e60e3cf07f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186238930} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e6c05c4-cb31-5b46-a21a-67880b6b5964" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e273e9fd-fb16-5b0e-b15b-fcbb70918d78" :question/math-academy-id "q-104995" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3y & 3{y}^{2}-3x\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(1,1)>0$\n- $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)<0$\n- The point $(1,1)$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3y & 3{y}^{2}-3x\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}-3y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}-3x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-3y)=6x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-3x)=6y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-3x)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6x & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))={6}^{2}-(-3)^{2}=27>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=6>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(1,1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)>0,$ the point $(1,1)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186240975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21f26a3f-f8aa-51d7-83bc-84d4d1f3e29c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id 17592186238350} {:answer/id #uuid "9aec57c3-1ff8-5ede-b25f-4126b769414d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186239404} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b955c8-064e-5c1a-9233-310b065bfe30" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id 17592186239754} {:answer/id #uuid "ce693687-08cd-5026-93b5-f84a347f03c7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186239863} {:answer/id #uuid "e60eec68-d021-5965-822a-8deec198de79" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id 17592186240076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21f26a3f-f8aa-51d7-83bc-84d4d1f3e29c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id 17592186238350} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d236ad3-8465-5855-af2b-83ac755c82b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0ffa6ad-f9fd-59d8-a6a4-bd61246d63d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-105064" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={n\\in N∣n-2\\ge 4}$ and $B={n\\in Z∣n+2\\ge 5}?$\n\n- $A⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $∅⊆A$\n- $B⊇A$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $n-2\\ge 4,$ that is to say, $n\\ge 6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA={6,7,8,9,10,11,…}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n+2\\ge 5,$ That is to say, $n\\ge 3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB={3,4,5,6,7,8,…}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is true. Every element in the set $A$ is an element of the set $B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is false. For example, $3\\in B$ but $3\\notin A.$\n\n- $∅⊆A$ is true. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\n- $B⊇A$ is equivalent to $A⊆B,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186240976 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4050aada-7658-541f-be9b-3f3781195813" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id 17592186238610} {:answer/id #uuid "645d28d3-fe8f-52fc-842b-4f5ec36cb5fa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186238935} {:answer/id #uuid "79bab5cd-3fc0-5a56-a4aa-07edf3fa9de1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186239104} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b76ccb-502a-51ef-89b3-83e8999e2f40" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id 17592186239787} {:answer/id #uuid "cc9bae77-3654-5d37-a473-0b619ae82373" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186239845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4050aada-7658-541f-be9b-3f3781195813" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id 17592186238610} :answer-field/id #uuid "52ecb4b5-78e4-53b4-9866-6cccc44f6d8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e6954872-228a-577f-9a57-37ed3b0eaeb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-105065" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in Z∣x\\le 5}$ and $B={x\\in Z∣-7\\le x<3}?$\n\n- $A⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $B⊈A$\n- $A⊇B$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the integer numbers that satisfy the inequality $x\\le 5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA={…,-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $-7\\le x<3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB={-7,-6,-5,-4,-3,-2,-1,0,1,2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false.  For example, $5\\in A$ but $5\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true, while $B⊈A$ is false. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $A⊇B$ is equivalent to $B⊆A,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186241031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f6b3754-c8d5-5b37-8d18-ef83f5e9749c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186238185} {:answer/id #uuid "27e14937-1c82-559e-a37e-1b482fa437f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186238401} {:answer/id #uuid "366bc6b5-4bab-5ba8-8018-c4bc7223b115" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186238525} {:answer/id #uuid "69582467-05df-57a2-b597-530abbc3877d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186238970} {:answer/id #uuid "93d981b6-c7f1-52e4-a22e-60dd625357db" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186239333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27e14937-1c82-559e-a37e-1b482fa437f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186238401} :answer-field/id #uuid "b17fe9cc-10a6-5ca0-8dc9-5ed726aaaab2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c05cb83d-c779-50b1-a772-7d4551eea239" :question/math-academy-id "q-105308" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}+8{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}-16{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 10 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -12 & -16 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -12 & -16 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements I and III are false, while statement II is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186241036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d87b39f-38fd-5072-8df9-199dc3e0354b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186239142} {:answer/id #uuid "9363b93b-17ff-5622-aad6-e074a8b8cff9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186239327} {:answer/id #uuid "9a31168b-c0d9-57f7-abbb-5a6d79071e4b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186239395} {:answer/id #uuid "de1e8102-fb34-585a-b310-16891b8d4acb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186239996} {:answer/id #uuid "e66fc050-4e7a-5846-932f-1446097346fb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186240079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de1e8102-fb34-585a-b310-16891b8d4acb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186239996} :answer-field/id #uuid "2020bb5b-ccfc-53a2-812c-aa951625fd94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e39d7a95-6796-5061-a2aa-706f3f881251" :question/math-academy-id "q-105315" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nIII. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}-12{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -5 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+3(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3{x}_{1}+2(0)+5(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are true, while statement I is false."}] [{:db/id 17592186241038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49f4c6f1-939a-5b88-9bf3-a8adf2ea2c3a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186238698} {:answer/id #uuid "572c908c-7588-5745-b1fb-06f0deaa26f8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186238807} {:answer/id #uuid "9ef98273-4408-5085-9187-33c5ddd46ea3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186239443} {:answer/id #uuid "c13e9a64-4df3-5744-803e-cb874d004f59" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186239746} {:answer/id #uuid "fa77997b-23f4-5dcb-b0af-e29180f4d714" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186240269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ef98273-4408-5085-9187-33c5ddd46ea3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186239443} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6f9d469-87fe-56b1-97f4-7fe1b4596a1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "81d16fc8-2819-579f-80e6-748f22f14098" :question/math-academy-id "q-105319" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -15 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 8{x}_{1}-15{x}_{2}+20{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}+12{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -15 & 20 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 12 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 12 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+4(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-2{x}_{1}+4(0)-4(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements I and II are true, while statement III is false."}] [{:db/id 17592186241040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48ea8503-592e-5da9-bf94-dbf1c1894619" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186238691} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3cb585-f127-5930-9e3b-7bcca744902f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186238718} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b5c69f-96d8-52e0-a849-ceb99bf9c83b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186239510} {:answer/id #uuid "d8eef8cf-fdfa-56b9-be1c-3b3a679c443b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186239949} {:answer/id #uuid "e723e2bb-2b91-51b6-b191-0ff5b0c1e575" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186240089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7b5c69f-96d8-52e0-a849-ceb99bf9c83b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186239510} :answer-field/id #uuid "01b2343f-c078-5f49-8bf2-efe62f09b44b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c2c7d13-747e-5ac0-bf88-b6f7f16d61aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-105334" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>4\\},\\,∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ belongs to $A$\n\nII. The point $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ is a boundary point of $A$\n\nIII. The point $Q(2,2)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie outside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/a1/a1371f9abdeae5083171f5ddfa5d3e237b7e223ba2ba5cf8a822322c34a4676c.png)\n\n \n\nLet's add the points $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ and $Q(2,2)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/02/021ebd260ca399ee47bf8268d02a1a05019d63eef8ab2f5facc56f8576195a80.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ do not satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>4.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $Q$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is fully contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'."}] [{:db/id 17592186241042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "438dc9b9-4b4c-5c7c-8f1f-ef06b777ccee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186238640} {:answer/id #uuid "863a21f0-1bc2-56d2-9c36-a6fabc7dd44a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186239223} {:answer/id #uuid "990a09cd-f697-5ab6-845b-769bca59df37" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186239385} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf99d1d-2ca4-5a96-93f3-6bba5cba0073" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186239849} {:answer/id #uuid "dafd1bdc-8b8a-5ac7-a122-fcacd9a19c9b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186239965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dafd1bdc-8b8a-5ac7-a122-fcacd9a19c9b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186239965} :answer-field/id #uuid "954844d7-9a40-5243-995c-752e01be8ec1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26f835f8-28cf-54e5-acfe-863ecc606ff1" :question/math-academy-id "q-105357" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 9},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(3,0)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(3,0)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(0,2)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or outside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/b9/b9b92085d112ab87592aa8c4d6a56aa4a61ea859d560bac316ce8c97a0af20e5.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(3,0)$ and $Q(0,2)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/27/27806c9bb67b60d52cfbae8e881d307d71a3a5fb9465f36d978035f74d78f4bc.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(3,0)$ satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(3,0)$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 9.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $Q$ is *not* an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only'."}] [{:db/id 17592186241043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "220d3b6e-16be-5d03-8218-f5badf84607c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186238352} {:answer/id #uuid "38f9139a-fc27-571a-b253-0ced64754412" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186238547} {:answer/id #uuid "8ea25008-a389-52eb-b71d-5d28d07da799" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186239294} {:answer/id #uuid "953f2088-4959-5da7-870c-162bf4221448" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186239347} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9414f6-0aee-551b-8d5f-6308c06b3b35" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186240279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38f9139a-fc27-571a-b253-0ced64754412" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186238547} :answer-field/id #uuid "e850dda3-900d-5f07-872b-367021ae60ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f57db6a6-9edb-5f0f-98dd-62f31492deff" :question/math-academy-id "q-105365" :question/problem "The set $B$ and its boundary $∂B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}<12},\\,∂B={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=12}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(3,4)$ lies on the boundary of $B$\n- The point $P(3,4)$ belongs to $B$\n- The point $Q(-3,6)$ is an interior point of $B$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $B$ consists of all points that lie inside the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=12.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/ad/adee860d39b4d9c4511db2b2f1ad6e7f46342cc474da47303af742be1cc911b3.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(3,4)$ and $Q(-3,6)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/21/2185e7129cd799eb1cbb888789566a406b1594dd5a649b9129bec491a1dce1c7.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(3,4)$ does not satisfy the equation $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=12.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie on the boundary of $B.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(3,4)$ satisfy the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}<12.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $B.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $B$ and points that do not belong to $B.$ The point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'."}] [{:db/id 17592186243917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e40bb52-d447-5b63-8524-b2a531405e15" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186241441} {:answer/id #uuid "9291f5e0-661c-5b67-b5f9-98411143c89c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186242466} {:answer/id #uuid "b5bc1ea3-bf67-5bbc-afec-5c54acd87a0d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186242768} {:answer/id #uuid "d10a3eb1-dd7e-54ff-a306-219702a84f3d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186243022} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0d8c7b-86b5-51b3-a866-ce768aef7b8a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186243302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9291f5e0-661c-5b67-b5f9-98411143c89c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186242466} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd08eacf-dccc-58c1-bd33-7fea6c93187f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0880be83-dbd5-5dc8-a3cc-bdb045c80795" :question/math-academy-id "q-106684" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices with real entries and determinant $0.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- The  zero-matrix $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $L.$\n- For any $A\\in L,$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A\\in L$ such that $A+(-A)=O.$\n- For any $A,B,C\\in L,$ we have $(A+B)-C=A+(B-C).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|=0.$ Hence, $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A\\in L,$ there is a corresponding negative matrix $-A$ such that\n\n$$\nA+(-A)=A-A=O.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n|-A|=|-1\\cdot A|=(-1)^{2}\\cdot |A|=1\\cdot 0=0,\n$$\n\nhence $-A\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any matrices $A,B\\in L,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n(A+B)-C=(A+B)+(-C)=A+(B+(-C))=A+(B-C).\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186243920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50e801e2-503a-559b-936f-f3458cb4c980" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186241902} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0376d8-916a-5c20-b820-b920ea81d4ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186242150} {:answer/id #uuid "c66a9c3d-3226-5038-8929-f96ac925e96f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186242932} {:answer/id #uuid "e35537bf-65b9-5ffa-a98b-f238d2931250" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186243206} {:answer/id #uuid "f976c12a-1c69-5418-8d74-80ce87edd353" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186243376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c66a9c3d-3226-5038-8929-f96ac925e96f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186242932} :answer-field/id #uuid "a62f5fb8-4988-5219-9bbb-89bef4000d32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2555255-85b1-529f-85fb-94b6f9684401" :question/math-academy-id "q-106688" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices with real entries and determinant $1.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. The identity matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $S.$\n\nII. For any $A\\in S,$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A\\in S$ such that $A+(-A)=O.$\n\nIII. There are matrices $A,B,C\\in S,$ such that $(A+B)+C\\ne A+(B+C).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=1.$ Hence, $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in S.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A\\in S,$ there is a corresponding negative $2\\times 2$ matrix $-A$ such that\n\n$$\nA+(-A)=A-A=O.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n|-A|=|-1\\cdot A|=(-1)^{2}\\cdot |A|=1\\cdot 1=1,\n$$\n\nhence $-A\\in S.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For any matrices $A,B,C\\in S,$ we have\n\n$$\n(A+B)+C=A+(B+C).\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186243926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "487a4843-7c15-5ab1-8920-ea735dfdde90" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186241837} {:answer/id #uuid "5b7f17af-f142-53cc-8323-3a6aab476f8e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186242005} {:answer/id #uuid "70a81903-8370-5b92-9e82-aeee09fbedcf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186242186} {:answer/id #uuid "8832c7a4-ead6-55c1-ba06-ea22126f602e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186242375} {:answer/id #uuid "8aa223a8-d4f3-51ef-a918-0edfc6559919" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186242402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b7f17af-f142-53cc-8323-3a6aab476f8e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186242005} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c6e7b59-87d4-567b-a3cf-d17339b0c4a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f184570-b064-50c3-a82a-5d76cef8f98e" :question/math-academy-id "q-106700" :question/problem "Let $M$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & 1 \\\\ b & c\\end{aligned}],$ where $a,b,c\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- For any $A\\in M,$ we have $-A\\in M$ such that $A+(-A)=0.$\n- For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A\\in M,$ we have $A+O=A.$\n- For any $A,B,C\\in M,$ we have $(A+B)+C=A+(B+C).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\in M.$ Then $-A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the corresponding negative matrix such that $A+(-A)=O.$ However, $-{a}_{12}=-1\\ne 1,$ hence, $-A\\notin M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the zero-matrix $O$ and any matrix $A\\in M,$ we have\n\n$$\nA+O=A\n$$\n\nsince $O$ is the unique zero element in the set of all matrices.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, For any matrices $A,B,C\\in M,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n(A+B)+C=A+(B+C)\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186243929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d5ab6fa-7f55-5ca4-b0fd-47c66f401c73" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186241426} {:answer/id #uuid "68cb4930-5346-5250-b195-1320dc59351d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186242121} {:answer/id #uuid "7e3cfbd3-7c51-51e1-a1cc-651adea01a28" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186242288} {:answer/id #uuid "a3041743-ae01-5749-b3bd-e63042670543" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186242605} {:answer/id #uuid "af66c2ef-de66-52df-845a-a3736459f400" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186242709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d5ab6fa-7f55-5ca4-b0fd-47c66f401c73" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186241426} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b0d854e-3563-5ab5-a40f-3c0263e3318d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c35f03a5-0abf-5f24-a015-92c06eed08bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-106703" :question/problem "Let $T$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients and whose degree is at most $10.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- For the zero-polynomial $0$ and any $p(x)\\in T,$ we have $p(x)+0=0.$\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in T$, we have $p(x)+q(x)=p(x)-q(x).$\n- For any polynomial $p(x)\\in T$ we have $-p(x)\\in T$ such that $p(x)+(-p(x))=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}$ in $T.$ Then,\n\n$$\np(x)+0={x}^{2}+0={x}^{2}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the polynomials $p(x)=q(x)={x}^{2}\\in T.$\nThen:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x)+q(x) & ={x}^{2}+{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2} \\\\ p(x)-q(x) & ={x}^{2}-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $p(x)+q(x)\\ne p(x)-q(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any polynomial $p(x)\\in T,$ there is a corresponding negative polynomial $-p(x)$ such that\n\n$$\np(x)+(-p(x))=p(x)-p(x)=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}⁡(-p(x))=\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p(x))\\le 10,\n$$\n\nhence $-p(x)\\in T.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186243932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7041d5fd-aab1-57e4-9487-b5c9882af600" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186242180} {:answer/id #uuid "72850430-5595-545e-a2f2-2771edf7ffa6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186242195} {:answer/id #uuid "83fc1536-7b10-52d0-956e-7849f2977d11" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186242335} {:answer/id #uuid "bbe59f91-6410-5a6d-8e3e-2fcdb03f1f4f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186242826} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa01516-17a5-5f65-a695-9c94fa04b033" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186243258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83fc1536-7b10-52d0-956e-7849f2977d11" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186242335} :answer-field/id #uuid "4127109b-95ff-5b82-9a34-cadc07c4058a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c34661b-de46-5e42-88de-7fe1ed369bf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-106710" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the set of all polynomials $p(x)$ such that $p(0)<0.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The zero-polynomial belongs to $C.$\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x).$\n- The addition of polynomials in $C$ is associative." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $C$ contains only polynomials that evaluate to a negative number at $0.$ However, the zero-polynomial is *not* such a polynomial, as it equals $0≮0$ at $0.$ So, $0\\notin C.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\np(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x)\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in C,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)=p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)]\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186243955 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e469b5-07c6-584b-b3bd-1a166c6c0cd9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186241327} {:answer/id #uuid "36663d6f-568a-5128-b12e-c1c00efc0541" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186241659} {:answer/id #uuid "4c3d6cce-e8b2-553a-80b1-192aefaf4bec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186241862} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd26119-c084-57ff-a50a-31cfe1d896f3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186241876} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d83713-39f9-5e61-ab77-6113890a9500" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186242879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10e469b5-07c6-584b-b3bd-1a166c6c0cd9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186241327} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8abf082-e05d-5b29-89ee-0c3cab9c4c33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d83ac51c-0c75-5956-b55e-b6c1972a1661" :question/math-academy-id "q-106809" :question/problem "Let $N$ be the set of all functions of the form $f(x)=a|x|,$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- For any $f(x)\\in N,$ we have $(-1)\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n- For any $f(x)\\in N$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).$\n- For any $f(x),g(x)\\in N$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, consider $f(x)=|x|\\in N.$ Then,\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot f(x)=(-1)\\cdot |x|=-|x|\\ne |x|.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1)\\cdot f(x)\\ne f(x).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any function $f(x)\\in N$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any functions $f(x),g(x)\\in N$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the results of $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186243956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aea2174-dfeb-5a96-8044-c2efb4e92930" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186241278} {:answer/id #uuid "1f09035c-a73f-5bc0-b1e9-62489ebb423b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186241452} {:answer/id #uuid "86070dcd-5d3e-5df7-96bd-50a77e905437" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186242352} {:answer/id #uuid "d059ad96-5ba2-538b-be61-12470c585a77" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186243016} {:answer/id #uuid "fc5f3220-69a4-5a66-9699-c599ac5f66f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186243403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d059ad96-5ba2-538b-be61-12470c585a77" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186243016} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8d3769e-1942-5ecd-9ab4-4be4b985cc68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "253cf041-a7dc-53ad-a439-778e84d95095" :question/math-academy-id "q-106810" :question/problem "Let $J$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients and whose degree is at most $2.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\frac{1}{x}\\in J$\n\nII. For any $p(x)\\in J,$ we have $1\\cdot p(x)\\in J$\n\nIII. For any $p(x),q(x)\\in J$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $J$ contains only polynomials whose degree is at most $2.$ However, $\\frac{1}{x}$ is *not* a polynomial. So, $\\frac{1}{x}\\notin J.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in J$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot p(x)=p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x),q(x)\\in J,$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the results of $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186243957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03039dc6-1dfb-5fb8-9cd9-a75ee2ded12a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186241207} {:answer/id #uuid "054b883f-bba5-5e0c-a4b6-5ae661f7f6b6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186241224} {:answer/id #uuid "1082defe-5e72-57fe-9385-7ab66130f25b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186241322} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9bf2a8-2d06-5f33-a19e-4f65b98a806b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186242967} {:answer/id #uuid "e71f2082-95c7-5af8-a69a-50b89c1d1e5c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186243229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e71f2082-95c7-5af8-a69a-50b89c1d1e5c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186243229} :answer-field/id #uuid "54287edb-3a24-5a97-817d-0ce7b6fd42c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43cc36f0-0278-53e6-b346-3f4ef69a0aaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-106812" :question/problem "Let $G$ be the set of functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)>0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- For any $f(x)\\in G,$ we have $1\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n- For any $f(x)\\in G$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha -\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)-\\beta f(x).$\n- The scalar multiplication of functions in $G$ is associative." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in G$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n(1\\cdot f)(x)=1\\cdot f(x)=f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in G$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha -\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)-\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in G$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta f(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )f(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186244007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124485 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21e27a77-17e4-5b05-b8b5-42ddb50a3663" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186241473} {:answer/id #uuid "338251e4-d90f-50e0-b7e1-23d7e28826c6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186241634} {:answer/id #uuid "467f0cad-0f65-5c2a-a7dd-e55f70fc3b18" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186241817} {:answer/id #uuid "5c9a7045-01d4-5909-9fff-f5c6752fec27" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186242016} {:answer/id #uuid "f4501265-2c93-55db-a331-1380629939f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186243341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c9a7045-01d4-5909-9fff-f5c6752fec27" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186242016} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff9c9993-a272-54fc-b7cd-ff51bde6faba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d584308b-6723-5e86-a9ec-6146a097d3d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-107042" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly dependent and ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ has a non-zero solution\n- ${v}_{4}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$\n- ${v}_{3}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly dependent set, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nhas a non-zero solution.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, solving equation ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ for ${v}_{4},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{v}_{4}=-\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{4}}{v}_{2}-\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{4}}{v}_{3}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, ${x}_{4}$ can't be zero because then\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nwill have a non-zero solution, which contradicts the fact that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\nHence, ${v}_{4}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent set, the equation \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nhas no non-zero solution. Hence, ${v}_{3}$ is not a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186244008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124485 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cd92405-02c6-5a62-b8b5-c0adc23129d3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186242157} {:answer/id #uuid "8e744672-b586-517b-b695-2c0349dfe2d5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186242432} {:answer/id #uuid "adbfe95b-b76c-5d99-948c-1a376b43ff69" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186242698} {:answer/id #uuid "b9613222-8a5b-5a7a-b79e-e341b2f7bdab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186242805} {:answer/id #uuid "d41f0180-5fdd-5302-b5d0-55961dd132a9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186243053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cd92405-02c6-5a62-b8b5-c0adc23129d3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186242157} :answer-field/id #uuid "d55f8088-4d40-5543-b0fb-123e13d3f497" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4ec52379-6b69-5ec0-af4a-1a5b49cab942" :question/math-academy-id "q-107043" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{v}_{1}+{v}_{3},{v}_{2}+{v}_{3},2{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}({v}_{1}+{v}_{3})+{x}_{2}({v}_{2}+{v}_{3})+{x}_{3}(2{v}_{3}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}){v}_{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ which means that \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1}+{v}_{3},{v}_{2}+{v}_{3},2{v}_{3}}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${x}_{1}{v}_{2}+{x}_{2}{v}_{3}=0$ must have only the zero solution ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$  If this equation has a non-zero solution, $a\\cdot {v}_{2}+b\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n0\\cdot {v}_{1}+a\\cdot {v}_{2}+b\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot {v}_{1}+1\\cdot {v}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot ({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186244009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124485 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "222b789c-0257-5c2a-8c82-1ee5269a4d4a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186241476} {:answer/id #uuid "bc7b40d3-fcda-5ecf-9c92-3c6c218bb18b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186242831} {:answer/id #uuid "c7060f10-ae7a-5fea-8b08-3a056ded01ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186242939} {:answer/id #uuid "d9890a30-c73d-5093-b06f-472427379792" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186243116} {:answer/id #uuid "e89bd240-441e-5e1c-bf22-e77a404985a9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186243238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "222b789c-0257-5c2a-8c82-1ee5269a4d4a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186241476} :answer-field/id #uuid "372644fd-41a7-5d60-8044-2e3b5f1533d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c7243a9b-252c-5b36-9021-bf96acea16e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-107044" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{v}_{1}-{v}_{3},{v}_{2}-{v}_{3}}$ is linearly  independent\n- ${0,{v}_{1},-{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{1}}$ is linearly independent" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}({v}_{1}-{v}_{3})+{x}_{2}({v}_{2}-{v}_{3}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}-({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}){v}_{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0,$ which means that \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{3}}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot {v}_{1}+0\\cdot (-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}0+{x}_{2}{v}_{1}+{x}_{3}(-{v}_{2})=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that ${0,{v}_{1},-{v}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1\\cdot ({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})+1\\cdot ({v}_{2}-{v}_{1})=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}\\cdot ({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})+{x}_{2}\\cdot ({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that ${{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{1}}$  is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186244010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124485 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "744ac182-5331-5399-ac3f-a45616618736" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186242212} {:answer/id #uuid "a353f784-6dc7-5793-99a1-9934ad655646" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186242610} {:answer/id #uuid "c1cd0903-b1cd-531a-8dbc-6ce05e0f83e0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186242878} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb8e2e5-274d-5836-9ead-f357143e0d8c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186243299} {:answer/id #uuid "ef86df2e-b21b-58b7-9537-78f29395fa6e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186243305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a353f784-6dc7-5793-99a1-9934ad655646" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186242610} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dfb5cb8-49f4-5b8c-871f-2198fcf107a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "24ab3e85-383c-51f5-887f-f697f1697031" :question/math-academy-id "q-107045" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${2{v}_{1}+{v}_{2},2{v}_{2}-{v}_{1},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},-{v}_{1}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{v}_{1},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2{v}_{1}+{v}_{2})+{x}_{2}(2{v}_{2}-{v}_{1})+{x}_{3}{v}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}){v}_{1}+({x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}){v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ which means that \n\n$$\n{2{v}_{1}+{v}_{2},2{v}_{2}-{v}_{1},{v}_{3}}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot {v}_{1}+0\\cdot {v}_{2}+1\\cdot (-{v}_{1})=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}(-{v}_{1})=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},-{v}_{1}}$  is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{3}=0$ must have only the zero solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Indeed,  if this equation has a non-zero solution, $a\\cdot {v}_{1}+b\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\na\\cdot {v}_{1}+0\\cdot {v}_{2}+b\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186244011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124485 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0294268a-ffaa-5520-8c92-07df70d529e8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186241205} {:answer/id #uuid "31281f32-d780-5a37-a753-3c2296df564f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186241616} {:answer/id #uuid "41615e68-b2d7-5ccc-861a-5869eea99b3b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186241766} {:answer/id #uuid "84a3e361-7151-5b36-98ea-7398355861d0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186242340} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c7d678-217f-51e6-b859-c0e49ebb4f73" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186242638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84a3e361-7151-5b36-98ea-7398355861d0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186242340} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e7fd45e-b6b0-5903-83c6-bbe887fbcc07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "931e27f9-e2c3-59ee-b477-76a2f89436c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-107048" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${2{v}_{1},-3{v}_{2},4{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${-{v}_{1},-{v}_{2},-{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2{v}_{1})+{x}_{2}(-3{v}_{2})+{x}_{3}(4{v}_{3}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{x}_{1}){v}_{1}+(-3{x}_{2}){v}_{2}+(4{x}_{3}){v}_{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ which means that \n\n$$\n{2{v}_{1},-3{v}_{2},4{v}_{3}}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n(-2)\\cdot {v}_{1}+3\\cdot {v}_{2}+1\\cdot (2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}(2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2})=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, suppose\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-{v}_{1})+{x}_{2}(-{v}_{2})+{x}_{3}(-{v}_{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, implies that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-{x}_{1}){v}_{1}+(-{x}_{2}){v}_{2}+(-{x}_{3}){v}_{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,  since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, it\nmust be ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$  Therefore, the set \n${-{v}_{1},-{v}_{2},-{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186244019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124591 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b1bb974-826a-590f-8d32-cdc868ca827c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Polynomial Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a60d388-bc83-5af7-8225-3508acff2075" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186241407} {:answer/id #uuid "486f34a6-fce7-59c0-b718-04843afaa8fc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186241836} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4f8115-6911-5b89-ae5f-2e6c858659ef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id 17592186242272} {:answer/id #uuid "d742eee0-3a02-5298-9b21-8bd95e26bb86" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id 17592186243090} {:answer/id #uuid "e42fe48a-c999-5f78-b148-00f5fd4a9bbd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186243212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a60d388-bc83-5af7-8225-3508acff2075" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186241407} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a253ae0-6c8f-5b4c-b09b-a9105706149e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3056b610-3deb-5e11-aff8-96abed8c674b" :question/math-academy-id "q-107102" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{5}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $5,$ and its subset $A$ containing all polynomials of the form $a{t}^{5}+b,$ where $a,b\\in R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\nII. $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $A$ is a subspace of ${R}_{5}[t]$\n\nIV. $A$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{5}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(t)={a}_{1}{t}^{5}+{b}_{1}$ and $g(t)={a}_{2}{t}^{5}+{b}_{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =({a}_{1}{t}^{5}+{b}_{1})+({a}_{2}{t}^{5}+{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2}){t}^{5}+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(t)=a{t}^{5}+b$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =k(a{t}^{5}+b) \\\\  & =(ka){t}^{5}+(kb),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true while statement IV is false. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186244021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124590 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d872b22-7cba-5036-8273-9a20efcc7e2f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186241729} {:answer/id #uuid "78c2ab8b-9c2d-551d-8848-61255645e2a9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id 17592186242245} {:answer/id #uuid "7c438634-2ca9-5de8-a418-b73be0ed3bda" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186242270} {:answer/id #uuid "eec86e1b-cd5f-56da-9600-e976237c1328" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186243300} {:answer/id #uuid "fdfa47ed-d690-5ca9-ab1b-37e40f90f69e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id 17592186243415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eec86e1b-cd5f-56da-9600-e976237c1328" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186243300} :answer-field/id #uuid "0be788ee-6d06-5deb-86a5-8c46bba98ee6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f27a4a5-4686-5577-a411-11aa97fab74a" :question/math-academy-id "q-107112" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & -a\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a\\in R\\}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $C$ is closed under matrix addition\n\nII. $C$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $C$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nIV. $C$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{a}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are two matrices from $C,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{a}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a matrix from $C.$ So, $C$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $A=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & -a\\end{aligned}]$ is an element of $C$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =k[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & -a\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}ka & 0 \\\\ 0 & -ka\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a matrix from $C.$ So, $C$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $C$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186244022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124590 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a55fd99-cc21-54f6-9f21-32f483b49ad1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id 17592186241272} {:answer/id #uuid "195ea848-9a4e-54b7-bc92-80ae80187e99" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186241399} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad960d0-30c8-5b7c-9750-0570f53aed4b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186242001} {:answer/id #uuid "ac58dc68-ab41-5333-9612-568acb1da65f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186242687} {:answer/id #uuid "d068d781-d242-5a93-af8b-2d493319de3f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id 17592186243017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ad960d0-30c8-5b7c-9750-0570f53aed4b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186242001} :answer-field/id #uuid "b08e5c99-11cc-5ab5-b8be-9ab5b73b56f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89286e95-724b-57e9-aee8-7d71e484cafb" :question/math-academy-id "q-107113" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $A={[\\begin{aligned}0 & a \\\\ b & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a,b\\in R}.$  \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $A$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $A$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & {a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1} & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & {a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2} & 0\\end{aligned}]$ are two matrices from $A,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & {a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1} & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & {a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & {a}_{1}+{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+{b}_{2} & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a matrix from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & a \\\\ b & 0\\end{aligned}]$ is an element of $A$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =k[\\begin{aligned}0 & a \\\\ b & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & ka \\\\ kb & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a matrix from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186244023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124590 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "230a6e01-18bc-58d0-8387-1d66163f631b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186241484} {:answer/id #uuid "426b663e-f591-5196-99d7-5a2d5500a45d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186241777} {:answer/id #uuid "4db4b11e-cc15-5b26-8274-c5279317ce7f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id 17592186241875} {:answer/id #uuid "504d1f79-8308-5f06-a1aa-17458982e5eb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186241897} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ef2203-b098-59db-9ad6-e52a17c8c5f6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id 17592186243223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "504d1f79-8308-5f06-a1aa-17458982e5eb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186241897} :answer-field/id #uuid "9183d31f-6b62-5ef0-8cb0-c498d066f789" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72b2f218-4463-5051-8c3b-5d02162b5905" :question/math-academy-id "q-107115" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$  and its subset $D$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices such that the sum of all its entries equals $0.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $D$ is closed under matrix addition\n\nII. $D$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $D$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nIV. $D$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Suppose ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {b}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {d}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {d}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are two matrices from $D.$  then \n\n$$\n{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & {b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2} & {d}_{1}+{d}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n and since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2})+({c}_{1}+{c}_{2})+({d}_{1}+{d}_{2}) & = \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{b}_{1}+{c}_{1}+{d}_{1})+({a}_{1}+{b}_{1}+{c}_{1}+{d}_{1}) & = \\\\ 0+0 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe can conclude that ${A}_{1}+{A}_{2}$ also belongs to $D.$ So, $D$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $A=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\\in D$ and let $\\alpha \\in R.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\alpha A=[\\begin{aligned}\\alpha a & \\alpha b \\\\ \\alpha c & \\alpha d\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n and so\nthe sum of the entries of $\\alpha A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha a+\\alpha b+\\alpha c+\\alpha d & =\\alpha (a+b+c+d) \\\\  & =\\alpha \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $D$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $D$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186244025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124590 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50a208c6-34bf-520c-81bc-1c8293d73759" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186241900} {:answer/id #uuid "60b1b0ce-241e-57de-bbd8-fd74eb2dc23c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186242049} {:answer/id #uuid "7874aacf-c573-5a68-b713-b24449852cd0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id 17592186242241} {:answer/id #uuid "c45adc05-64d4-5d99-839e-ad37dc03a079" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id 17592186242908} {:answer/id #uuid "d835a3fb-965a-563d-9127-d900d25d31ce" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186243102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50a208c6-34bf-520c-81bc-1c8293d73759" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186241900} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6c23cbe-3714-5f55-acd5-0f4a19ef1a32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3b977ec-88a0-5b97-96bf-4f197129ba2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-107119" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$  and its subset $T$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ 0 & c\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $T$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $T$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $T$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {b}_{1} \\\\ 0 & {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {b}_{1} \\\\ 0 & {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ are two matrices from $T,$  then \n\n$$\n{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & {b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ 0 & {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nis also an element of $T.$ So, $T$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, if $A=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ 0 & c\\end{aligned}]\\in T$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\alpha A=[\\begin{aligned}\\alpha a & \\alpha b \\\\ 0 & \\alpha c\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nalso belongs to $T.$ So, $T$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $T$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186244029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124592 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2588e089-4786-5346-b42d-65c2740ae2ab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Polynomial Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27a74844-13bd-593e-99ab-4f9dc151d2ad" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186241524} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c34971-a13e-5098-aa51-ca5b8bb3c244" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186242652} {:answer/id #uuid "d2dfa3ec-1ba8-51fb-b08c-5e0b7b2c95a4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186243039} {:answer/id #uuid "d91da20c-4f2b-5dc4-bca6-039d62d36a28" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186243111} {:answer/id #uuid "ff284b33-0629-565a-bd48-d911095443c0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186243426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2dfa3ec-1ba8-51fb-b08c-5e0b7b2c95a4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186243039} :answer-field/id #uuid "3eba7ddb-ab05-5220-a1f6-52a824d383b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "38344b2a-4c7e-5844-831c-94e852b34e5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-107131" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${0,1,t}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{2}[t]$\n- $2-t$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${0,1,t}$\n- ${0,1,t}$ is a basis of ${R}_{2}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1+0\\cdot t=0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n2-t=0\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot t.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186244030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c65e49f-e63b-520e-a8d8-1e64704afdf7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186241717} {:answer/id #uuid "ac59755b-369e-5e18-80e8-907aa3ebb11f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186242688} {:answer/id #uuid "b855b893-85c7-565f-8791-cc779d46a7ed" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I,II, and III" :db/id 17592186242798} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd72eee-922b-5b5e-9443-e97544e5996c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186242837} {:answer/id #uuid "f59267b4-aa62-5bfd-b574-01d1a5f291e0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186243351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac59755b-369e-5e18-80e8-907aa3ebb11f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186242688} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d81c556-c49b-59ba-8c07-d213256d2da3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "eace6f7a-724d-598c-b716-132ee8202185" :question/math-academy-id "q-107137" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{5}.$\n- An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =6.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $7v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =2.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement is true. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=5v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=5{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $5$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=\\frac{1}{5}v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{6}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(7v)=7Av=7(2v)=2(7v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(7v)=2(7v),$ and $7v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $7v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186244032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124488 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06018602-8f20-5e46-b8f1-ec60b62415c5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id 17592186241231} {:answer/id #uuid "0ef4b0bd-56c6-538d-ab62-c3ca16878682" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186241306} {:answer/id #uuid "3d70afbc-3a63-51cf-ba1c-d7579c6c413a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id 17592186241728} {:answer/id #uuid "93da9f44-fdb7-5ebb-9ee9-46862efef29e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186242477} {:answer/id #uuid "a1252964-96da-5719-b42a-5b3be0c79793" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id 17592186242590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93da9f44-fdb7-5ebb-9ee9-46862efef29e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186242477} :answer-field/id #uuid "65afd75d-9f84-5f07-a4b8-dbf9039b6bba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7a7e364-e908-54cb-a453-163231b198f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-107139" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $N$ containing all functions such that $f(0)=0.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $N$ is closed under function addition\n- $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $N$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $N$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, suppose $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are functions from $N.$ Then $f(x)=g(x)=0$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(0) & =f(0)+g(0) \\\\  & =0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $N$ is closed under function addition.\n\n- Statement II is true.  Indeed, suppose $f(x)\\in N$  and let $\\alpha \\in R.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha f)(0) & =\\alpha \\cdot f(0) \\\\  & =\\alpha \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $N$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186244034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124488 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d4e378a-86ef-5a01-91ea-9154881076fb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186241874} {:answer/id #uuid "7c705c5a-285d-53ad-8486-e12ad0a6a4f7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186242274} {:answer/id #uuid "8b8c5f9c-2d1f-5da8-bcdf-40e16a6fe181" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186242415} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a424b7-ed1b-55fd-a7a8-9c6ca5776f8c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id 17592186243062} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e210c5-19e4-5d18-b820-1f58ace4cd59" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id 17592186243324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d4e378a-86ef-5a01-91ea-9154881076fb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186241874} :answer-field/id #uuid "707f3600-9de3-5ff3-b345-2eba81f53fed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a3ebe5cf-3b69-5829-b6a6-d075f8831709" :question/math-academy-id "q-107141" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $W$ containing all polynomials of degree greater than $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $W$ is closed under function addition\n- $W$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $W$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $W$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{3}+1$ and $g(x)=-{x}^{3}+1,$ then\n\n$$\nf(x)+g(x)={x}^{3}+1+(-{x}^{3})+1=2,\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial of degree zero. So, $W$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\\cdot {x}^{3}=0.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $W$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $W$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186244035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124488 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e03976e-08a6-5648-ae7d-b8408176b5b8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186241437} {:answer/id #uuid "6448cf7a-2122-5687-a2f2-8abc1fbf0cc4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id 17592186242082} {:answer/id #uuid "8184bde3-685a-5aa4-b0dd-04ebb9ce9687" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186242314} {:answer/id #uuid "d48eb932-44cc-5a49-86db-aba41c5c0503" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186243061} {:answer/id #uuid "ec861a27-2c70-5732-bd70-ff1420c5a451" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id 17592186243270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6448cf7a-2122-5687-a2f2-8abc1fbf0cc4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id 17592186242082} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ddaf70c-8377-50f0-9c4a-933b09df9474" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "997e8053-09a0-5e0a-853a-5f7c609b31ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-107143" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $M$ containing all functions that return an integer value at $x=1.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $M$ is closed under function addition\n- $M$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $M$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $M$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, if $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are functions from $M,$ then $f(1)$  and  $g(1)$ are integers and so $(f+g)(1)=f(1)+g(1)$ must be an integer number too. So, $M$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, $f(x)=1$ is a function in $M$, but $0.5f(x)$ is not in $M$ because\n$(0.5f)(1)=0.5\\cdot f(1)=0.5$ is not an integer. So, $M$ is *not*  closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $M$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186244045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d8dad9f-813f-5ee0-8b56-4e213fbd9e9f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186241584} {:answer/id #uuid "b610900a-7758-5500-abbe-f65a94a26875" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186242772} {:answer/id #uuid "b62e3fb1-f541-583d-b7dd-fcea40cef7c3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186242775} {:answer/id #uuid "cf4ba2f8-bab2-5a0b-bac1-77232870019d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186243009} {:answer/id #uuid "d2dffa3a-5847-5b0c-8e10-435f8b6ecf88" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186243040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2dffa3a-5847-5b0c-8e10-435f8b6ecf88" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186243040} :answer-field/id #uuid "463fc030-fafe-5c38-871c-9424a7e4d0ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "580e535d-24ca-5116-ba35-64ccc5c2ef28" :question/math-academy-id "q-107314" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{7}$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =7.$\n- An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =1.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2},$ then $-v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=\\frac{1}{7}v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{7}{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $7$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=7v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $7.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(-v)=-Av=-(\\frac{1}{2}v)=\\frac{1}{2}(-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(-v)=\\frac{1}{2}(-v),$ and $-v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186244046 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124489 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10d9e9eb-bd2b-53b9-908b-3cefdc2f7abc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186241324} {:answer/id #uuid "5962710f-f671-5ebf-848b-bf1ec4b3852d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186241985} {:answer/id #uuid "d94b4f70-6a9e-5e47-a422-9232a86505e3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186243113} {:answer/id #uuid "e3e3ebbb-ecbf-5b41-9468-b137f8adf22d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186243211} {:answer/id #uuid "ec2d2b65-8257-5f91-8d5b-5a6347330d5d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186243269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec2d2b65-8257-5f91-8d5b-5a6347330d5d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186243269} :answer-field/id #uuid "162e4ac8-ff20-5c93-b906-61516bd643aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "265a681e-3e0d-553b-975a-40472b8deb57" :question/math-academy-id "q-107347" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-matrix is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]+(-3)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186244069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124489 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f733f0d-7e29-56f9-9ebf-5a85bfa39026" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186241457} {:answer/id #uuid "7fecbffa-150f-5edd-b337-e26de5eb732a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186242302} {:answer/id #uuid "a5bfaec9-dba4-5628-a71c-4931987e5008" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186242637} {:answer/id #uuid "b61ce3ba-080b-5ff6-8f0a-29dd16a73603" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186242773} {:answer/id #uuid "c2b01a2c-55b7-59eb-882b-87a2a49d3b01" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186242888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2b01a2c-55b7-59eb-882b-87a2a49d3b01" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186242888} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc0d681c-0891-5641-b651-e58caface354" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "727e9706-00b1-50a3-b21c-2eb79073afed" :question/math-academy-id "q-107534" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{21}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & -a \\\\ 0 & 2b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186244070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124489 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bcfec00-ddba-56d0-891f-ec779c705f62" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186242009} {:answer/id #uuid "8e096644-cddd-5be1-bc7d-ddeed9e4ab6b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186242429} {:answer/id #uuid "9c826cad-2051-56d1-95c1-7fca5e3a70f7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186242547} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd5c2d7-feb7-5fc0-8f91-227b428c2cb2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186243170} {:answer/id #uuid "e9e7c716-faf0-5a7c-94b5-5525a548d2c0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186243249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfd5c2d7-feb7-5fc0-8f91-227b428c2cb2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186243170} :answer-field/id #uuid "19ce8875-c21e-50de-b4c8-8746ee90ff05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3a9f3d6-7047-5a33-b81e-ab3641786305" :question/math-academy-id "q-107536" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere some coefficients are nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]=1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186244093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca956d3e-37a8-5f0f-a062-97d0567d54e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Bases of Abstract Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2619addf-a4eb-5abe-a3f0-58ff32c18c62" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186241510} {:answer/id #uuid "394402fa-e5dc-545e-83dc-f87e15f003a2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186241692} {:answer/id #uuid "4d10664d-6704-5141-9beb-d2bf289242ff" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186241870} {:answer/id #uuid "69c85ab3-3852-52c8-a0ea-63c45aa500d0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186242130} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f71ada-1b72-5c61-8692-2c9384b9560b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186243179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2619addf-a4eb-5abe-a3f0-58ff32c18c62" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186241510} :answer-field/id #uuid "68a2a4e9-260a-589e-85e8-521a45a0ca09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d745992-cf32-533c-af78-280d0db594aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-107587" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n\nII. $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n\nIII. $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{3}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}\\}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains a vector from another vector space.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{2}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}({c}_{1}+{c}_{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}){c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}{c}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{3}\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $C$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}{c}_{3}=({x}_{1}-{x}_{3}){c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}({c}_{1}+{c}_{3})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186244094 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca956d3e-37a8-5f0f-a062-97d0567d54e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Bases of Abstract Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b86c8ce-b8e9-570a-baf5-6350efd57a37" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186241282} {:answer/id #uuid "69db45df-8924-5a9f-b27e-f9864533f21a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186242131} {:answer/id #uuid "90a38045-6eab-56a2-bb7f-87c9f2fe114b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186242445} {:answer/id #uuid "c881833e-3338-5654-b8e5-23ee253cf2b2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186242947} {:answer/id #uuid "fc84dbfc-e148-5b66-bae5-f923459ee483" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186243406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc84dbfc-e148-5b66-bae5-f923459ee483" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186243406} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2bb7e5e-91d1-5c16-88c1-922128ced4b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "013a3d9d-c3ab-5357-a425-3057635e56b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-107588" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3},x}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- ${{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n- ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},2{c}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$, any vector of ${R}^{3},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This means that ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3},x}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}({b}_{1})+{x}_{2}(2{b}_{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+(2{x}_{2}){b}_{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, ${{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}}$ spans ${R}^{2}$ since any linear combination of $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2}(2{b}_{2})\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The set ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},2{c}_{2}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the third vector is a linear combination of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This means that the set is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186244115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a08f845-4d02-5fde-ac33-5808f7af3c3b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186241551} {:answer/id #uuid "3841d5ee-d6ef-5a2b-a960-284b5da1417b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186241681} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e45e2b-9a66-58f6-8fb6-eabdad956976" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186242761} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd95721-2e08-5f1b-a6d0-ebd4461fee3f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186242981} {:answer/id #uuid "fa97473c-2351-5b78-a5f1-f1453fa45667" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186243388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4e45e2b-9a66-58f6-8fb6-eabdad956976" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186242761} :answer-field/id #uuid "f07c1c6b-78ee-5056-b7d9-0a7582d6493b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fa81e71-e4f5-5405-bfdd-41cd7387b276" :question/math-academy-id "q-108044" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$\n\nII. $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$\n\nIII. If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$ then $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-6.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (but not $-1$).\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=-3v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(3v)=3Av=3(-2v)=-2(3v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(3v)=-2(3v),$ and $3v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (but not $-6$).\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186244251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22442fa5-3ac2-5b6d-8550-6ad60d10e199" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186241477} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb021b2-1015-50c9-82f0-4e3db31fe829" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186241864} {:answer/id #uuid "664f1704-9bf7-5ed2-bdcd-e673e693ceac" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186242104} {:answer/id #uuid "67b18cc0-15fc-5a64-b685-24c74f1d28a8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186242111} {:answer/id #uuid "b4a32f11-c203-592f-95cc-5eba4cbdfb5f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186242760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "664f1704-9bf7-5ed2-bdcd-e673e693ceac" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186242104} :answer-field/id #uuid "05b988a7-f80c-506d-a67f-2ddc646b8c7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2cf2c42d-88e3-50f2-bb3a-16ab087d4389" :question/math-academy-id "q-109236" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A-I$ and $A+4I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n\nII. $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n\nIII. $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since\n\n$$\nw\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-I$ maps $w$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1,$ not $2.$\n\n- Statement II is  true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nu\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-I=A-1I$ maps $u$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $u$ is a generalzed eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+4I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ \ncorresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186244253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d2b9d26-8293-5a06-81bf-a5d8768ab1eb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186241725} {:answer/id #uuid "4e059d3f-50a9-5d66-bbba-0d70399855ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186241881} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd4d0aa-ccaf-528d-a95d-b920e6aa13a8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186242177} {:answer/id #uuid "76f397bc-53a2-5ba3-b32c-891ac2f4b5c4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186242234} {:answer/id #uuid "9de02649-0cd7-593b-a2c5-9ca8945e60f7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186242560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fd4d0aa-ccaf-528d-a95d-b920e6aa13a8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186242177} :answer-field/id #uuid "417546af-5e04-5c33-856e-84ad4da01a32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3a61bdaa-5171-5017-8ae8-0bac73d29cdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-109244" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A-6I$ and $A+7I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ of $A$\n\nII. $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A$\n\nIII. $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since\n\n$$\nw\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-6I$ maps $w$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1,$ not $3.$\n\n- Statement II is  true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nx\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+7I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalzed eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+7I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ \ncorresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186244262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38b9e8f5-7efd-5c18-bf88-188eb603a2d1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186241687} {:answer/id #uuid "8e1a1d3d-5999-505c-8006-2854a108fe38" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186242430} {:answer/id #uuid "b359f90b-b32a-5dc7-bfa5-6dd8cc3aaf21" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186242744} {:answer/id #uuid "c9bab71f-02a0-5e3f-985a-696af2ceab0c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186242957} {:answer/id #uuid "fcea623c-c12b-53a7-8b53-be6c8385daba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186243409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9bab71f-02a0-5e3f-985a-696af2ceab0c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186242957} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e66481c-5b58-5415-acac-c78fe3ae1923" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1122434b-dbdd-5a52-98a7-34990f65e51f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109273" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+8I$ and $A$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is a generalized  eigenvector of rank $1$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-8$ of $A$\n- $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-8$ of $A$\n- $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I  is false. Since\n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+8I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nu\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+8I$ maps $u$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $u$ is an generalized eigenvector of rank $3$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-8$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector), not $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186244274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14e9efe6-968d-54c9-ba7f-d8bfc0344af0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186241358} {:answer/id #uuid "20dac993-1771-57da-aea4-5a53bae88d61" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186241464} {:answer/id #uuid "971f6555-302a-5456-818a-0917a567ea60" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186242505} {:answer/id #uuid "b7db1f32-24e0-5572-949c-0c739c96c41d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186242791} {:answer/id #uuid "d20d30f0-c9d8-57d3-8218-55ec4117f2f7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186243030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20dac993-1771-57da-aea4-5a53bae88d61" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186241464} :answer-field/id #uuid "04917658-3d46-5d2e-85f2-f45f68c5a185" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65e6b2ee-a472-5db0-bf96-384d33845a1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109360" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+9I$ and $A-I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-9$ of $A$\n\nII. $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n\nIII. $z$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false.  Since \n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\n and $v\\ne 0,$ \ni.e. $A+9I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $1.$\n\n- Statement II is  false. Since \n\n$$\nx\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\n \ni.e. $A-I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3,$ not $2.$\n\n- Statement III is true.  Indeed, since \n\n$$\nz\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-1I$ maps $z$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186244291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1490f53f-36e8-579e-baaa-ee60b79acfff" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186241357} {:answer/id #uuid "44c2fcb0-27d0-5adc-acd6-22f405f3006e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186241800} {:answer/id #uuid "47ed9179-deba-536c-a345-ba506c37d4e4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186241831} {:answer/id #uuid "a625259a-b1c9-5ecb-aa3f-26c1057fe73c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186242643} {:answer/id #uuid "f34bcf8c-c075-5e5e-87f7-75a1f6c029f7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186243337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47ed9179-deba-536c-a345-ba506c37d4e4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186241831} :answer-field/id #uuid "99207296-e951-504b-aa0b-c4a74f77114a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c683813-90e6-5581-9ed4-32de3ac9d89f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109431" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\overset{A+20I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+20I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A-15I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-15I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+20I$ and $A-15I$ on the nonzero vectors $v,w,x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-20$ of $A$\n- $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =15$ of $A$\n- $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-20$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+20I\\,}{\\to }w,\n$$\n\nand $w\\ne 0,$  the vector $v$ is  **not** a eigenvector corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-20$  of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since\n\n$$\nx\\overset{A-15I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-15I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\n \ni.e. $A-15I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\nw\\overset{A+20I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+20I$ maps $w$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-20$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186247218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092329 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09f265bb-5c41-5ffa-9b48-c6bb37a645ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186244399} {:answer/id #uuid "1d431c9b-8382-5d8f-b9b0-42283b245b8c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186244571} {:answer/id #uuid "348e7919-5f0f-5625-8d2f-451e4271f334" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186244784} {:answer/id #uuid "49555ae5-edf4-5ace-bbe0-b4e2e3742af4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186244965} {:answer/id #uuid "abb5ac38-ec95-5ab2-b980-446bdef09cc9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186245850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d431c9b-8382-5d8f-b9b0-42283b245b8c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186244571} :answer-field/id #uuid "b19d5584-696a-50eb-abf1-46b3d0cabe51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186246871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bc384b99-060e-5785-8885-6518d3e3cae1" :question/math-academy-id "q-111385" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $4,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P+Q$ is always a polynomial\n- $P-Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $3$\n- $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}$ and ${x}^{4},$ respectively. Since there is no ${x}^{4}$ term in $P$ that could cancel the ${x}^{4}$ term in $Q,$ the leading term in $P-Q$  still contains ${x}^{4}.$  Therefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must always be $4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For the cases where $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is a polynomial, we need that the degree of $P$ should be greater or equal to the degree of $Q,$ which is not the case.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186247219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092329 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5124ae32-baff-5a5e-b443-7ff8988b2ea4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186245035} {:answer/id #uuid "5810337e-b168-587f-9b44-8d368a1827df" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186245093} {:answer/id #uuid "9e86bfe1-c666-593f-a98d-a90a7d6bf104" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186245725} {:answer/id #uuid "b740e7a5-64e2-54bf-82df-ed08e3b74b2a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186245950} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a0434c-285c-5ba8-b87f-5a72e3cfa744" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186246091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8a0434c-285c-5ba8-b87f-5a72e3cfa744" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186246091} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c6062c3-fb1b-5de1-abbd-70ab8d629b3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186246648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5d80a52b-66a2-53ce-b5ad-ecebb8222def" :question/math-academy-id "q-111390" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P\\cdot Q$ is a polynomial of degree $4$\n- $P-Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $1$\n- $P+Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must both contain ${x}^{2}.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P\\cdot Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{2+2}={x}^{4},$ meaning that $P\\cdot Q$ has degree $4.$\n\n- Statement II is true. For example, suppose that $P={x}^{2}+x$ and $Q={x}^{2}.$ Then, $P-Q={x}^{2}+x-{x}^{2}=x,$ which is a polynomial of degree $1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{2}$ and $Q=-{x}^{2}.$ Then, $P+Q={x}^{2}+(-{x}^{2})=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $2.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186247254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38aa6a51-31ca-5984-9d5d-d19d60a0c449" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186244815} {:answer/id #uuid "ad536d9f-7cd2-5ed6-a2c1-bc63f51b8a4c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186245861} {:answer/id #uuid "b2b8db5c-a01c-5ca1-9e35-92ba207ede86" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186245902} {:answer/id #uuid "b82741c2-c980-5382-a1c8-7d0bc3f9f44e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186245961} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe3ff0b-8b5c-5e7f-b030-48ed4481b008" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186246130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbe3ff0b-8b5c-5e7f-b030-48ed4481b008" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186246130} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1ce1d12-ffa5-58ce-9d0b-375fc82bc537" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186246832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eeced2e5-384e-5308-b21f-d88eb6c697e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-112252" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-4I:$\n\n$$\nA-4I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-4I:$\n\n$$\n(A-4I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-4I:$\n\n$$\n(A-4I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-4I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186247409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105709 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07aa5c51-0e03-50e7-b127-cad1de226daa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186244376} {:answer/id #uuid "16990bdb-28f1-595b-ad43-6305d816d9ba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186244522} {:answer/id #uuid "8ae18d57-318f-51e4-b723-891cce5c78b6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186245531} {:answer/id #uuid "b5002583-6da1-59ef-88b9-d912b357602c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186245928} {:answer/id #uuid "ea114e6e-30f2-504b-8968-68d0a2630e4c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186246388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16990bdb-28f1-595b-ad43-6305d816d9ba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186244522} :answer-field/id #uuid "0024239a-9bf1-56f5-ab34-159537b096ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186246549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4af7007e-9d89-5ab4-8e7e-891076eaef01" :question/math-academy-id "q-113756" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+4,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ is a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is a local maximum\n- $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a local minimum" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}-2x \\\\  & =2x(3x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(3x-1)\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,\\frac{1}{4},1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)(3(-1)-1)=8>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-\\frac{1}{4}) & =2(\\frac{1}{4})[3(\\frac{1}{4})-1]=-\\frac{1}{8}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =2(1)(3(1)-1)=4>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\frac{1}{3})$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $(\\frac{1}{3},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{1}{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186247410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105709 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14fe28ee-7cd7-5245-817f-aae50a8083b9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186244508} {:answer/id #uuid "24898c24-dbb3-5dad-a90c-ff2a954b9b54" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186244639} {:answer/id #uuid "9352c71d-da35-5891-9bd9-e0ebc7930970" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186245598} {:answer/id #uuid "a43f80cd-1a3a-55a0-9a8b-9f1bd94318c1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186245781} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa3efef-c16a-5eb6-abff-4303c4319b1e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186246397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eaa3efef-c16a-5eb6-abff-4303c4319b1e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186246397} :answer-field/id #uuid "873e81e1-6c75-5e95-a8f1-743624915346" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186246782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04886f18-ebcf-50be-b62d-6d531bb447d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-113758" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is a local minimum\n- $x=\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local minimum" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}+10x \\\\  & =2x(3x+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(3x+5)\\,⟹\\,x=0,-\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=-\\frac{5}{3}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =2(-2)(3(-2)+5)=4>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)(3(-1)+5)=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =2(1)(3(1)+5)=16>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3})$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ | $(-\\frac{5}{3},0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\frac{5}{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186250217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186184151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44469cf4-0087-5823-a317-eedd143765ce" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186248034} {:answer/id #uuid "47c407f3-6741-5f37-9308-c03497e32323" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186248062} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7a33a0-cf52-5f23-9361-e3cd70807f95" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186248274} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9c2fba-b47e-55cd-baaf-38ba767fce22" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186248374} {:answer/id #uuid "c029c445-7d11-5037-9659-d7acdfa66c9f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186249132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c029c445-7d11-5037-9659-d7acdfa66c9f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186249132} :answer-field/id #uuid "251d5cda-74a2-56e1-9838-ba89c8ed4c15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186249735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aab077f8-99df-5fb1-8e2c-0476334114c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-114270" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x+5}{{e}^{x}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-1,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,-1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-({x}^{2}+4x+5){e}^{-x}+(2x+4){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x}-2(x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x} & >0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+1) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=(x+1)(x-1).$\n\n![](images/09/09b4b237ec87a63e6de47eea5ed9156febbed0271dd0688c0be1c22a62c50c4e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $(x+1)(x-1)>0$ on  $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$  So the function is concave up on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $(x+1)(x-1)<0,$ which has the solution $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186250220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186184151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "269bcc9e-7349-5022-98fe-d1a04c20e249" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186247763} {:answer/id #uuid "427e810b-a1d1-55a0-aac5-04284f5c7978" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186248016} {:answer/id #uuid "6c6b32a0-9de0-5a56-ab94-84cad56f95ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186248382} {:answer/id #uuid "9ba17e09-0461-5443-8598-4289371cd848" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186248807} {:answer/id #uuid "d141f34c-d878-5d86-861b-0997e09d6538" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186249280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "427e810b-a1d1-55a0-aac5-04284f5c7978" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186248016} :answer-field/id #uuid "a48a241d-5e61-5925-814c-614001409430" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186249959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9e263520-c83b-578e-886f-bcaf93059761" :question/math-academy-id "q-114273" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}(5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x),$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,e)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,e)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(e,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x(5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =2x(5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-2x \\\\  & =2x(4-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2(4-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-2x\\cdot \\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =8-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-4 \\\\  & =4(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & >0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & <1 \\\\ x & <e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on  $(0,e).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $4(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)<0,$ which has the solution $(e,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186250261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07d50fff-56b7-5f92-bc0a-0e5f926b2ff4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186247494} {:answer/id #uuid "14e95a3a-ad27-5884-b28b-756c2876e80d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id 17592186247612} {:answer/id #uuid "91c35d63-f2bc-52f1-848d-2178b02cc3ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186248717} {:answer/id #uuid "93667d31-d08f-599a-a0eb-d2fc75e2367b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186248732} {:answer/id #uuid "a099e6de-a8b8-51b6-b6a2-c60eabd847d9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186248859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14e95a3a-ad27-5884-b28b-756c2876e80d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id 17592186247612} :answer-field/id #uuid "f86fae06-35b1-5d2b-83fc-975e717bcae4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186250109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b90d338d-3db1-5cee-ac01-ae8e8b265add" :question/math-academy-id "q-114415" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2}){e}^{x},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0$\n- ${f}^{″}(0)>0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2}){e}^{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{x}+(-1+2x){e}^{x}=({x}^{2}+x){e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+x){e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ x(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=-1,0.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=({x}^{2}+x)\\cdot {e}^{x}+(2x+1){e}^{x}=({x}^{2}+3x+1){e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =-{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I and II are true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so there is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statements III is false. $x=1$ is not a critical point.\n\n- Statement IV is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186253256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75c67e5-3469-5730-9181-54df4ec6d5b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Inverse of a Larger Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "252eb562-a59a-54e9-a0e7-a82ebe0fed97" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186250870} {:answer/id #uuid "910ad069-914c-534a-9508-c27c627d2f9e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186251789} {:answer/id #uuid "b8a39af0-7ff5-592f-84e8-03eb4ed8f333" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186252176} {:answer/id #uuid "d89c7671-fc3e-5675-abfd-446c27281eb9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186252467} {:answer/id #uuid "df9833a5-548b-5a39-b460-6d31e34b2f42" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186252526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "910ad069-914c-534a-9508-c27c627d2f9e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186251789} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f13427e-6207-5184-b73e-8b87eb3704b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186252911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9db68f61-a1ba-5fec-8a45-32913742b631" :question/math-academy-id "q-11634" :question/problem "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,C=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $AB=BA={I}_{3}$\n- $AC=CA={I}_{3}$\n- $B={A}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{3}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{3}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false. As a result, $B\\ne {A}^{-1}$ and statement III is false too.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{3}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $CA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CA & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true. \nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186253257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75c67e5-3469-5730-9181-54df4ec6d5b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Inverse of a Larger Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "949bed7b-70d0-5e48-b756-90f80a4e2eb4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186251831} {:answer/id #uuid "a5b1e844-6b92-5d1c-864e-2a61bbbba312" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  only" :db/id 17592186251982} {:answer/id #uuid "e47ea405-b037-512c-82fb-169bad0765ac" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186252557} {:answer/id #uuid "ec8cc7c7-f494-5778-abfe-ddf4346490fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186252625} {:answer/id #uuid "fab7818a-d935-521f-821a-14e4a265a64b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186252750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5b1e844-6b92-5d1c-864e-2a61bbbba312" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  only" :db/id 17592186251982} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea0b2479-da3a-5c69-9eab-fdc63ba4ff6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186253214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a987f21-4e16-5f44-b8f8-8e69cf93fc24" :question/math-academy-id "q-11636" :question/problem "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -17\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -17\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $AC=CA={I}_{3}$\n- $AD=DA={I}_{3}$\n- $D={A}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "- First, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{3}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -17\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $CA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CA & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -17\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AD=DA={I}_{3}.$ Computing $AD$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AD & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -17\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 7 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{3}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false. As a result, $D\\ne {A}^{-1}$ and statement III is false too.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186253258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087699 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b9ef325-2358-5041-8a8d-b459e8ed9f09" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "203d12f3-8c3a-5557-837d-97614900aecd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186250827} {:answer/id #uuid "37fa1324-7dc6-58cd-a405-664a3029c979" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186251017} {:answer/id #uuid "43c9ad1f-5027-564e-8d6f-f65d7b6af604" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id 17592186251120} {:answer/id #uuid "7d5992ac-076a-5473-a6d9-3e4bba83cb3b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186251599} {:answer/id #uuid "b34057e3-6fe3-51a0-8d42-660e355aaf1a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186252122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b34057e3-6fe3-51a0-8d42-660e355aaf1a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186252122} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c14b9f0-5ae4-545c-9581-c501daf9503a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186253001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "297364cd-4444-5a41-ba36-21b226267ecd" :question/math-academy-id "q-11637" :question/problem "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,E=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $CD=DC={I}_{2}$\n\nII. $DE=ED={I}_{2}$\n\nIII. $D={C}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "- First, we check whether $CD=DC={I}_{2}.$ Computing $CD$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $DC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DC & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true. As a result, $D={C}^{-1}$ and statement III is true too.\n\n- Now, we check whether $DE=ED={I}_{2}.$ Computing $DE$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DE & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ -2 & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186253501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e93fd7f-070f-5359-aabb-cba5cc275015" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent or Divergent Integrals With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "256aa671-a669-57d4-b91d-ced24db81e43" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186250876} {:answer/id #uuid "5a277d68-b884-5e7d-b52b-01b0500937b3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186251295} {:answer/id #uuid "ac672eaa-7a45-596b-aff7-c0a0076f4abb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186252053} {:answer/id #uuid "c6240e34-c165-5a5d-b60f-e16d15bc4bb8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186252304} {:answer/id #uuid "eb6959c8-58ee-530d-bb52-f3078500cbf9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186252618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "256aa671-a669-57d4-b91d-ced24db81e43" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186250876} :answer-field/id #uuid "92e60e35-c089-5683-b48c-03659e05f5af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186253059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "260fa3cd-f59d-50c6-a56e-8cfbd29393f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-118755" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n\nII. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}+4$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4))|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, we have that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{2}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, we have that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement III is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements II and III are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186253504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e93fd7f-070f-5359-aabb-cba5cc275015" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent or Divergent Integrals With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c9540bd-3579-55ee-9427-762171958cc0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186251189} {:answer/id #uuid "6fee837b-87c6-54d7-a3ac-b451e92ebd95" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186251465} {:answer/id #uuid "7540a8de-35a1-54d9-8dcb-7408266ac064" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186251520} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb220c0-023c-5f4c-9950-83aa36861022" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186251897} {:answer/id #uuid "c40cfe51-cc69-500e-a887-804197c0c982" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186252290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7540a8de-35a1-54d9-8dcb-7408266ac064" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186251520} :answer-field/id #uuid "3046fd95-8b83-50f6-b6e3-36de5d1c4279" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186252887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "96df78fc-91b5-5b18-90a4-e309d8513bb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-118761" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}))|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement III is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186253550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3aa84ae8-6663-5aa1-844b-89090bcce735" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10006a78-2990-5fbf-824c-f21d0f1f27ed" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186250692} {:answer/id #uuid "6adfdb6c-e9a9-5110-96b1-5d2a004665a0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186251429} {:answer/id #uuid "797abc94-c803-5003-96a4-c3c807c594b4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186251561} {:answer/id #uuid "c7050d0d-7313-55d2-8d35-7977fc1cbbbb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186252311} {:answer/id #uuid "c98da25f-3bba-5828-9399-33c0fe2d1fe9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186252336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6adfdb6c-e9a9-5110-96b1-5d2a004665a0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186251429} :answer-field/id #uuid "26eaa285-2f95-5895-8123-747f0c43b2f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186252876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19539888-a72b-5a8c-9bb4-399cabb49fb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-119194" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2x+2$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $x=0,$ the slope field has a positive slope\n- At every point along the line $x=-1,$ the slope field is horizontal\n- At every point along the line $x=-2,$ the slope field has a positive slope" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x,$ the slope does not vary with $y.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,y)}=2(0)+2=2>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(0,y)$ is positive for any $y.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(0,y)$ is equal to $2$ for any $y.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(-1,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,y)}=2(-1)+2=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(-1,y)$ is horizontal for any $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(-2,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,y)}=2(-2)+2=-2<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(-2,y)$ is negative for any $y.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(-2,y)$ is equal to $-2$ for any $y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8d/8d40c405f3f4db66a18e06a5327e86600081a6bc57b502ebed9c94471a0d5857.png)"}] [{:db/id 17592186253552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3aa84ae8-6663-5aa1-844b-89090bcce735" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46656338-4739-5650-ad33-f76f66264ce9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186251137} {:answer/id #uuid "485dee28-4452-538d-9880-9c643ec716cc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186251156} {:answer/id #uuid "60dcbe0e-d588-51af-a154-a3b7cb766ab8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186251344} {:answer/id #uuid "627f1ca5-8fc9-5332-914c-95e93fdc6407" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186251353} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b59e1f-156d-532f-ba64-499c0eacd612" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186252731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "627f1ca5-8fc9-5332-914c-95e93fdc6407" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186251353} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c0ed69e-071f-52f5-91be-3fbd5bc46bf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186253047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6eca5ac4-aebb-5558-a54f-a273db0d7d0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-119206" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}-3x$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $x=2,$  the slope field has a negative slope\n- At every point along the line $x=0,$  the slope field is horizontal\n- At every point along the line $y=1,$ the slope of the slope field does not vary" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x,$ the slope does not vary with $y.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(2,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,y)}={2}^{2}-3(2)=-2,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(2,y)$ is negative for any $y.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,y)}={0}^{2}-3(0)=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(0,y)$ is horizontal for any $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,1)}={x}^{2}-3x,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(x,1)$ depends on $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"\n\nWe can use the information given by the true statements to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e2/e2ce8d7df3d0ceee4f8c9dcf368940006f1266a751c2f8e5ea8afdcfb90bf7ac.png)"}] [{:db/id 17592186253554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3aa84ae8-6663-5aa1-844b-89090bcce735" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f52f696-291c-580c-9fe7-e956c46af8a9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186250955} {:answer/id #uuid "727eb153-c7fc-58e6-a439-cfeaf3020a4b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186251494} {:answer/id #uuid "8cd863f8-ab86-5b05-923c-8fa8ec0dcb3d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186251743} {:answer/id #uuid "90802024-e6f7-5693-82a4-3b6e8dd5c6a3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186251781} {:answer/id #uuid "bde2e344-fcdb-5936-ac4e-4bdaf30f5bee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186252233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bde2e344-fcdb-5936-ac4e-4bdaf30f5bee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186252233} :answer-field/id #uuid "0518aa8b-609d-5778-afa6-bb1ba34520e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186252817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e9d7c3e-9e55-5477-a8a9-aed5b3e5371d" :question/math-academy-id "q-119209" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-{x}^{3}$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $x=1,$  the slope field is horizontal\n- At every point along the line $x=0,$  the slope field has a positive slope\n- At every point along the line $y=2,$ the slope of the slope field varies" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x,$ the slope does not vary with $y.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(1,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,y)}=1-{1}^{3}=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(1,y)$ is horizontal for any $y.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,y)}=1-{0}^{3}=1>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(0,y)$ is positive for any $y.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(0,y)$ is equal to $1$ for any $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,2)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,2)}=1-{x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(x,2)$ depends on $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"\n\nWe can use the information given by the true statements to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/85/85d8f71c4680a56d5722b87fa5f7b2a7623fd2457f540c2cd15cd3bfa29694f3.png)"}] [{:db/id 17592186253568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172036 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1343e4de-56ee-563c-bdc8-f8c0cdc54f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38f784c0-4ffa-57ee-9f1d-ad1d0c921ca7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186251025} {:answer/id #uuid "42d7fdf7-2d34-5ca5-9453-2a9056e46e13" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186251111} {:answer/id #uuid "a417dc6e-709d-544f-b95d-a1eabe504d46" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186251967} {:answer/id #uuid "dc857a0c-a1b2-5245-9a22-f478ab643975" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186252493} {:answer/id #uuid "f29468d1-a700-51d9-b407-95be3e26ae96" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186252678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38f784c0-4ffa-57ee-9f1d-ad1d0c921ca7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186251025} :answer-field/id #uuid "425e4036-da62-5a42-b19e-1d8e14d29d21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186252919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95486801-04a3-50a0-ad5b-8d31467cc59e" :question/math-academy-id "q-119324" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $y=2,$ the slope field has a positive slope\n- At every point along the line $y=0,$ the slope field is horizontal\n- At every point along the line $y=-2,$ the slope field has a negative slope" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,2)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,2)}=-2<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,2)$ is negative for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,2)$ is equal to $-2$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,0)}=-0=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is horizontal for any $x.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,-2)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-2)}=-(-2)=2>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is positive for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is equal to $2$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8f/8fc0b5be5c9147c29410ff7a082316403302f2bce7ec25c665ab7ccc5bf90b0f.png)"}] [{:db/id 17592186256520 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "264f5372-70d7-5181-833b-4003340e25a7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/id 17592186254010} {:answer/id #uuid "4b8f2571-08a9-585c-ad63-46a55a052d55" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186254319} {:answer/id #uuid "5e4c2e89-dfae-55d6-b161-a7a82e38e30d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id 17592186254507} {:answer/id #uuid "a2633cdb-34a8-52c8-b9e5-4431a054921b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id 17592186255103} {:answer/id #uuid "e75b7301-8e33-5bfa-b1e5-7156a044b335" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id 17592186255705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "264f5372-70d7-5181-833b-4003340e25a7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/id 17592186254010} :answer-field/id #uuid "adba1f90-63df-547d-9c23-20010b796f64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186256234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc49fe00-4ce7-5ef1-a571-3c5c05755fd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-1212" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in N∣-1\\le x+1\\le 3}$ and $B={x\\in Z∣{x}^{2}\\le -2}?$\n\n- $A⊆B$\n- $A⊈B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $A⊇B$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $-1\\le x+1\\le 3,$ that is to say, $-2\\le x\\le 2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA={1,2}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}\\le -2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB=∅.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false, while $A⊈B$ is true. For example, $0\\in A$ but $0\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true. The empty set is a subset of every set.\n\n- $A⊇B$ is equivalent to $B⊆A,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II, III, and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186259616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186179769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36996ffc-7dbc-51f8-8efe-46ed2c897045" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Affine Transformation is Orientation-Preserving"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ccab3c0-0254-5090-9c86-829a851b15b0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186256907} {:answer/id #uuid "2c4c2cb2-f173-5288-817b-371883d27529" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186257179} {:answer/id #uuid "60740839-3d41-5df6-8849-4084e44e6ec1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186257630} {:answer/id #uuid "b74a87c3-7a0f-5446-af5b-58a1007116ff" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186258382} {:answer/id #uuid "d661a75a-735f-5ece-a15b-6a8a834246d2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186258665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b74a87c3-7a0f-5446-af5b-58a1007116ff" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186258382} :answer-field/id #uuid "33b4cd81-c3a3-52e0-a0d4-355e8ea3aed5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186259128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0a1ba57-9eac-5d49-9cdd-f7f25fec8c92" :question/math-academy-id "q-124192" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=2v+u+1,\\,y=u-v-2.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=-3$\n- The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2v+u+1 \\\\ u-v-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+2v \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 1=-3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)<0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is not orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186259620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186179770 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e39ae7fc-ff0a-5b97-8e13-1f15b12a07e5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Invertible Affine Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14310a37-4198-59f8-a67a-bb4605bcdefe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186256962} {:answer/id #uuid "3df23a95-1aa1-5f48-b3ea-cb555e63ff8c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186257335} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a06906-43e3-5906-962d-17a930bb44da" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186258370} {:answer/id #uuid "b74f4b9f-cdb3-530d-98f1-56fef3db1e29" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186258383} {:answer/id #uuid "fff284a7-4a7b-55bf-88b7-730055350deb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186259032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fff284a7-4a7b-55bf-88b7-730055350deb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186259032} :answer-field/id #uuid "cacc6956-8e11-5e85-a7f1-b3155060ce0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186259388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3230d0ef-3334-5e9c-bc28-426c4388c6b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-124213" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=2u-6v-2,\\,y=5v-u+9.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=4$\n- The transformation is invertible" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-6v-2 \\\\ 5v-u+9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-6v \\\\ -u+5v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\ne [\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|=5\\cdot 2-(-6)\\cdot (-1)=4\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $det(A)\\ne 0,$ the affine transformation is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186259621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186179770 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e39ae7fc-ff0a-5b97-8e13-1f15b12a07e5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Invertible Affine Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49404867-32eb-59ef-a4a4-b7da9a657dff" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186257423} {:answer/id #uuid "6767b9f2-5106-5aa5-bf2a-7adc83b01629" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186257699} {:answer/id #uuid "6c4d898f-71d3-5a35-8bb8-6866a7ebfa01" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186257738} {:answer/id #uuid "6e4b3bc4-0435-5de9-bb90-5b7a7be62e1e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186257754} {:answer/id #uuid "f697f1e3-10c3-54cf-9750-bd13eb63c2ce" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186258942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49404867-32eb-59ef-a4a4-b7da9a657dff" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186257423} :answer-field/id #uuid "36904d97-975f-5794-8fc8-e42061448caf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186259133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "67b5699e-cde1-5170-92cc-4d8a2c62f657" :question/math-academy-id "q-124215" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=8u-6v+2,\\,y=4u-3v-1.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=1$\n- The transformation is invertible" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}8u-6v+2 \\\\ 4u-3v-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8u-6v \\\\ 4u-3v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}8 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}8 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}|=(-3)\\cdot 8-(-6)\\cdot 4=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)=0,$ the affine transformation is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186259623 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186179770 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e39ae7fc-ff0a-5b97-8e13-1f15b12a07e5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Invertible Affine Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0940047a-73ea-5b9c-89a4-1b541f165713" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186256864} {:answer/id #uuid "20f49f6f-678f-5191-8372-4e67aebdffdc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186257052} {:answer/id #uuid "b64135b7-9dac-5aed-ba61-15db8bcca77c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186258367} {:answer/id #uuid "d5f52f2c-e0d1-5cdf-8a54-353813d97f87" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186258660} {:answer/id #uuid "de154b68-9f9e-5549-8d72-3a8853fe5182" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186258713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b64135b7-9dac-5aed-ba61-15db8bcca77c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186258367} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f5504b1-809e-5b02-b6a7-71863c2f0d86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186259169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8f35619-da30-55db-920a-269c9cc6d2af" :question/math-academy-id "q-124219" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=4u+2v-5,\\,y=2u+v+6.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=0$\n- The transformation is invertible" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4u+2v-5 \\\\ 2u+v+6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4u+2v \\\\ 2u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\ne [\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 2=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)=0,$ the affine transformation is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186259662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186179769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36996ffc-7dbc-51f8-8efe-46ed2c897045" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Affine Transformation is Orientation-Preserving"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cbf8e90-0fcd-5e9d-ad8e-5d029388c5ac" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186257325} {:answer/id #uuid "43d247fa-abed-5958-b466-10fc02c6fd8a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186257379} {:answer/id #uuid "4681c1f9-2a9f-5136-9cf7-ed7506a00643" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186257398} {:answer/id #uuid "5d0eefab-460e-5b30-b83c-d3d6a7dee230" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186257593} {:answer/id #uuid "7277e3e9-81db-502b-97dc-3210914ad7a7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186257788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cbf8e90-0fcd-5e9d-ad8e-5d029388c5ac" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186257325} :answer-field/id #uuid "ced03044-c848-5e8d-a236-e8367ae6dd32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186259397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6b75fa0-1178-5417-bc31-861bbdef5229" :question/math-academy-id "q-124321" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=2v-u+2,\\,y=2u+v-5.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=-5$\n- The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2v-u+2 \\\\ 2u+v-5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-u+2v \\\\ 2u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|=(-1)\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2=-5.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)<0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is not orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186259663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186179769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36996ffc-7dbc-51f8-8efe-46ed2c897045" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Affine Transformation is Orientation-Preserving"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3b15a36-4c42-5d07-9a51-dc6ad5c486df" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186258213} {:answer/id #uuid "ab597bed-1042-5692-b586-e88675ae5611" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186258276} {:answer/id #uuid "c51bd258-ccef-574e-8d8a-369f597425dc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186258507} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e0901a-36a7-5935-b68d-61058dd5acaa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186258757} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf750b6-b5ec-59b0-9432-1f0a64b2f1fb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186258829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c51bd258-ccef-574e-8d8a-369f597425dc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186258507} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b527301-31c5-53bf-9a72-f0270d30af58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186259216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8bc55bfe-e692-5546-9ff3-60dd10f588d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-124322" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=u+3v-5,\\,y=2v+u-4.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=1$\n- The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u+3v-5 \\\\ 2v+u-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+3v \\\\ u+2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 3=-1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)<0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is not orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186259664 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186179769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36996ffc-7dbc-51f8-8efe-46ed2c897045" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Affine Transformation is Orientation-Preserving"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "481926d1-ef5a-5d53-83ff-cfd16975918c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186257414} {:answer/id #uuid "557f4ffe-1d22-5c8b-9201-70b029ea63d2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186257515} {:answer/id #uuid "7cdff457-70dc-5437-9f0c-b944cf906cb9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186257883} {:answer/id #uuid "95977828-2ec8-5627-8808-2bfb34245e2a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186258097} {:answer/id #uuid "f264dc88-68bc-54ab-874c-ca48aa6dac07" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186258892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "557f4ffe-1d22-5c8b-9201-70b029ea63d2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186257515} :answer-field/id #uuid "f204c306-fb9f-519a-acf5-4bb2b535b275" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186259452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0642bf2f-73a7-542a-b570-be0c81694e35" :question/math-academy-id "q-124324" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=4u-3v-5,\\,y=-2u+2v+4.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=2$\n- The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4u-3v-5 \\\\ -2u+2v+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4u-3v \\\\ -2u+2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}|=4\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot (-2)=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $det(A)>0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186259666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186179769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36996ffc-7dbc-51f8-8efe-46ed2c897045" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Affine Transformation is Orientation-Preserving"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c2b23cf-b52f-58bd-aa08-d93e55b89a93" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186257320} {:answer/id #uuid "74058a71-0837-5bfd-b69a-0b03739da1d6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186257798} {:answer/id #uuid "91ccac56-2f99-5ce0-a1cf-b313d1671178" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186258069} {:answer/id #uuid "d55a8bc2-38dc-5084-bc68-22c681e86ac0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186258651} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b410c3-72ac-556b-9401-28386466a984" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186258908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d55a8bc2-38dc-5084-bc68-22c681e86ac0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186258651} :answer-field/id #uuid "404bb6d8-df1d-5a16-b9b3-4f8d94fec9aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186259148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e2308b7-d41e-52ef-9ac3-a8e7e1231e38" :question/math-academy-id "q-124326" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=u-1,\\,y=u-v-2.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=-1$\n- The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-1 \\\\ u-v-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 0=-1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)<0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is not orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186259667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186179769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36996ffc-7dbc-51f8-8efe-46ed2c897045" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Affine Transformation is Orientation-Preserving"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a636f48-1220-5efd-ae5e-4a26c3374e65" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186257156} {:answer/id #uuid "42f6d7ca-8ac9-5481-af94-5de7bedd3071" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186257372} {:answer/id #uuid "57de7293-cdb9-5955-b763-82a06940b176" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186257542} {:answer/id #uuid "8d4b13b3-f609-5c45-8906-79654b37fdc8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186258024} {:answer/id #uuid "a4591847-65ab-5ee0-afb3-cc24c35c7fd5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id 17592186258217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4591847-65ab-5ee0-afb3-cc24c35c7fd5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id 17592186258217} :answer-field/id #uuid "be183b9e-c1bf-5cc8-bf1b-f677c714e798" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186259361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2083542b-ea7a-58bb-b614-773ec287a63e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124327" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=v-2u,\\,y=u+3v-1.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=-7$\n- The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}v-2u \\\\ u+3v-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2u+v \\\\ u+3v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|=(-2)\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 1=-7.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)<0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is not orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186259668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186179769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36996ffc-7dbc-51f8-8efe-46ed2c897045" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Affine Transformation is Orientation-Preserving"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39ae90c1-c064-56e7-a097-a8780cfd4ec3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186257291} {:answer/id #uuid "558d0acd-b95f-508e-818b-d7aed7ccb8c3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186257517} {:answer/id #uuid "68442875-3c8e-5cbe-89fb-8a3004e6152a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186257705} {:answer/id #uuid "8be768e1-1047-59b7-976d-c2b3c8c6d97c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186258007} {:answer/id #uuid "f87afd55-6808-569a-9937-dbc59aca01bc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186258962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68442875-3c8e-5cbe-89fb-8a3004e6152a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186257705} :answer-field/id #uuid "f465cacf-0fa4-5950-9818-e0e076a19708" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186259456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76a65107-91d8-5259-9f73-6e2fee8f6ae9" :question/math-academy-id "q-124330" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=-2v+1,\\,y=4u-v-5.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=-4$\n- The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2v+1 \\\\ 4u-v-5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2v \\\\ 4u-v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}|=0\\cdot (-1)-4\\cdot (-2)=8.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $det(A)>0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186259836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186179743 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da220a9-2afc-5b63-a423-a4d2b3de9567" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation in Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e034501-818d-5819-9c93-5a81eda1a89c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186257029} {:answer/id #uuid "be456b97-51d9-568d-b82b-1aa5f17aee37" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186258444} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6b28d7-9bc1-5810-a054-b8b825e80551" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186258573} {:answer/id #uuid "d50afa2b-f7cb-54f1-a85d-5acd9f94c95c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186258646} {:answer/id #uuid "da7b34a4-e786-5389-98e6-e4c535a7c87e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186258690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da7b34a4-e786-5389-98e6-e4c535a7c87e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186258690} :answer-field/id #uuid "952c57f8-7816-5b03-aa69-f94b38b1a845" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186259294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b7ddac2-e3bd-590b-a396-56e8108da7f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-125647" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $∇f$ is conservative\n- ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- ${∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $∇f$ is conservative by definition.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186259837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186179743 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da220a9-2afc-5b63-a423-a4d2b3de9567" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation in Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "137da4ef-47d9-5de8-a462-4818ba6dc10d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186256957} {:answer/id #uuid "1dbadb2c-8ac8-53d5-8ffc-e3be10141db5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186257027} {:answer/id #uuid "66ece0e9-5a00-5213-b699-33320cdb4941" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186257692} {:answer/id #uuid "92ec44a1-640b-58b0-b50e-81f2515b9773" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186258080} {:answer/id #uuid "d6c0b00d-b88f-5f91-bbf7-408af30b23ba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186258669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6c0b00d-b88f-5f91-bbf7-408af30b23ba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186258669} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5f51cf9-6122-509c-b8e3-dc58e68ca09a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186259407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78e40fa2-e07f-5983-b8b1-ed694102f21b" :question/math-academy-id "q-125648" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)={e}^{y}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $∇f$ is **not** conservative\n- ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any piecewise linear path $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- ${∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any circle $C\\in {R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The vector field $∇f$ is conservative by definition.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186259847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72015667-33a9-53e2-a0c4-09d8ce916b54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Are Independent of Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "496ae432-c4f8-5a13-8e7d-526cea9118ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186257424} {:answer/id #uuid "669b0d8c-34fc-51f2-ba55-e5bc17d883b0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186257686} {:answer/id #uuid "6c84c9d7-a348-563a-9aeb-3f96b98f6ade" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186257740} {:answer/id #uuid "b00dd55c-3121-5e54-b05d-ba61234d7035" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186258317} {:answer/id #uuid "ed3644a7-5394-5fbe-8e53-a4df0a022306" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186258831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b00dd55c-3121-5e54-b05d-ba61234d7035" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186258317} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b5f6320-1357-595d-959c-9c5cc6e08ef0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186259078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "769cb3f6-44e5-5633-bebf-2f89e5fbe161" :question/math-academy-id "q-125723" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is conservative.\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=1,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186259853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72015667-33a9-53e2-a0c4-09d8ce916b54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Are Independent of Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28898fac-f08a-5a75-84e0-156244b73755" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186257143} {:answer/id #uuid "2c2e18d0-68a5-5b54-bf52-48427e607c00" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186257178} {:answer/id #uuid "38e946af-8405-57df-8377-ba9d08fd7c6d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186257283} {:answer/id #uuid "4149c3f5-cbde-563f-812c-bf48efd0df79" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186257364} {:answer/id #uuid "e956ab6b-da87-54ae-866a-dc5eda3ffc1f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186258806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4149c3f5-cbde-563f-812c-bf48efd0df79" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186257364} :answer-field/id #uuid "387dc8ef-18ee-5b03-ad0e-a4166fba523d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186259137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "230c0014-f97a-5ec3-b394-cdb780e495b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-125738" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨2x{y}^{2},\\,2y{x}^{2}⟩$ and the points $A(1,1)$ and $B(2,8)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is conservative.\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=255,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=255,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{3},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,8)-f(1,1) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}-{1}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 64-1 \\\\  & =255.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=255.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186259861 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72015667-33a9-53e2-a0c4-09d8ce916b54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Are Independent of Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "044479e8-8353-5925-b209-ed1dc86282e9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III and IV only" :db/id 17592186256817} {:answer/id #uuid "57304dfc-6cd8-5be2-b9e3-3bdc63240c3f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186257536} {:answer/id #uuid "755fa6cf-b59f-529b-9144-03ffa617ba29" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186257811} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d3a40e-4ce0-5a35-bd64-d727279a824f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186258643} {:answer/id #uuid "fae400e7-02fb-5abe-a2a5-6a53924db2e5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186258977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fae400e7-02fb-5abe-a2a5-6a53924db2e5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186258977} :answer-field/id #uuid "22c5d269-4a40-589d-b690-a0293e2b0946" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186259093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a9fbb00-26f5-5603-96a1-9b8b6773d49d" :question/math-academy-id "q-125789" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,1⟩$ and the points $A(1,0)$ and $B(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ in counterclockwise direction.\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{1}{3},$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path.\n- $F$ is conservative." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Parameterizing the circle, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}⟨{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}⟨1,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨1-t,\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨(1-t)^{2}+{t}^{2},\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨2{t}^{2}-2t+1,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2t-2{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{2}-\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative."}] [{:db/id 17592186259862 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72015667-33a9-53e2-a0c4-09d8ce916b54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Are Independent of Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bdef8f8-a7bf-5fb0-a41f-412497adfde1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186257174} {:answer/id #uuid "3856820c-3ecc-5707-859b-c09e81d097bf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186257280} {:answer/id #uuid "b89fae9d-29db-523b-9d17-1d52c0d00805" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186258397} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a8007e-fa4d-58d2-8ec7-17d2dd4c50de" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/id 17592186258511} {:answer/id #uuid "d68f1b5f-8b69-516a-b393-0fcd5a14284d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id 17592186258666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3856820c-3ecc-5707-859b-c09e81d097bf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186257280} :answer-field/id #uuid "113ba359-7abc-5570-9498-bb77e0272c69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186259063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8fb181d0-594b-53e9-a69e-a9a120629b9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-125791" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨y,\\,{x}^{2}⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{3},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{5}{6},$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path.\n- $F$ is conservative." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Parameterizing the curve, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{3}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨{t}^{3},\\,{t}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}+3{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{3{t}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{5}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨t,\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t+{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative."}] [{:db/id 17592186259864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72015667-33a9-53e2-a0c4-09d8ce916b54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Are Independent of Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "520e2d98-e741-50e8-8065-4873fc355d17" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186257480} {:answer/id #uuid "c56af481-1b7f-5728-8f73-5602dc0d8b8c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/id 17592186258509} {:answer/id #uuid "de5095db-8b56-536c-8a7b-2d3f319c9d41" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186258716} {:answer/id #uuid "f93c5a26-1372-5a8d-a1a7-7d8ed2b81cb9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186258969} {:answer/id #uuid "fe6667d7-c6d4-5a7a-b83f-f8ab326d3c03" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186259013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe6667d7-c6d4-5a7a-b83f-f8ab326d3c03" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186259013} :answer-field/id #uuid "904a3999-b6dd-533e-80b8-ef24d2a7d7df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186259284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "da972c83-042d-5f07-b576-d951f35ad923" :question/math-academy-id "q-125793" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{y}^{2},\\,-x⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(4,2)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{5}{6},$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{16}{3},$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path.\n- $F$ is conservative." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨4t,\\,2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨4{t}^{2},\\,-4t⟩\\cdot ⟨4,\\,2⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}16{t}^{2}-8t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{t}^{2}-t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{[\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =8[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Parameterizing the curve, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}⟨{t}^{2},\\,-{t}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2t,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{2}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3})-0 \\\\  & =8-\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative."}] [{:db/id 17592186262585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6128eca-7f76-5386-9d67-d81db2943eeb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Cartesian Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdaa1a87-6e99-5031-b9c8-860e9a813965" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186261687} {:answer/id #uuid "e3fe8843-09ad-592e-87ed-ac3d69bfc957" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186261880} {:answer/id #uuid "e7bca458-b993-5f97-9598-8c952434b6d7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186261906} {:answer/id #uuid "e9cb64c4-e195-5c60-8d7c-793cd6180b91" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186261923} {:answer/id #uuid "f3405623-3647-5bf1-b509-876eda8bdcdf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186262001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdaa1a87-6e99-5031-b9c8-860e9a813965" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186261687} :answer-field/id #uuid "df23ffbf-8b12-5fb1-aeae-7cd34233b519" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186262512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46648507-d053-5fe5-a8fd-c1de8f3e7a6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-1261" :question/problem "For the sets $X={x,y,z},$ $Y={v,w},$ and $Z=X\\times Y,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(x,v)\\in Z$\n- $(y,w)\\in Z$\n- $(w,z)\\in Z$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $X\\times Y$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $X$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $Y.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $X\\times Y$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/31/316a12102e4be81291f06403815cfb63749b1fd3f463c465294e031543123484.png)\n\nHence, the set $Z=X\\times Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nZ={(x,v),(x,w),(y,v),(y,w),(z,v),(z,w)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186262850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115234 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06497266-004d-59be-b434-27ee213d4da3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id 17592186259930} {:answer/id #uuid "15cd33db-73db-535d-b0c9-819460e83bae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  only" :db/id 17592186260065} {:answer/id #uuid "72dfc762-af03-5019-bea0-632e59016da2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  and  III only" :db/id 17592186260879} {:answer/id #uuid "768f2492-46d1-5ce0-b1f2-60b8db25ce27" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III  only" :db/id 17592186260911} {:answer/id #uuid "a8c267f6-962c-5b49-b551-f8a9d978a396" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  and  III only" :db/id 17592186261351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06497266-004d-59be-b434-27ee213d4da3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id 17592186259930} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1805e23-6f52-5992-81f4-50acbfc71da5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186262471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "012090a8-b5e2-5e56-87ba-9a6b486504ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-127918" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=-11 \\\\ y+7x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The coefficient matrix is not invertible\n- The determinant of the coefficient matrix is not zero\n- The lines $x+2y=-11$ and $y+7x=4$ intersect at infinitely many  points" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186262851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115234 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f450702-acf4-58a7-b147-c031752d7027" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186260019} {:answer/id #uuid "2ad1ab5b-2c16-5891-9da0-a94103b6a01f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186260253} {:answer/id #uuid "5af29382-2f60-52d5-be29-c6245e488118" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186260652} {:answer/id #uuid "add041e3-1b6e-56a8-8215-74a07df2021a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186261404} {:answer/id #uuid "b915bc94-a723-5075-99fa-b4d05aa76a1c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186261497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f450702-acf4-58a7-b147-c031752d7027" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186260019} :answer-field/id #uuid "1208d2a6-3b36-508a-a51f-4ba90eaa7fa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186262155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd31265e-20ac-5d02-8e25-ee3bd3fdfd6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-127919" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=b$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $det(A)\\ne 0$\n\nII. $A$ is  invertible\n\nIII. $Ax=0$ has a nonzero solution" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186262852 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115234 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dadc561-e438-570e-b44f-1b8e53f59378" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186260286} {:answer/id #uuid "752a682c-e1fd-5671-b804-f1cc65adc737" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186260900} {:answer/id #uuid "a62bbd2a-0bd5-5a53-aea1-69360b95edab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186261319} {:answer/id #uuid "b78a6953-b243-58b3-a577-05e5dad4e0d1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186261486} {:answer/id #uuid "dde02b6b-c5bc-578a-95ff-8693bed5e236" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186261836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b78a6953-b243-58b3-a577-05e5dad4e0d1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186261486} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc1f30c2-b014-5360-9716-aa752cee2e91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186262506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "112c3205-5323-5719-a0c7-431fff65b0ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-127920" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y=-1 \\\\ 3y-x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The lines $2x-2y=-1$ and $3y-x=1$ intersect at infinitely many  points\n- The coefficient matrix is not  invertible\n- The determinant of the coefficient matrix is not equal to zero" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186262854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a8d56e9-3626-5046-9b33-d237dc2abe22" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186260249} {:answer/id #uuid "38a7ddda-6916-5b65-94ec-0747657f9544" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186260377} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3f9eab-acb3-50bc-a9ee-8798c9949bab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186261089} {:answer/id #uuid "b887f738-86dc-5bdc-b540-c5f1fdbde499" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186261493} {:answer/id #uuid "f19961e2-d42c-5556-a4e9-3d5116c5fb42" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186261990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38a7ddda-6916-5b65-94ec-0747657f9544" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186260377} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cfe8fe5-5ef7-5490-9d3e-833f392f8ad1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186262302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "364a18ef-2de8-57d7-9e2f-631eb4651446" :question/math-academy-id "q-127931" :question/problem "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne -3.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $Ax=0$ has no solutions\n- The lines $x+3y=-1$ and $-x+ky=1$  intersect at exactly one point." :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =k+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $k+3\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -3.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the unique solution to  $Ax=0$ is the trivial one (zero).\n\n- Statement III is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186262856 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4af65db6-813d-59f3-a0e7-2fdd4a2a680e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186260518} {:answer/id #uuid "869eeb10-915c-53f0-8722-e594a9fe4255" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186261047} {:answer/id #uuid "c1758441-a00e-5e88-8098-1ee86374f298" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186261584} {:answer/id #uuid "c27ead3c-12bc-5368-b328-16fef0f4e445" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186261591} {:answer/id #uuid "fa58c139-591c-5b0f-a682-cd957a244838" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186262063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa58c139-591c-5b0f-a682-cd957a244838" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186262063} :answer-field/id #uuid "727b14d1-41af-5206-8144-a2695315bd92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186262316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98a38053-dfdc-5995-9d99-1db68e6c6765" :question/math-academy-id "q-127943" :question/problem "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$\n- ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the first part of the invertible matrix theorem, the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ must exist.\n\nFinally, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186262876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25bbb374-cd77-522e-b71b-84ac3a0970a4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186260205} {:answer/id #uuid "352a99d0-5e46-51fb-a66b-b28b810bbfcc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186260349} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb2229e-7778-59dc-84a2-22b08bd43f91" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186260701} {:answer/id #uuid "704abe56-59b3-539a-8b0b-dfc660097558" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186260861} {:answer/id #uuid "a9eeb765-7665-527e-aab8-5c657fd363d0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186261363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "704abe56-59b3-539a-8b0b-dfc660097558" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186260861} :answer-field/id #uuid "db542458-ecc5-56f1-93bb-b7ac180f87ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186262505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4f36ed6-3fc6-5f37-801d-3dafa17482c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-128124" :question/problem "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statements II and III are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186262877 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b104d3b-573a-5d16-aec6-dcf375b2c897" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186260654} {:answer/id #uuid "7115a909-500a-51cf-89a4-a0c3dbf2b1f7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186260866} {:answer/id #uuid "7f0893a2-c904-5b1a-9776-a5334d9fd885" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186260977} {:answer/id #uuid "8954d014-858e-5447-888d-6f0577f9bec2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186261071} {:answer/id #uuid "9d0d0b79-4064-540f-bc10-2ed4530a4611" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186261233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8954d014-858e-5447-888d-6f0577f9bec2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186261071} :answer-field/id #uuid "a96936a8-b59f-58ae-908b-74d177640816" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186262427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7887fe01-425a-54fe-9f35-ac58c1eba82c" :question/math-academy-id "q-128136" :question/problem "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 14 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne -4.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- The system  $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution\n- The lines $7x+14y=2$ and $-2x+ky=-3$ intersect at infinitely many points" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}7 & 14 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =7\\cdot k-(-2)\\cdot 14 \\\\  & =7k+28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $7k+28\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -4.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is the trivial one (zero).\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186262933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124475 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74f126da-5f3b-5af8-9aee-d6f0a4c61c49" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Shape of a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "026909dc-e9dd-517f-95bf-6823f8e3bc01" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186259892} {:answer/id #uuid "38140770-9691-5eb2-ad82-564a6f9d48cf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186260373} {:answer/id #uuid "48b9e9d1-a24c-55df-b4fb-a8d5b1fad5e5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186260505} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7319ce-b6b6-5eaa-8243-6ea4d88a73fa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186260665} {:answer/id #uuid "77e909ae-017b-58b1-aa6e-edb4e7831d28" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186260923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48b9e9d1-a24c-55df-b4fb-a8d5b1fad5e5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186260505} :answer-field/id #uuid "576917ef-8805-5f21-a670-caf9478ceb93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186262272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02aab097-5368-5094-843f-c0ac47beef5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-129058" :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+{y}^{2}}{x}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $c=-1$ gives a line\n\nII. $c=0$ gives a parabola, excluding the origin\n\nIII. $c=1$ gives the $x$-axis, excluding the origin" :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+{y}^{2}}{x} & =c \\\\ x+{y}^{2} & =cx \\\\ x(1-c)+{y}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=-1,$ the level curve is $x=-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2},$ which is a parabola that opens to the left (excluding the origin). So, I is false.\n\n- If $c=0,$ the level curves is $x=-{y}^{2},$ which is a parabola that opens to the left (excluding the origin). So, II is true.\n\n- If $c=1,$ the level curve is $y=0,$ which is the $x$-axis (excluding the origin). So, III is true.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186262935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124475 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74f126da-5f3b-5af8-9aee-d6f0a4c61c49" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Shape of a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21f38304-9a30-5c5f-8ebe-717c05f48ddc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186260168} {:answer/id #uuid "a73842c9-6b16-5ae5-ac63-12a00a5613b5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186261333} {:answer/id #uuid "a9703e01-992a-5a5c-b29e-e8394e3387aa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186261357} {:answer/id #uuid "d1768c59-3da5-56bc-abf9-f0e0ccae171c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186261722} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c8de9b-545c-5678-bc54-b80bb5221955" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186261878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1768c59-3da5-56bc-abf9-f0e0ccae171c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186261722} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1d3ad35-470f-551e-a1da-43e1172eb4b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186262544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42b1c0f3-5dd1-53b2-92c2-3218b1cbc331" :question/math-academy-id "q-129063" :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $c=-1$ gives a hyperbola\n- $c=0$ gives the $y$-axis, excluding the origin\n- $c=1$ gives the line $y=x,$ excluding the origin" :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} & =c \\\\ y & =cx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=-1,$ the level curve is the line $y=-x,$ excluding the origin. So, I is false.\n\n- If $c=0,$ the level curve is the line $y=0,$ which is the $x$-axis (excluding the origin). So, II is false.\n\n- If $c=1,$ the level curve is the line $y=x,$ excluding the origin. So, III is true.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186262999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1db38dcb-ebfc-5157-9004-84cdcdafd404" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186260129} {:answer/id #uuid "315b3cdc-c497-513f-8f8b-38de73605b2a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186260319} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c3bdf3-88b4-50c9-802e-026f7e4b351a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186261466} {:answer/id #uuid "c8d9dff4-12db-599f-a473-d8f1aa1fbff0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186261650} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8fe8e5-d7c2-5c76-8304-7bb64cd53611" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186262074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb8fe8e5-d7c2-5c76-8304-7bb64cd53611" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186262074} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab4841e2-a3d4-5ff6-8e58-5411a4dc36f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186262431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d967e86-9fda-51c5-b084-5eaf2a90df28" :question/math-academy-id "q-129239" :question/problem "Given the two planes $2x-5y+z+1=0$ and $3x-z+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes are parallel.\n- The planes intersect on a line." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $2x-5y+z+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-5,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x-z+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-5,1⟩\\times ⟨3,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =5i+5j+15k \\\\  & =⟨5,5,15⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186263000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00fe5981-0e36-5262-bfcb-d756c603a810" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186259880} {:answer/id #uuid "8bb21bd8-156f-5d7c-afec-10490c8d2a59" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186261095} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1b0500-2265-524b-92af-5974b43b21b7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186261409} {:answer/id #uuid "ca6c32c5-72fb-511e-820e-b8e512f790b2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186261662} {:answer/id #uuid "ea03ffce-f0fc-5cd6-94b3-3faf2a8e2f25" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186261927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00fe5981-0e36-5262-bfcb-d756c603a810" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186259880} :answer-field/id #uuid "93115b8c-64e5-5dea-84e2-5b0ca475114d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186262385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48d9b691-f927-556a-a0f4-054f943d7dcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-129240" :question/problem "Given the two planes $3x-y+2z=0$ and $9x-3y+6z+5=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n- The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n- The planes are coincident." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x-y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,-1,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $9x-3y+6z+5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨9,-3,6⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${n}_{2}=3{n}_{1}.$ So, the normal vectors are parallel.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. \n\nFor the two planes to coincide, the equation of one plane should be a scalar multiple of the other. However, if we try to manipulate the equation of the second plane to resemble the equation of the first plane, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x-3y+6z+5=0 \\\\ 3x-y+2z+\\frac{5}{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nClearly, this equation is not the same as the equation of the first plane. So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186263001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c55c92d-ec2e-5d8c-9396-852038e7910f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186260663} {:answer/id #uuid "69db1960-3550-5f95-977e-01b203b94327" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186260803} {:answer/id #uuid "8134cf56-1f24-5310-a062-babe48a5824d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186260999} {:answer/id #uuid "d98f6c4e-981a-548d-b812-20c865cfb008" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186261795} {:answer/id #uuid "f0dcf53a-a5df-542a-bb24-1b27bdbb250c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186261985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d98f6c4e-981a-548d-b812-20c865cfb008" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186261795} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ea43199-0044-5fd3-903c-6a01bdb815ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186262374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c9f9477f-0f39-55d8-91e7-cff41b6de7c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-129241" :question/problem "Given the two planes $x-3y+2z=0$ and $x-2y+5=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n\nII. The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n\nIII. The planes intersect on a line." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-3y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-3,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-2y+5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-2,1⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =4i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨4,2,1⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186263002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10a09fbe-515b-57cf-8dcf-59d4cf635ca0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186260027} {:answer/id #uuid "791d7e03-2398-5d57-a6f1-b2656a2c53dc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186260938} {:answer/id #uuid "b85f0f67-a1ad-5f66-89e8-4a14e682bd89" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186261492} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5c003d-ae68-5b29-95bb-beffa6bf343e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186261699} {:answer/id #uuid "db417898-4e38-5132-b7f5-64297340a226" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186261812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b85f0f67-a1ad-5f66-89e8-4a14e682bd89" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186261492} :answer-field/id #uuid "56a5d9fd-3df3-562c-b78d-8959c87ef9de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186262271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d75bf5e3-9255-56f7-a884-2df425d0b593" :question/math-academy-id "q-129258" :question/problem "Given the two planes $x-5y+2z=0$ and $3x-2y+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n\nII. The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n\nIII. The planes intersect on a line." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-5y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-5,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x-2y+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-5,2⟩\\times ⟨3,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =4i+6j+13k \\\\  & =⟨4,6,13⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186263003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29ac533f-924f-5d80-9ff3-699f8b13d36d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186260242} {:answer/id #uuid "555ab25a-6e89-5a85-a70d-d6ec1d66f479" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186260604} {:answer/id #uuid "5e1edb88-c2cc-5ebd-85a2-ac00d1d8098c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186260682} {:answer/id #uuid "78d311d3-d53f-53ff-b4c7-992aeda49486" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186260935} {:answer/id #uuid "859a958e-114f-5388-8a48-2208244529e3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186261034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e1edb88-c2cc-5ebd-85a2-ac00d1d8098c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186260682} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c30d9c2-1cc1-5def-8ddd-c6e34b51e133" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186262330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df6925db-161f-5f40-8f9d-0f308e52b4c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-129259" :question/problem "Given the two planes $2x-y+3z=0$ and $5x-2y+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨7,15,2⟩.$\n- The planes intersect on a line.\n- The planes are coincident." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $5x-2y+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\times ⟨5,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+15j+k \\\\  & =⟨6,15,1⟩\\ne ⟨7,15,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes cannot be coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186265703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "155a56d5-fa69-5283-8d38-fd2a417a14c5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186263199} {:answer/id #uuid "3181060a-65c8-5959-b773-0c34bed7fbcc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186263424} {:answer/id #uuid "7b808253-3379-5b12-bb25-b064404a5aec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186264068} {:answer/id #uuid "d6afc5a6-6c3e-5633-bd62-2dc494c7b3eb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186264867} {:answer/id #uuid "ec802259-8a68-5bac-9b04-d978ab8fcbcb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186265066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3181060a-65c8-5959-b773-0c34bed7fbcc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186263424} :answer-field/id #uuid "588004b0-40e3-5ab7-b5da-329cfb687081" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "999cfdc7-ea79-5a7b-bd15-dce6f8b9e282" :question/math-academy-id "q-129260" :question/problem "Given the two planes $3x+y-2z=0$ and $2x+y+3z+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨5,-13,1⟩.$\n- The planes are parallel.\n- The planes intersect on a line." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x+y-2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,1,-2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+y+3z+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,1,3⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,1,-2⟩\\times ⟨2,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =5i-13j+k \\\\  & =⟨5,-13,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the corresponding planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only I and III only are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186265704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f65dfcb-5864-5d50-af6c-6a4251ca9c68" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186263560} {:answer/id #uuid "86b04a7c-8b39-5e2c-be2e-f5fc7e8cb5f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186264161} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d3b82a-7794-57d3-b4eb-20dcf20c6612" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186264406} {:answer/id #uuid "b719d9a6-488b-529d-a34d-78457bdd81a5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186264587} {:answer/id #uuid "c1daba3d-adf5-57b9-a8c3-b934909958cf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186264683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4d3b82a-7794-57d3-b4eb-20dcf20c6612" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186264406} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a50ef2d-62ea-5f4c-b657-1b8e0a96f6ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d9644136-e70e-5412-95e4-ddf24de80c84" :question/math-academy-id "q-129261" :question/problem "Given the two planes $x-y+2z=0$ and $5x+y-13=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨-3,12,6⟩.$\n- The planes intersect on a line.\n- The planes are coincident." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-1,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $5x+y-13=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-1,2⟩\\times ⟨5,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-2i+10j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-2,10,6⟩\\ne ⟨-3,12,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the corresponding planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes could not be coincident either.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186265722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0016a292-9097-5977-930d-8d7887754513" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186263004} {:answer/id #uuid "05bc8728-06d0-5733-a118-83884c24d250" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186263052} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd7aa43-3f1d-58d1-9be4-8905661b1e62" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186263113} {:answer/id #uuid "312440d5-b9a1-5862-986f-fe92f4fdfbdd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186263419} {:answer/id #uuid "a3fbd8d5-590a-5259-a945-0b6d97ac2165" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186264394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bd7aa43-3f1d-58d1-9be4-8905661b1e62" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186263113} :answer-field/id #uuid "db3e568e-434f-51a1-820b-72bb367ab266" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88abe9a3-7bd6-5ff9-8f3e-e809f8b5b178" :question/math-academy-id "q-129406" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=0$\n- The limit evaluates to $\\frac{2}{3}$ along the path $y=x$\n- The limit does not exist" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2(0)(0)}{3({0}^{2})-{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2x(0)}{3({0}^{2})-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x(x)}{3{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186265723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0abede4b-aa82-5d1b-8fa1-c68e74f8e96c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186263103} {:answer/id #uuid "6cd36ab9-2bff-5a26-9311-657b79ae5fc7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186263937} {:answer/id #uuid "7906012f-95de-5e3b-80a0-4ee7922e4562" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id 17592186264049} {:answer/id #uuid "8c2fc7a7-dbf2-54ac-8264-740aa164acc9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186264197} {:answer/id #uuid "e68b75d7-d8f8-5bdc-8522-a4eab89e25e1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186265008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e68b75d7-d8f8-5bdc-8522-a4eab89e25e1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186265008} :answer-field/id #uuid "e92bfcbb-546d-55cb-8123-b6c869c5fdfe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7bd92abc-0899-50b8-83be-62e32b920d95" :question/math-academy-id "q-129408" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $4$ along the path $y={x}^{4}$\n- The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $x=0$\n- The limit does not exist" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2({0}^{2})\\sqrt{0}}{{0}^{4}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{4}}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}}}{{x}^{4}+({x}^{4})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{8}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}(1+{x}^{4})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2\\cancel{{x}^{4}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{4}}(1+{x}^{4})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+{0}^{4}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,x=0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{2({0}^{2})\\sqrt{y}}{{0}^{4}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186265727 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186100869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b494140f-206a-5317-8079-ff719a253140" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Matrices Are Conformable for Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c1c6c52-93de-5221-9a1a-e20de89b38bd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186263536} {:answer/id #uuid "63a83dec-8f37-53ab-a28c-c3fa6a30140d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186263862} {:answer/id #uuid "a3687bd6-0b22-503d-9c7e-4685c73a5a88" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186264388} {:answer/id #uuid "cd10bb14-ce8d-50bc-8a76-b367c9b2330b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186264794} {:answer/id #uuid "e5607bc1-aeb6-5235-a6bf-f2196aeeaee3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186264998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63a83dec-8f37-53ab-a28c-c3fa6a30140d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186263862} :answer-field/id #uuid "d89592b8-2db3-5901-80d6-bde3df02e0a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22b7c2e7-9621-5013-a411-a722a7ceea01" :question/math-academy-id "q-129437" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,S=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $P,R,$ and $S?$\n\n- The product $RS$ is well defined\n- The product $RP$ is well defined\n- The product $SP$ is well defined" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $P$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix, $R$ is a $1\\times 3$ row vector, and $S$ is a $1\\times 5$ row vector. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $R$ and $1$ row in $S.$ Since the number of columns in $R$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $S,$ the matrices $R$ and $S$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{R}_{1\\times 3}{\\times }_{\\ne }{S}_{1\\times 5}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $3$ columns in $R$ and $5$ rows in $P.$ Since the number of columns in $R$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $P,$ the matrices $R$ and $P$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{R}_{1\\times 3}{\\times }_{\\ne }{P}_{5\\times 3}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $5$ columns in $S$ and $5$ rows in $P.$ Since the number of columns in $S$ equals the number of rows in $P,$ the matrices $S$ and $P$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{S}_{1\\times \\boxed{\\text{5}}}{\\times }_{✓}{P}_{\\boxed{\\text{5}}\\times 3}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statement III is true, while statements I and II are false."}] [{:db/id 17592186265893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119931 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6a3d254-c96f-5aa0-9db6-a587eb7cb2de" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression Involving Given Determinants and Matrix Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1915d697-74d2-5ba7-ab3c-924dda066fdb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186263239} {:answer/id #uuid "71941411-32be-5902-a121-3109163ca332" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186263983} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2454e9-c2c9-5258-a3a5-771056629c63" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186264612} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a04c0e-d0be-5019-85eb-78169493412f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186264693} {:answer/id #uuid "dc55b5d1-afcf-5dbb-8c95-bacee4ab6ce2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186264919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba2454e9-c2c9-5258-a3a5-771056629c63" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186264612} :answer-field/id #uuid "b26385bd-4fdc-5602-9d9e-060ee8c21ec7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dcce1957-fa55-5f89-a722-cbd09d149dbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-130364" :question/problem "Given that $det({A}^{T})=7$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $3\\text{det}(A)=21$\n- $-2\\text{det}({A}^{T})=14$\n- $\\text{det}(A)+2\\text{det}(I)=9$" :question/worked-solution "From the properties of determinants, we know that \n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)=\\text{det}({A}^{T})=7\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ndet(I)=1.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n3\\text{det}(A)=3\\cdot 7=21.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n-2\\text{det}({A}^{T})=-2\\cdot 7=-14\\ne 14.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)+2\\text{det}(I)=7+2\\cdot 1=9.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186265894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119931 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6a3d254-c96f-5aa0-9db6-a587eb7cb2de" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression Involving Given Determinants and Matrix Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "443474d4-e054-5bee-8c3c-9c70e1d7b4da" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186263600} {:answer/id #uuid "56b6e95b-5228-5e26-bec6-243abaf01f8b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186263749} {:answer/id #uuid "6cce03c4-47df-5829-b260-0ebd1658ce63" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186263936} {:answer/id #uuid "b545427e-1243-5eea-ac37-96e079e5e06d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186264565} {:answer/id #uuid "c17a5dd6-1f7f-5a9c-84c0-38b265cd57ab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186264679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cce03c4-47df-5829-b260-0ebd1658ce63" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186263936} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa8bd260-f4fe-5407-957e-a361e80bd3f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a27b85a3-48a0-5d82-bd5c-966a92360669" :question/math-academy-id "q-130366" :question/problem "Given that $det(A)=5$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{det}({A}^{T})=\\frac{1}{5}$\n- $5\\text{det}({A}^{T})=1$\n- $\\text{det}({A}^{T})-3\\text{det}(I)=2$" :question/worked-solution "From the properties of determinants, we know that\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)=\\text{det}({A}^{T})=5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ndet(I)=1.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have,\n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})=5\\ne \\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n5\\text{det}({A}^{T})=5\\cdot 5=25\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})-3\\text{det}(I)=5-3\\cdot 1=2.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186265906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115228 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a870c4a-daa5-5df2-8968-d8bf9ac0bd75" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Matrix Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "115ea0de-16e4-597e-8ae6-ee2e62042894" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186263158} {:answer/id #uuid "1704e901-00d1-5bcf-ab37-f26603f2d84a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186263216} {:answer/id #uuid "56e15d30-dcec-506e-9489-929d13785c31" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186263751} {:answer/id #uuid "b487f697-fef5-5f0e-a2e0-03d016cb2188" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186264550} {:answer/id #uuid "fc527037-edc5-5234-a514-ce08dd3a7352" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186265216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b487f697-fef5-5f0e-a2e0-03d016cb2188" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186264550} :answer-field/id #uuid "dff5ca4f-0a81-576e-920b-a26abea2aefe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8250ce52-a1e9-536b-9512-ee968aac3cef" :question/math-academy-id "q-130446" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $4\\times 4$ matrices which of the following statements are always true? \n\n- ${A}^{2}B=A(AB)$\n- $A(B-C)=AB-CA$\n- $B(CA)=(CB)A$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is associative and  ${A}^{2}B=AAB=A(AB)$ is always true.\n\n- Statement II is false.  Matrix multiplication is distributive and  $A(B-C)=AB-AC.$ However, matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AC\\ne CA$ in general and therefore $A(B-C)\\ne AB-CA$, in general.\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is associative and  $B(CA)=(BC)A.$ However, matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $BC\\ne CB$ in general and therefore $B(CA)\\ne (CB)A$, in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement  I is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186265907 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115228 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a870c4a-daa5-5df2-8968-d8bf9ac0bd75" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Matrix Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2150ae64-493e-56d5-b2d0-84693c4610a2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186263299} {:answer/id #uuid "34358467-0a82-5372-8499-e4a4a76191a1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186263455} {:answer/id #uuid "4f1dd1de-ef71-553f-9af5-3b4768178ed3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186263688} {:answer/id #uuid "5b3ccdd5-22ed-5b56-8a8b-05edeb3419aa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186263787} {:answer/id #uuid "9befb232-207c-597f-bf1c-35929af7ae56" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186264334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b3ccdd5-22ed-5b56-8a8b-05edeb3419aa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186263787} :answer-field/id #uuid "24a2ab62-6961-5a10-9ad4-2e74750a2b90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a216ad8-b070-56d6-a23a-d32e7322b41f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130449" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $3\\times 3$ matrices, and  ${I}_{3}$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix, which of the following statements are always true?\n\n- $AB=A{I}_{3}B$\n- $A(B+{I}_{3})=AB$\n- ${I}_{3}A=A{I}_{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $A{I}_{3}B=(A{I}_{3})B=AB.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B+{I}_{3})=AB+A{I}_{3}=AB+A.$ However,  $AB+A\\ne AB,$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is not commutative in general, however, multiplication by the identity matrix is always commutative. In this case, we have  ${I}_{3}A=A{I}_{3}.$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186265909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115228 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a870c4a-daa5-5df2-8968-d8bf9ac0bd75" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Matrix Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fbfd4c4-068a-5868-bc97-1329c0713938" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186263143} {:answer/id #uuid "6643c090-5848-523b-8705-add98d1a39e8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186263883} {:answer/id #uuid "b6fe2291-2804-5ef7-b520-dbedc179c766" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186264586} {:answer/id #uuid "ed8fb8cb-7920-5aae-88ae-630ed4a34532" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186265075} {:answer/id #uuid "ee0bccfd-146e-59bb-9ce6-0631931c2bc2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186265079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fbfd4c4-068a-5868-bc97-1329c0713938" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186263143} :answer-field/id #uuid "b22fab6b-24d8-51e9-b884-f29b98fe1103" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da216097-76ab-5a2e-b3ad-662534506edb" :question/math-academy-id "q-130455" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $6\\times 6$ matrices which of the following statements are always true? \n\nI. $A(BC)=(AB)C$\n\nII. $A(B-C)=AB-CA$\n\nIII. $CA=AC$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is associative and  $A(BC)=(AB)C$ is always true.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B-C)=AB-AC,$ but $AC\\ne CA$ because matrix multiplication is not commutative in general.\n\n- Statement III  is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $CA\\ne AC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement  I is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186265913 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115228 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a870c4a-daa5-5df2-8968-d8bf9ac0bd75" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Matrix Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e5ac890-22fc-54f7-9607-9a95a2e231fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186263279} {:answer/id #uuid "3635b958-5415-5817-8373-ca8488751022" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186263481} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0f02c6-7626-510a-bad2-6cbd7c7a714e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186263645} {:answer/id #uuid "9066ab79-6b99-5d64-9d4a-2779f6ce1336" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186264240} {:answer/id #uuid "ef1630d2-e1e5-5b71-ac8f-a1da74a6c640" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186265095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b0f02c6-7626-510a-bad2-6cbd7c7a714e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186263645} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dafd0ae-8912-5698-9e33-d0db11481517" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c886d4f7-2a2b-5730-a24b-3976b9e31d35" :question/math-academy-id "q-130463" :question/problem "Given that $A$ and $B$  are $8\\times 8$ matrices,  and ${I}_{8}$ is the $8\\times 8$ identity matrix,  which of the following statements are always true?\n\n- $A{I}_{8}={I}_{8}A$\n- $AB=BA$\n- $A\\cdot 2{I}_{8}=2A$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is not commutative in general, however, multiplication by the identity matrix is always commutative. In this case, we have  $A{I}_{8}={I}_{8}A=A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot 2{I}_{8} & =A({I}_{8}+{I}_{8}) \\\\  & =A{I}_{8}+A{I}_{8} \\\\  & =A+A \\\\  & =2A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statements I  and III are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186265914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115228 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a870c4a-daa5-5df2-8968-d8bf9ac0bd75" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Matrix Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06d03d9e-bf84-54fe-8269-06c7d1eb34d0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186263061} {:answer/id #uuid "455f6e3e-27ef-5b24-b87b-4e6464fd85b7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186263610} {:answer/id #uuid "c0525f23-d0f6-5b07-b487-58264dbf4bcb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186264674} {:answer/id #uuid "c3793162-b21b-5b4e-a89c-88eb8c204290" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186264706} {:answer/id #uuid "fff628a0-94b8-5551-93cd-77bece44c081" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186265252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3793162-b21b-5b4e-a89c-88eb8c204290" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186264706} :answer-field/id #uuid "811f169e-f057-5602-aa83-3f3dff47bbdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ca328f5c-fde3-5a0e-88ed-9bc8ff6b0334" :question/math-academy-id "q-130478" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $7\\times 7$ matrices which of the following statements are always true? \n\n- $(CA)B=C(BA)$\n- $(A+A)B=2AB$\n- $CB=BC$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I  is false. Matrix multiplication is associative and  $(CA)B=C(AB)$ is always true. However,  matrix multiplication is not commutative,   $AB\\ne BA$ in general. Therefore, $(CA)B\\ne C(BA)$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+A)B=AB+AB=2AB$ is always true.\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative in general, so $CB\\ne BC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186268906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0611905f-8dcd-582c-bda8-2bb4d3022010" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186266197} {:answer/id #uuid "15dd3a1d-b931-553f-a798-779e9586dd0d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186266327} {:answer/id #uuid "459e124e-4436-52f6-b779-e1f3223f7b2c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186266765} {:answer/id #uuid "cce41217-38eb-5c92-8bfb-dc475c6ba1ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186267977} {:answer/id #uuid "e597a3d4-68b3-5202-88f2-a50428b0a480" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186268175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cce41217-38eb-5c92-8bfb-dc475c6ba1ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186267977} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b178f3f-36ed-50d2-8f73-29434cf588a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186268427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72f1f14f-947a-5dd6-902d-418c94ea31fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-132675" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)?$\n\n- The partial derivative with respect to $y$ is defined at $(-1,-1,0)$\n- ${f}_{x}(0,1,1)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{z}(1,-1,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =z\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{x} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =z\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{y} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(z) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(-1,-1,0)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(-1,-1,0)=0\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡((-1)\\cdot (-1))=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(-1,-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,-1,0) & =\\frac{0}{(-1)}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,1,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,1,1)=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement III is also false. If we substitute $(1,-1,1)$ into ${f}_{z}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{z}(1,-1,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot (-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186268907 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25ba8136-3f97-5995-83e3-95432551c0d1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186266481} {:answer/id #uuid "a1cedf13-a8c5-5f74-9ead-309286311701" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186267599} {:answer/id #uuid "a2810b5e-9362-516a-94d2-1ba39e99e36b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186267605} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4bcb4b-ab4b-5be4-b1d4-d1183b7511c4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186267681} {:answer/id #uuid "cfeb3149-070f-5012-8119-2a98c9b1323e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186268002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1cedf13-a8c5-5f74-9ead-309286311701" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186267599} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb59d576-4495-5a01-a6f2-41656c1c4ed5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186268738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9dc50a8a-45d6-510c-8209-df3404b44621" :question/math-academy-id "q-132680" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}?$\n\nI. Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(4,2)$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(0,2)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{y}(0,2)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(4,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(4,2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{4}}{{2}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(4,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(4,2) & =\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})\\sqrt{4}}=\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ {f}_{y}(4,2) & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{4}}{{2}^{3}}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,2)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,2)=\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})\\sqrt{0}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(0,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{0}}{{2}^{2}}=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(0,2)=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{0}}{{2}^{3}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186268914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "313ed8a6-5624-51e9-8247-921676b60bf0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186266577} {:answer/id #uuid "409a59c0-29a1-5253-ba63-e612221ceedc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186266729} {:answer/id #uuid "635f6241-0e3a-5ddf-84d9-85447f3da39e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186267018} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e2402e-0272-58c9-af19-86fc33ef5471" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186268033} {:answer/id #uuid "e54c682f-12fe-57b4-a4b2-763baf12853b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186268168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "409a59c0-29a1-5253-ba63-e612221ceedc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186266729} :answer-field/id #uuid "61fa50ca-fd4a-5d40-a9dd-ff3a34de4409" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186268584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a68d3690-fbcf-53d1-acbf-a5134efed5cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-132728" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2)?$\n\nI. Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(0,2)$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(1,0)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{y}(1,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=0 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(2)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is also false. If we substitute $(1,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2}+{1}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186269218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162008 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6289ad34-2393-5f5f-b7f0-a1ab1a3916f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Quadratic Form Types Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3043245e-f89a-5a82-8c99-dcba6fad79da" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186266571} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ac5e5e-ab33-5c84-8176-ea249d51783b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186267880} {:answer/id #uuid "d44f83d7-dd65-5e3a-80a3-4ca47490587e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186268038} {:answer/id #uuid "e15c22e4-8112-57b0-bf64-4ce52415ebf2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186268135} {:answer/id #uuid "f245aae7-e681-5e8e-9fed-62996b00217d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186268297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2ac5e5e-ab33-5c84-8176-ea249d51783b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186267880} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ebfddea-dc7a-5e03-8c08-1c276b2e1c2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186268433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "44381bf9-c027-58df-ba36-2d3bed72d50c" :question/math-academy-id "q-139436" :question/problem "Which of the following statements regarding quadratic forms are true?\n\n- $R(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}$ is indefinite\n- $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]x$ is negative-definite\n- $T(x)={x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ is positive-definite" :question/worked-solution "Recall that, a quadratic form $Q(x)$ is:\n\n- positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- indefinite if \n\n- $Q({x}_{1})>0$ for some ${x}_{1}\\in {R}^{n},$ and\n\n- $Q({x}_{2})<0$ for some ${x}_{2}\\in {R}^{n}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$ So, the quadratic form is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0},$ the quadratic form is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Substituting ${x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({x}_{1}) & =1\\cdot 1=1>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting ${x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({x}_{2}) & =1\\cdot (-1)=-1<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic form is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186269222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162009 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4105f1cb-eb48-5946-92f7-cd5e67689912" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type by Calculating the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ff068f7-6a37-50be-9726-09410423546d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186266277} {:answer/id #uuid "26b8d67d-a172-5d66-a98c-3f3f68eacea8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186266490} {:answer/id #uuid "94afd7f1-eafb-5978-b0ae-80d939af6725" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186267473} {:answer/id #uuid "a43d55a6-29c9-578d-89ba-6433c5c13c81" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186267624} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f20b4c-f2f0-5c81-8f55-6dd1cd22c154" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186267958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a43d55a6-29c9-578d-89ba-6433c5c13c81" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186267624} :answer-field/id #uuid "225ef32b-a5c1-5cfa-a89c-b365b093db6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186268462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e96bcaa6-6221-5f7c-9daa-4047225c2c05" :question/math-academy-id "q-139457" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is indefinite\n- $A$ is negative semi-definite" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, by computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-(4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since both eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is positive semi-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186269224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162009 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4105f1cb-eb48-5946-92f7-cd5e67689912" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type by Calculating the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04a724ad-b3ee-52d8-82e0-4bd377a9b21f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186266185} {:answer/id #uuid "06ef6c4e-b125-5fac-9b05-83fb3a606423" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186266204} {:answer/id #uuid "ac89844d-dabe-589b-b3a5-2ad45b9db935" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186267699} {:answer/id #uuid "c8369794-da46-5cd4-8db6-6a2727a24fd4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186267938} {:answer/id #uuid "daff401f-b2e2-5216-87b0-25786b96565a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186268083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac89844d-dabe-589b-b3a5-2ad45b9db935" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186267699} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6375020-1f93-58af-8c70-b31286f0c56d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186268812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36a88a67-992a-5e00-85ac-8bb48fc32f6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-139460" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive semi-definite\n- $A$ is indefinite" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186269225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162010 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbebdd1b-0716-55cb-8df6-12a35b517ab5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "477117b1-275d-51b1-ae75-dc88272a3b8e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186266783} {:answer/id #uuid "56d0e845-743b-59b1-8aff-239bd010506a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186266915} {:answer/id #uuid "939c8127-4667-55db-b69c-59192fc70100" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186267465} {:answer/id #uuid "b9fe6102-69cc-5971-b635-bc6ead18dd05" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186267811} {:answer/id #uuid "c6ccb7bb-934a-5010-b596-f2de092b095d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186267926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9fe6102-69cc-5971-b635-bc6ead18dd05" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186267811} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b4ced4d-5942-5066-9d9b-d7987531d699" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186268569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "906865b3-32d2-5d7f-b7fc-d38ff3d60bed" :question/math-academy-id "q-139462" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=2$ and $det(A)=-3$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=2$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-3.\n$$\n\n- Statements II and  III are false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=2>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, neither the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ nor the matrix $A$ is positive-definite or negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186271986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124509 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f020b27f-1cd4-550b-a83a-2afbb7e5db72" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Dimension of the Null Space of Matrices Using Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01a42ad2-59e7-50a1-bc17-a696d5f174af" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186269276} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba6f8af-006d-5b88-8471-165345877a19" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186269813} {:answer/id #uuid "9674979b-b692-5fd8-8eb7-292d37f102e9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270591} {:answer/id #uuid "af3bbfd8-56fc-56de-a1d4-08d3b1632864" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270828} {:answer/id #uuid "e425b23f-6e42-5a6d-8294-6490bf9bc2a3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186271280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01a42ad2-59e7-50a1-bc17-a696d5f174af" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186269276} :answer-field/id #uuid "4afe83c7-6296-5f61-a86a-21690a8ae43c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c75ae37f-79fb-5023-b676-a4acf7953ba3" :question/math-academy-id "q-139973" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2.$\n\nII. If $B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\,0\\end{aligned}]$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(B))=1.$\n\nIII. If $C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(C))=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The dimension of the null space of a matrix is the number of non-pivot columns in the matrix. \n\nAlso, all of the given matrices are in row echelon form.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The matrix $A$ has $2$ pivot columns and no non-pivot columns. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The matrix $B$ has $2$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $3$rd column). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(B))=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The matrix $C$ has $3$ pivot columns and no non-pivot columns. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(C))=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "285f74e5-9973-5406-a292-4b6bf8d6c46e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186269631} {:answer/id #uuid "62bafafa-d701-54aa-ae9b-1d2ea1ec3663" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270140} {:answer/id #uuid "879b5bd5-af15-5abf-bbae-a66ab655f9a9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186270442} {:answer/id #uuid "90eb7ee8-741b-506b-9d1e-503fcedbcbdf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270527} {:answer/id #uuid "e8cb67e4-5059-5a96-95b4-8bf8fd1c8d00" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186271302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8cb67e4-5059-5a96-95b4-8bf8fd1c8d00" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186271302} :answer-field/id #uuid "71f3d8b4-b78e-51cb-88eb-2ff86a59b5d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "992aa4b0-2c51-5ac6-900d-30c5c0b14be1" :question/math-academy-id "q-140079" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{7}\\to {R}^{7}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})$ for some distinct vectors  ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}?$ \n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- $T$ is invertible\n- $\\text{rank}(T)=7$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{7}$ into ${R}^{7},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=7 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129213 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "434a82c2-7964-525a-85b9-eb17fa7b5b26" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Connections Between Matrices of a Linear Transformation Relative to Different Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09f81da2-1a75-5f55-8d79-8c2274041ce5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186269358} {:answer/id #uuid "66275c26-ff38-5753-9c00-3c8ab5e52584" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270177} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9a1869-b9bb-5e1a-86a3-a0b65d7624de" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270801} {:answer/id #uuid "b1aef7d0-44ca-54af-bea9-b756d13ca4e1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186270860} {:answer/id #uuid "e4c16bc5-32d2-569b-946e-08ac6935f2fd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186271286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66275c26-ff38-5753-9c00-3c8ab5e52584" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270177} :answer-field/id #uuid "3304b220-96b4-54f2-aaaf-0f382e67ec76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf5001e6-d49e-526a-bbd2-b37f328e04e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-140361" :question/problem "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $B$ to basis $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$\n- $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$\n- $[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129213 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "434a82c2-7964-525a-85b9-eb17fa7b5b26" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Connections Between Matrices of a Linear Transformation Relative to Different Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c42a1c1-86fe-59bc-9f0a-3e1bd222c784" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186269502} {:answer/id #uuid "48c52bab-433b-55d3-b20b-830726dde6f8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186269934} {:answer/id #uuid "b96f8a18-200d-579a-a147-0023f2922dab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186270906} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1de8a8-2fd8-5db0-9470-db2fec46edbb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186271077} {:answer/id #uuid "d8cc3afe-be4c-5e2f-83f2-6d76c7314bd4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186271172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b96f8a18-200d-579a-a147-0023f2922dab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186270906} :answer-field/id #uuid "29078bb2-cd84-54b6-9b00-8b8a30600335" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc68e9b9-f221-584c-883c-3c892a06e266" :question/math-academy-id "q-140362" :question/problem "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $B$ to basis $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$\n- $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$\n- $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124571 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef311af0-0e95-5574-a151-2971ad66bd08" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Eigenvalues of a 4x4 Matrix Containing a Block of Zeros"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "025bf3dc-65a2-5980-9de5-504713e44ac7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186269290} {:answer/id #uuid "6965124d-345b-50d1-85a1-e0688b3be84e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186270203} {:answer/id #uuid "77bcfcf4-a25d-5a49-946d-144dcf244a05" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186270330} {:answer/id #uuid "85c3df0c-47d2-59a5-8213-c08edc670bff" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270426} {:answer/id #uuid "a2695c9b-1195-5c04-ad47-6a93a769893e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85c3df0c-47d2-59a5-8213-c08edc670bff" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270426} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d987ec8-4c29-5fef-b7be-dd652491072d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a042e23b-9a4b-5f08-a06a-17d78f4dd358" :question/math-academy-id "q-140450" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -4 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n- $\\lambda =-4$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n- The eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,P-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nP-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 2 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda  & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -4-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(P-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 2 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda  & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -4-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 0 & 7 \\\\ -3 & -4-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5-\\lambda )\\cdot (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -3 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)(\\lambda -2)\\lambda (\\lambda +4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $P$ are $\\lambda =5,$ $\\lambda =2,$ $\\lambda =0,$ and $\\lambda =-4$  (all with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e81030a-0151-5dea-b9bc-74aae2499482" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Orthogonal Projection Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15d1ff54-798c-5495-ac77-7c70131ecd65" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186269455} {:answer/id #uuid "699d0687-452d-5bb8-8095-db807468d7c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186270205} {:answer/id #uuid "8e661237-401b-59f7-9700-6fd117cb0fe8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186270502} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e2da0a-9db2-5571-a16f-ea09cd190f8d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270979} {:answer/id #uuid "eecf636f-9554-5ca1-92d5-73c878cfccc5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186271367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0e2da0a-9db2-5571-a16f-ea09cd190f8d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270979} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff75cce5-5510-50fb-bc04-9b62533591ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6043910d-249c-5dcb-8998-243aef51387f" :question/math-academy-id "q-140516" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${A}^{T}=A$\n- ${A}^{2}=A$\n- $A$ is an orthogonal projection matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $Q$ is an orthogonal projection matrix if and only if ${Q}^{2}=Q$ and ${Q}^{T}=Q.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since ${A}^{T}\\ne A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since not both statements I and II are true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0562db51-66a7-5a18-a492-af715264f543" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186269315} {:answer/id #uuid "71005342-d55f-5332-b578-95e6f906faab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270273} {:answer/id #uuid "a0c3fac8-64b6-560e-a9cd-252aed9b1291" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270694} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d033de-73ce-5f72-b75d-36907f8d890e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186271275} {:answer/id #uuid "f223e07f-474f-53ea-9772-c00afbe6a869" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186271394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0c3fac8-64b6-560e-a9cd-252aed9b1291" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270694} :answer-field/id #uuid "7165e98a-3520-5bd3-93ee-a5107bf92b82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1e4b04d4-e7d3-5042-809a-2a3512a7a902" :question/math-academy-id "q-140556" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=2(t-1),\\,{f}_{2}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{f}_{3}(t)={e}^{(t-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,2]$ of continuous functions over $[0,2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨2(t-1),{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2(t-1)({e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2(t-1) & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=2, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2(t-1){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{0}^{2}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2(t-1){e}^{t}){|}_{0}^{2}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2{e}^{2}-(-2))-2{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}+2-(2{e}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =4\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2(t-1),{e}^{(t-1)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2(t-1){e}^{(t-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={e}^{(t-1)^{2}}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =e-e \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09787070-5733-5c1f-945a-b8fade9da8b3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186269355} {:answer/id #uuid "4ba01b4f-8964-54bd-b8f1-feba4517a205" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186269949} {:answer/id #uuid "94ab7e7b-5ee0-5fef-a0ba-ef59e46d7fb9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186270572} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb5dee7-98f6-5027-ad6e-e7a62c557037" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186270674} {:answer/id #uuid "f420726b-eeff-586b-8e0a-647d2d964209" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186271409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09787070-5733-5c1f-945a-b8fade9da8b3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186269355} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dfbd69f-3a9f-5022-b832-bc4aefd85166" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1812a9f8-6cf3-5d8f-b5c7-1adf1d688a91" :question/math-academy-id "q-140835" :question/problem "A matrix $A$ has the characteristic equation $2{\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-4=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- $A$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =2$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ 2(2)^{3}-3(2)^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ 16-12-4 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the characteristic equation, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02780196-fa75-5062-a6b7-0cc51285054c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186269291} {:answer/id #uuid "06658ca9-e22b-55a3-8fe2-e2d7a44c4438" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186269326} {:answer/id #uuid "8f2e262b-113e-5bd8-aeda-f355760ebb0b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186270516} {:answer/id #uuid "9616fe83-1849-50bd-9922-51207d18a239" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186270588} {:answer/id #uuid "c5def0e8-1b57-557b-b93f-16d8d092b9bc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186271014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02780196-fa75-5062-a6b7-0cc51285054c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186269291} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e003ad9-af8d-5f1c-83de-8cc274501c50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47d1848e-d69a-5675-a8fe-fca5e30be264" :question/math-academy-id "q-140838" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =-4$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(A+4I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- $\\text{Col}(A+4I)={R}^{2}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(A+4I)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A+4I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =4$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A+4I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A+4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $M=A+4I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A+4I)\\ne {R}^{2},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+4I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124529 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75c45048-6da9-5e66-9999-d55d942f1986" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186270309} {:answer/id #uuid "8be0f024-03a9-5fcb-8b70-e4bd7fa12b77" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270481} {:answer/id #uuid "9c33154b-55e0-5408-a9c3-b312471c1b83" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186270647} {:answer/id #uuid "aab925ad-4045-522f-8476-b618a445ccbe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270795} {:answer/id #uuid "e4736cff-c2ee-574d-b818-12f2036f2384" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186271282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4736cff-c2ee-574d-b818-12f2036f2384" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186271282} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4e537fe-7191-5ea1-8bce-61de982dbea3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f77ae0b2-0cbc-5c99-a8dc-82a79dae8fdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-140840" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(A-7I)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A-7I$ is singular\n- $\\text{nullity}(A-7I)=0$\n- $\\lambda =7$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $2$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=2.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A-7I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A-7I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(A-7I) & =2-\\text{rank}(A-7I) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=A-7I$ is singular, then $(A-7I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =7$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124529 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37c3ab4a-d011-567e-a4bb-8e9d36570202" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186269773} {:answer/id #uuid "486195e9-68e7-52bc-a0d1-cda7e04826cb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186269928} {:answer/id #uuid "7623ec57-6450-5b90-acba-bcedc4817b39" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270312} {:answer/id #uuid "9df4d4f0-b93f-5520-888d-09db2b51a7f3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186270668} {:answer/id #uuid "b9dd595d-5db6-5f4c-8302-dae9d8ff3b2b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186270911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7623ec57-6450-5b90-acba-bcedc4817b39" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270312} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c037997-12d3-5b7c-aa0f-1a879bb19a43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "78a8d2b4-93c9-5ecc-ada3-6436c09c3461" :question/math-academy-id "q-140842" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A+8I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A+8I$ is singular\n- $\\lambda =-8$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- $\\text{rank}(A+8I)=2$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A+8I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A+8I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that if $M=A+8I$ is singular, then $(A+8I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =-8$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A+8I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(A+8I) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186184220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc36f534-6b27-5619-a29e-9954d0310adb" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity of Two-Piece Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11cec077-c70f-523b-8da0-293ef503e9bc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186269426} {:answer/id #uuid "4acd5f49-665e-5cd3-8d90-5a84ccec631f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186269944} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb28856-23da-5eab-9761-5cc5a1fa6401" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270822} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6e7269-21de-58a6-9e79-d467bce4e42d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186271199} {:answer/id #uuid "e9d79f8c-1051-5516-9d4e-f14a14271dc5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186271318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4acd5f49-665e-5cd3-8d90-5a84ccec631f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186269944} :answer-field/id #uuid "c57c2e77-1d97-5e77-a7c0-cee20a1d0d4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3fd6961-1be3-5be2-829e-70725d6ca86c" :question/math-academy-id "q-141001" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2},\\, & x\\le 1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $f(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\nLet's start by computing $f(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=1$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & =f(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=1$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & =f(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=1,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186184220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc36f534-6b27-5619-a29e-9954d0310adb" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity of Two-Piece Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bb9afd2-a23b-5213-8b6f-0774be368ae6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186270084} {:answer/id #uuid "6dcf50eb-9b14-5609-9e07-45ac00f5a323" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270246} {:answer/id #uuid "85788f2a-421f-595a-a301-4a9f34043c84" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186270423} {:answer/id #uuid "9a42944f-c4d0-5902-b7e6-420fbb0680cf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186270626} {:answer/id #uuid "de31b10c-345d-5d28-af4f-62601a087cbb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186271218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85788f2a-421f-595a-a301-4a9f34043c84" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186270423} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe3cb052-ae4e-5b7b-89f7-2a9670a41e56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce7bb2c8-a5d2-5cd9-93b3-f572c13f436f" :question/math-academy-id "q-141002" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $h(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\nh(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-27,\\, & x<3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2),\\, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=3$\n- $h(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=3$\n- $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $h(x)$ at $x=3.$\n\nLet's start by computing $h(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(3) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=3$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}h(x) & ={3}^{3}-27 \\\\  & =27-27 \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & =h(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=3$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}h(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & =h(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=3,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aad624f-4b38-5923-afbd-1579952dbf84" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186269364} {:answer/id #uuid "3b956d8b-d0de-5dcf-817b-4188dc3372de" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186269811} {:answer/id #uuid "591adc53-138b-519d-9fb5-74c10f838635" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186270055} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f1c832-b011-546e-af5d-c70d12222e25" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186271386} {:answer/id #uuid "fdaa9cbc-8e1e-5488-8e27-7caa05c408ff" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186271489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b956d8b-d0de-5dcf-817b-4188dc3372de" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186269811} :answer-field/id #uuid "85a148f5-fd30-5518-8877-e3dc571076f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "525bde81-7550-5f13-8e28-5c16a04ee9f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-141159" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩=⟨b,a⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in R$\n- $⟨a,a⟩>0$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement II is true. For any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\overset{―}{\\alpha }=\\alpha .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $a=0,$ then $⟨a,a⟩=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e483cc2-b46f-5512-a9a7-0f10ea8a953d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186269394} {:answer/id #uuid "4288a7d7-b334-57bd-981d-9101054485a0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186269869} {:answer/id #uuid "4bf5a7d9-2cd0-5edb-8159-5892ad0bc218" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186269954} {:answer/id #uuid "6d857f4b-9335-543f-9a26-45ba2569561b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270242} {:answer/id #uuid "cf364781-4d57-5ea3-a715-fb67ec1553ec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186271085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d857f4b-9335-543f-9a26-45ba2569561b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270242} :answer-field/id #uuid "672798eb-6f95-52b3-882f-b5bf772f3083" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "396a82c3-ba69-5d62-8d39-94fffd303517" :question/math-academy-id "q-141160" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $⟨ia,b⟩=i⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n\nII. $⟨a,\\overset{―}{\\alpha }b⟩=⟨\\alpha a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n\nIII. $⟨ia,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true.  Indeed, the inner product in ${C}^{n}$ is linear in the first argument. So, a constant multiple from the first argument can be factored out.\n\n- Statement II is true. By the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument and linearity in the first argument, we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,\\overset{―}{\\alpha }b⟩=\\overset{―}{(\\overset{―}{\\alpha })}⟨a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩=⟨\\alpha a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $⟨a,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}.$ Therefore, if $a\\ne 0,$ then\n\n$$\n⟨ia,a⟩=i⟨a,a⟩\\notin R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124511 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74ef5d4c-f33d-597d-a2fa-aa2bd980b69e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270302} {:answer/id #uuid "91213c6e-a951-5f10-ad74-2238bac4f2f0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270532} {:answer/id #uuid "945a196e-773a-511b-a5a8-a31f4dcb23f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186270565} {:answer/id #uuid "956627f2-fd1b-55e8-b3da-f529dc0dbe2e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186270581} {:answer/id #uuid "eafe7eb0-f4b1-5c2e-a83b-f7f47ae1704d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186271328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eafe7eb0-f4b1-5c2e-a83b-f7f47ae1704d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186271328} :answer-field/id #uuid "31dc903f-41ad-5ad5-b457-03d1579fa938" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f63719f4-7a1e-5b43-8302-7786de4d0619" :question/math-academy-id "q-141199" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is a solution of the equation $Ax=0?$\n\n- $\\text{Null}(A)={0}$\n- $A$ is not invertible\n- $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Since $v\\ne 0$ and $Av=0,$ \nthen $v\\in \\text{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne {0},$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124511 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "188d8c1d-5896-5f10-81c9-bd96ad0517aa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186269475} {:answer/id #uuid "52e6f0c7-0a62-59e3-a5cb-80e9b7f54638" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186270005} {:answer/id #uuid "54208619-14dc-516d-b1aa-20ed09a0bb47" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186270018} {:answer/id #uuid "77353fd7-ee88-5664-aec8-e00589b061d8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186270324} {:answer/id #uuid "af2612fc-39ba-5012-92ba-66032676ab46" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af2612fc-39ba-5012-92ba-66032676ab46" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270827} :answer-field/id #uuid "94b73dce-7f8c-597e-ae8e-4e20e2b2ff1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8dad0d98-37c5-5af9-9c0a-4f0fabec2bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-141200" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and  $det(A)=-1?$ \n\nI. $A$ is invertible\n\nII. $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}$\n\nIII. $\\text{nullity}(A)=6$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=6=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=-1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be  invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is invertible,  it must be $\\text{nullity}(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30c30111-8967-5105-a0b6-6a23513210c3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186269710} {:answer/id #uuid "631335b6-7ef6-59ce-8e7e-749eea9ebc94" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186270145} {:answer/id #uuid "668c0601-7469-5a85-8564-3965eb0e1791" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270180} {:answer/id #uuid "bf4f4ba1-902f-5cdd-9b4c-bb432b2696b0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186270962} {:answer/id #uuid "dfabd0e7-90be-5aeb-9da3-657d3cdb0121" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186271230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf4f4ba1-902f-5cdd-9b4c-bb432b2696b0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186270962} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d9f08da-5269-5b1f-bbcd-8c03985365d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c894da3b-5f0a-59a9-8060-52795d04ecee" :question/math-academy-id "q-141460" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{7}\\to {R}^{7}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T(x)=v$ has no solution for some vector $v?$ \n\n- $T$ is not invertible\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$\n- $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{7}$ into ${R}^{7},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=7 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $T(x)=v$ has no solution, we have that $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ hence $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{7}.$ According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{7},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "208d1741-9b10-5838-b5d0-cf5b8af2cbc3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186269544} {:answer/id #uuid "6e85d1f1-1d07-59dc-a62c-55b15d9603cf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270250} {:answer/id #uuid "70ac0a62-886f-57c1-aea0-e66bd7901f22" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186270268} {:answer/id #uuid "9754293a-effe-5066-a8b6-e33c8c81be55" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186270596} {:answer/id #uuid "dffd9eb1-554e-5064-8f37-aa14a874c3f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186271233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e85d1f1-1d07-59dc-a62c-55b15d9603cf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270250} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cdc4b03-2ed5-57f1-96a3-caaa4804b205" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2fc1f275-1b92-5d39-91a9-c77fc478abd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-141461" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{4}\\to {R}^{4}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T$ is a bijection? \n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- $\\text{rank}(T)=4$\n- $T(v)=0$ for some $v\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{4}$ into ${R}^{4},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=4 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0},$ which means there is no non-zero vector $v$ such that $T(v)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272347 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124511 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "302eb5c9-529d-52e1-a2c8-d1933af5ef1d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186269702} {:answer/id #uuid "3248b20a-91c4-5f7a-8779-ea6de359ff74" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186269724} {:answer/id #uuid "50abda73-b0d4-5a29-bbed-94088d819a8e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186269993} {:answer/id #uuid "81cab381-305e-5227-96c9-4247414173f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186270391} {:answer/id #uuid "aa256175-82e0-56ad-9450-2d16ed6ca6ba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186270786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "302eb5c9-529d-52e1-a2c8-d1933af5ef1d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186269702} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecf48405-f1ff-5682-8eff-4a48eebe4c60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4927fd87-c83e-52d3-b9bb-877110a8c514" :question/math-academy-id "q-142474" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R\\}?\n$$\n\nI. $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1$\n\nII. $A$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{rank}(A)<4$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 4,$ or equivalently $\\text{rank}(A)<4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186272354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186184172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e145cb2-806a-579d-8299-8a91a6feaad1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem and the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14c41b95-9596-5622-8c50-ab125bfd2d1b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186269450} {:answer/id #uuid "219e0837-40ab-56ac-83a2-e830963ec4b3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186269555} {:answer/id #uuid "31ab7072-4441-5672-aae6-91ba5c541bbf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186269716} {:answer/id #uuid "36aebee8-74d8-57f5-bd26-af2dbc3f65cc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186269765} {:answer/id #uuid "a3580b5c-8adc-5496-932c-814534e0f45c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186270724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36aebee8-74d8-57f5-bd26-af2dbc3f65cc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186269765} :answer-field/id #uuid "d78fac35-412b-5106-bc11-6026065b218b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a4ded73-dd50-5431-9dd3-a7f4831d94c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-142544" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix which has only $3$ non-pivot column?\n\n- $A$ is  **not** invertible\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $4$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{7}.$\n- $\\text{nullity}(A)=1$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=7=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $A$ has $n=7$ columns and $3$ of them is a non-pivot one, it follows that    \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Col}(A))=7-3=4\\ne 7.\n$$\n\n Thus,  according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is  **not** an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true.  Indeed, since $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=\\text{rank}(A)=4$,  then $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $4$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{7}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $n=7$, according to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 4+\\text{nullity}(A) & =7 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =7-4 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186275119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186184172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e145cb2-806a-579d-8299-8a91a6feaad1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem and the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54e429d6-5947-5ad4-8a8e-1b4250c00d74" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186273111} {:answer/id #uuid "5c3fa507-d42c-5103-9bce-8c5f53c501b8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186273180} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c0cc8f-fdd7-579a-8085-29cf09cbcaa8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186274381} {:answer/id #uuid "f79576de-3c96-5d9f-9017-a24e98f90c5a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186274559} {:answer/id #uuid "fe165258-8e9f-54a9-8b34-94b679b7bcec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186274621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe165258-8e9f-54a9-8b34-94b679b7bcec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186274621} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b6204e6-2ff1-5255-b86a-87eaf2ede716" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186274763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5342a760-4f6b-57f7-989c-35eadd9600b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-143533" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and  $\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a,b\\in R}?$ \n\n- $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $det(A)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=3=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$  \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a,b\\in R}=\\text{Span}{\\,[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\n \nwe have that $\\text{nullity}(A)=2.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=3,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =3 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(A)=2\\ne 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $A$ is not invertible we must have   $det(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186275135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08aa5e40-f907-50f4-951b-c71140a5352e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186272462} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8077df-ab1d-5da4-88d8-80f362bafcad" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186272895} {:answer/id #uuid "9a5e2eb0-f74e-552b-b366-11725709bbb7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186273721} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf1cae4-760d-5c26-a55c-ccba7d6302c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186273739} {:answer/id #uuid "b3e1a958-b3c4-543b-af2a-389cf376a09a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186273946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a5e2eb0-f74e-552b-b366-11725709bbb7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186273721} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ad96634-e2da-5afd-bd87-58989b7b67fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186274709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df88ef9d-a81d-55a1-b088-252f805046b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-143685" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $A$ is a square matrix, then $A+{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is a symmetric matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists).\n- The sum of two $n\\times n$ matrices is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any square matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any non-singular symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{-1})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{-1} \\\\  & ={A}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their sum is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186275136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59511ecd-760f-5a26-8413-c806af70d849" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186273150} {:answer/id #uuid "74394f47-61a5-59ed-8042-940ea3fed5d7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186273382} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b1f62c-834f-579c-a300-554956679735" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186273963} {:answer/id #uuid "c0528799-16a1-5629-96a3-8f50e1d8f001" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186274052} {:answer/id #uuid "dc006e14-42a2-5536-a14c-1676307fd9ce" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186274318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74394f47-61a5-59ed-8042-940ea3fed5d7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186273382} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d8b66c2-34e1-5bb1-904f-4db1604f1532" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186274740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "416a1d82-1004-5007-b882-19d5aaa3af4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-143686" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The product of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+{A}^{2}$ is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ their product $AB$ is also diagonal, and any diagonal matrix is symmetric.\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, $A+{A}^{2}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+{A}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any square matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}A)^{T} & =(A{A}^{T})^{T}-({A}^{T}A)^{T} \\\\  & =({A}^{T})^{T}{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & =A{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186275137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15274c66-a01d-5e11-adcf-f5b7af833716" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186272577} {:answer/id #uuid "33f93cc6-5c19-5d84-abe2-dc0f4f827464" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186272849} {:answer/id #uuid "3bfa656f-46fd-5b47-8681-a4125453c988" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186272905} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec937ca-a0b9-5506-bdd2-a01d7c5c3993" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186273474} {:answer/id #uuid "c17c205f-6fa2-54a3-af74-d30ca7ac801f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186274067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ec937ca-a0b9-5506-bdd2-a01d7c5c3993" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186273474} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f76ddf6-2fd6-51e1-9516-877b87bf72c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186274662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "62cdcd34-c338-59e0-b65d-2d882ff231e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-143687" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The sum of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$  matrix, then $A-{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n- The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, $A-{A}^{T}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{A}^{T} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186275138 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "048a1426-e467-5f7e-8f82-a4d34764959c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186272427} {:answer/id #uuid "5277c838-1f6f-5a65-99df-70de2b3be811" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186273088} {:answer/id #uuid "91b72d80-d1b0-5714-90ef-ff9b08666590" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186273654} {:answer/id #uuid "96acc4c6-843e-5d92-95f4-295d7b6576f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186273692} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb2b63c-1f8a-5d48-8978-0439e3ae672a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186274196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfb2b63c-1f8a-5d48-8978-0439e3ae672a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186274196} :answer-field/id #uuid "efa0f764-9003-584a-9cb0-87716a826d67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186275060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "812925c4-9b82-5457-b638-58a29d609de3" :question/math-academy-id "q-143688" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $A$ is symmetric, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists).\n- The sum of *any* two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any non-singular symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{-1})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{-1} \\\\  & ={A}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{T}A)^{T} & ={A}^{T}({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186275245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186184220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc36f534-6b27-5619-a29e-9954d0310adb" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity of Two-Piece Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31450770-cd81-593c-9756-bf59a3210e96" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186272828} {:answer/id #uuid "4001b683-f061-56c7-b09f-ae4da2370e1a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186272936} {:answer/id #uuid "748af4df-aea9-5cea-aff7-54752ec720f8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186273384} {:answer/id #uuid "7e50cbd9-a49c-5495-b667-fa105a197df9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186273468} {:answer/id #uuid "cae87b05-0578-5e12-b78c-39423534de2e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186274154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e50cbd9-a49c-5495-b667-fa105a197df9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186273468} :answer-field/id #uuid "9725aa84-a0a7-5f0e-96c0-136120bcc8ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186274899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0a8af66-eea1-5639-ab77-2bc1523780a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-149748" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x-2, & x\\le 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{x}, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=4$\n- $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=4$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$" :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $f(x)$ at $x=4.$\n\nLet's start by computing $f(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=4,$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x) & =4-2 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & =f(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=4,$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x) & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & =f(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=4,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186275310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186064316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8edb0a6c-f22f-56cf-9545-2fae93896db2" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity at a Point Using the Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f9e8008-048e-5d52-af32-ca8c30b1b34b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186272665} {:answer/id #uuid "ab920b6e-5aad-535d-80b7-194b6cbe5ae3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186273868} {:answer/id #uuid "da72c52a-a470-53be-a5e3-a28564f510be" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186274296} {:answer/id #uuid "ec99f1e0-fc55-55ce-a5ad-c6c0246e4283" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186274476} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b629aa-e668-5735-bf87-c9e28b80e3be" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id 17592186274538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab920b6e-5aad-535d-80b7-194b6cbe5ae3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186273868} :answer-field/id #uuid "b94fcde2-7a90-5f43-b9d9-5ca2e2cb0449" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186274957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc9788c2-4a6c-53d6-898a-18877d069efc" :question/math-academy-id "q-152925" :question/problem "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3-x}, & x\\le -1 \\\\ x+1, & x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)?$\n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)$ exists\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)=f(-1)$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(-1)=\\sqrt{3-(-1)}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is false. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}(\\sqrt{3-x}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3-(-1)} \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}(x+1) \\\\  & =(-1)+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-1$ does not exist. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement III is false since the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-1$ does not exist. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since not all statements I-III are true, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186275358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19c33c51-bf11-5a81-a0fd-8381d8e0b9d7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15021175-4a1a-5a9f-8136-d7bc7497c251" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186272576} {:answer/id #uuid "487ca92a-3acf-55d7-85da-b1c19c648b1e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186273005} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9ba917-c2d9-5909-bb4b-e0359c38a146" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186273031} {:answer/id #uuid "53fcc9c3-6907-5f83-8eac-a58c2d61aa30" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186273103} {:answer/id #uuid "6234a335-fac5-5204-8a4f-d91e0c834031" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186273232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15021175-4a1a-5a9f-8136-d7bc7497c251" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186272576} :answer-field/id #uuid "de2177fe-6b49-5a9d-9447-4b016a086300" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186275021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "238a8f80-b240-55d1-a1f3-be7bf8e77e18" :question/math-academy-id "q-156624" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $x=2y-1$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2y-1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $x=2y-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2y-1 \\\\ (0) & =2(0)-1 \\\\ 0 & =-1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1+1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2y-1 \\\\ (0) & =2(1)-1 \\\\ 0 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186275363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83796fe8-f045-5dbc-94d1-6d6e031f3ed8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09dafb17-d53e-58e3-8f2c-867bbfbd5018" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186272475} {:answer/id #uuid "78b3c987-8686-55a2-9fb9-32e3742b5cab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186273417} {:answer/id #uuid "af6bc78d-b4de-5c47-980a-a819110c10bd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id 17592186273904} {:answer/id #uuid "b4682617-6c44-5562-8d58-aef5888f6886" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186273951} {:answer/id #uuid "bf68df2a-a19d-5a66-977c-803603cd56aa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186274042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf68df2a-a19d-5a66-977c-803603cd56aa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186274042} :answer-field/id #uuid "c59d2e8c-e25b-594f-8642-a7de4539a7f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186274980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "951ef63e-740e-55a7-b946-00aea8b9f2dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-156727" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(1,2,3)$ and $(3,2,1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false.The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{2}=\\frac{z-3}{-2},\\,y=2\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since the second coordinate of any point on this line satisfies\n\n$$\ny=2\\ne 0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHowever, point $(4,4,4)$ does not belong to $L$ since it does not satisfy the equation of the line. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\ny=4\\ne 2.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186275364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f3018fd-9d16-578f-b050-966890844c9b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Plane Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49b5b039-f8d1-5ac0-84a2-bcd1b48b5132" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186273015} {:answer/id #uuid "4b401c7f-1af6-5c54-8b8e-0d0fa1d379c8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186273023} {:answer/id #uuid "68f0fe86-66a6-5d7f-a553-e10227b7a089" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id 17592186273294} {:answer/id #uuid "7677fc23-197f-5d23-aedf-ed8e18b43479" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186273396} {:answer/id #uuid "93643b07-3279-5a83-b854-54ef0257fec3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id 17592186273669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7677fc23-197f-5d23-aedf-ed8e18b43479" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186273396} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e067aec-a6e4-5314-ab5a-93c2cad83e21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186274796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36959921-7ebb-5f39-a723-592fbf72eec8" :question/math-academy-id "q-156728" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=2x-5y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ 2x-5y\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=2x-5y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2x-5y \\\\ (0) & =2(0)-5(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ 2{b}_{1}-5{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ 2{b}_{1}-5{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ (2{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2})+(2{b}_{1}-5{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ 2({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})-5({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=2x-5y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k(2{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ 2k{a}_{1}-5k{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=2x-5y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, the plane $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186275376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6adcf6ee-01fc-563f-b9b6-d1b3c45b5e61" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186273307} {:answer/id #uuid "8244231c-94b3-554a-a514-496b5826501b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186273504} {:answer/id #uuid "c1fee998-0b79-5cf1-9270-9df51c0696d6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186274072} {:answer/id #uuid "c84b21f9-514b-56b8-8250-9200be09b088" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186274126} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ad941b-6a02-50a2-80b9-0f1a196e7ec9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186274204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6adcf6ee-01fc-563f-b9b6-d1b3c45b5e61" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186273307} :answer-field/id #uuid "28a9989e-d052-5f69-af3d-b89ae23e841c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186274705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8e786624-3795-5bd2-9044-40e13fec09b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-157162" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0.$\n- ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n- The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =-2[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is true, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186275381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d82b46-aff2-515f-9d9e-763c1d8d6d4b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186272442} {:answer/id #uuid "9ae733d2-cc04-5192-bed0-23294bc49d61" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186273728} {:answer/id #uuid "ac235c0f-3456-5dc9-bf15-4e714898bb4b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186273872} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e7a6e4-13cc-528d-b440-977d47c6f5a9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186274257} {:answer/id #uuid "f92b98ef-714d-56ae-84c7-20ba88faf358" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186274572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ae733d2-cc04-5192-bed0-23294bc49d61" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186273728} :answer-field/id #uuid "3340ab30-5665-5c4b-ac26-0abb6f1cea04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186274724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "97d72a17-2281-52f3-a3f9-d658f3c735d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-157224" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $-2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3} & =-2[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6-2+0 \\\\ 0+4+0 \\\\ 2-2+15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186275382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20033992-bd64-57d8-8dd4-bf7e04b02470" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186272670} {:answer/id #uuid "3eac531e-241f-5b68-9928-e92f5bdfff6b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186272923} {:answer/id #uuid "a6e42fdd-34c7-51a7-9c00-a2ceb9b43224" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186273832} {:answer/id #uuid "b99f3082-b5fa-59ad-8334-cdcbcfd0a82c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186274001} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a967b6-1436-5449-9aaf-9ed05176d4ad" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186274231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3eac531e-241f-5b68-9928-e92f5bdfff6b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186272923} :answer-field/id #uuid "73031230-f674-5477-83c8-f4a76f429a85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186274830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3b60b8aa-49bb-5ecd-ad04-b68a0c43f769" :question/math-academy-id "q-157225" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{1}-5{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3}=0.$\n- ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+0+0 \\\\ 5+0-9 \\\\ -2-20+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -19\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is true, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186275383 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "100da246-a4f5-543a-91d0-e6d3a1f36af7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186272530} {:answer/id #uuid "3f886435-adcb-5d14-a0ea-d8238504ef8b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186272932} {:answer/id #uuid "74151cc2-d101-54b9-b1d6-6503644e7ec2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186273380} {:answer/id #uuid "8e9e7313-37e7-5fd3-8c10-525805d2da7a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :ans